indicator("Movement Engine Pro v4.2", shorttitle="ME Pro v4.2", overlay=true, max_boxes_count=500, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=500, max_bars_back=1500)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.2 — SLOW, FRAGMENTED, MISTAKEN. THREE COMPLAINTS, THREE DIFFERENT CAUSES.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// All three were reported together and none of them share a cause. Everything
// below was either read off v4.1's own source or measured on the sim/ harness,
// 15m, 150 days x 6 seeds, with identical entries and identical stops in every
// row — only the thing under test moves.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// MISTAKEN — v4.1 could not agree with itself about what trade it was in.
//
// Four places decide what a signal IS, and they used THREE different priority
// orders:
//
//     buyType      name and alert       MSS   > FVG  > SWEEP > BAND
//     buySlAnchor  where the stop goes  SWEEP > BAND > FVG   > MSS
//     buyChaseRef  the chase reference  SWEEP > BAND > FVG   > MSS
//     tpMeanBuy    target style         BAND, whenever a band was live at all
//
// When MSS and a sweep fire on the same bar the chart says MSS, the stop is
// placed beyond the SWEEP's wick, the chase cap is measured from the swept
// level, and a live band injects the VWAP mean — a reversion target — into what
// is labelled a continuation trade. One signal number, four different trades.
//
// This is the MAJORITY case, not an edge: 9,832 of 17,738 bullish trigger bars
// carry two or more distinct triggers, 55%. And v4.1's confluence score AWARDS
// A POINT for stacking (buyTrigCount >= 2), so the engine rated highest exactly
// the bars where its four descriptions disagreed most.
//
// FIXED: the trigger is selected ONCE (buySel / sellSel), ordered by how
// precisely each defines its own invalidation — SWEEP > BAND > FVG > MSS, which
// is the order the STOP already used, so stop placement does not move. The name,
// the chase reference and the target style now agree with it. A stacked bar is
// labelled with a trailing "+" instead of being silently relabelled.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// MISTAKEN, SECOND — the reward gate was never the gate it documents.
//
// v3.5.8 added the engine's only reward check: TP1 at least i_minRR = 1.0 times
// the stop. v3.5.26 added an alternative, and the test became
//     rrOk = effMinRR <= 0  or  rrStrict  or  blendOk
// An `or` short-circuits, so the strict test only decides anything when blendOk
// is false. Measured over 8,198 setups reaching the gate:
//
//     blended reward, median                    2.68   against a 1.3 threshold
//     share of setups whose blend clears 1.3     100%
//
// Every one. i_minBlendRR has never rejected a single setup, so blendOk collapses
// to its other term — the 0.5R floor. THE ENGINE'S REAL REWARD REQUIREMENT WAS
// 0.5R while it documented, displayed and defended 1.0R. 19% of admitted trades
// entered on that gap.
//
// The blend clears so easily because it prices all three legs at 33/33/34 when
// the last two almost never fill (below). FIXED: the blend is weighted by
// measured reachability, 0.55 / 0.30 / 0.15. i_blendWeighted off restores the
// v4.1 arithmetic exactly.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FRAGMENTED — the scale-out does not scale out, and could not be watched.
//
// v4.1 kept activeTp1Px and activeTp3Px and NO activeTp2Px. TP2 was drawn on the
// chart, published in the alert payload, and the engine never knew whether it
// traded. A three-leg plan was tracked as two legs. The v3.5.41 manual TP2
// override moved the drawn line and wrote to nothing, because there was no
// tracked value to write to — dead input, dead since it shipped.
//
// Then the fills, which is the substantive half:
//
//     ladder                TP1   TP2   TP3   bars    SL
//     1.5/2.5/4.0 R (v4.1)  50%   14%    6%      6   50%
//
// The second third of the position fills once in seven trades and the last third
// once in sixteen. A plan that leaves in thirds realises nothing like thirds.
//
// FIXED: activeTp2Px exists, is tracked, is honoured by the manual override, and
// the dashboard prints the legs as they fill ("1-3", "12-3").
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SLOW — every target was a multiple of the STOP.
//
//     tp = entry + dir * risk * rmul          rmul = 1.5 / 2.5 / 4.0
//
// The stop is structural and capped at 3.0 ATR, so TP3 at 4.0R can sit 12 ATR
// out. The plan was slowest and least reachable exactly when the structure was
// widest — backwards: a wide invalidation says the setup needs room, not that
// the market owes you a bigger move. Median TP3 measured 6.18 ATR.
//
// FIXED: i_tpUnit measures the multiples in ATR. Same entries, same stops:
//
//     ladder                TP1   TP2   TP3   bars    SL
//     1.5/2.5/4.0 R (v4.1)  50%   14%    6%      6   50%
//     1.5/2.5/4.0 ATR       52%   16%    8%      6   48%
//     1.0/1.8/3.0 ATR       58%   19%    8%      5   42%
//     0.8/1.4/2.2 ATR       62%   24%   11%      4   38%
//
// Every fill rate rises, the stop-out rate falls 50% -> 38%, the median hold goes
// 6 bars -> 4. The time stop also ships ON at 24 bars; shipping it disabled is
// what let v3.5.23's hanging trades happen and there was no reason to repeat it.
//
// THE HONEST COST: a nearer target is a smaller win. This buys reachability, a
// working de-risk and a scale-out whose legs actually fill. It does not buy edge.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN, and it is the same caveat every table in this file
// carries. The data is synthetic and driftless, where expectancy is ~0 by
// construction. FILL RATES, hold times, the stop's position relative to
// structure, and the gap between what the gate assumes and what the plan
// delivers are all GEOMETRY and do not depend on drift — those are the numbers
// reported above. Profitability is not measured here and nothing above should be
// read as if it were. Run the strategy build over one range before and after.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.1 — EVERY MODE TRADES. MODE IS A STANDARD, NOT A TRIGGER BAN.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v4.0 fixed trigger supply and left one mode broken. Strict could only see MSS
// — FVG, sweep and band were switched off for it — and on top of that it had a
// location rule and an FRVP requirement no other mode carried. Three vetoes
// stacked. Measured over 150 days x 6 seeds it produced 0.38 signals/day on 15m,
// 0.21 on 30m and 0.07 on 1H. That is not a strict mode, it is a mode that does
// not trade, and no market condition caused it: the location rule alone was the
// SOLE cause of 1066 of 30m Strict's blocks and appeared in 69-74% of all of them.
//
// WHAT CHANGED. Every trigger is admitted in every mode. The mode now sets how
// many INDEPENDENT READINGS of the entry bar must agree — participation, body
// conviction, close location, two triggers stacking, and price at a band:
//
//     Aggressive / Scalp   0 of 5      Balanced   1 of 5      Strict   2 of 5
//
// A bar can satisfy that anywhere on the chart, where the old rule needed a rare
// price location, which is the whole of the supply difference:
//
//     signals/day        15m     30m     1H      4H
//     Strict v4.0        0.38    0.21    0.07    0.13
//     Strict v4.1        1.45    1.08    0.63    0.31
//
// And the ladder now behaves the way a mode ladder is supposed to — fewer trades
// AND better selection as it tightens, rather than fewer trades alone:
//
//     15m   Scalp 3.17/day 44.6%  ->  Balanced 1.68 46.9%  ->  Strict 1.45 46.5%
//     4H    Scalp 0.50/day 47.6%  ->  Balanced 0.33 48.8%  ->  Strict 0.31 48.9%
//
// One correction inside the score: location counts only for a trigger that is
// NOT itself a band rejection. A band trigger sits at a band by construction, so
// scoring it there is counting one fact twice — and it made Strict and Balanced
// emit identical signals on 1H and 4H, where band rejections are 74% of entries.
//
// v4.1 also adds the OUTER (2σ) band rejection as its own trigger. A 2σ
// excursion never satisfies the 1σ close-back-inside test on the same bar, so
// that supply was simply unreachable before: it is 26% of 5m and 17% of 15m
// entries.
//
// WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN. Every figure above comes from synthetic driftless
// OHLCV, where expectancy is ~0 by construction. The harness can measure trade
// COUNT, spacing, and whether selection improves across the ladder. It cannot
// measure profitability, and nothing here should be read as if it did.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.0 — REBUILT TRIGGER SET. VWAP IS NOW AN ENTRY, NOT JUST A FILTER.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every number below was MEASURED with the sim/ harness, not reasoned.
//
// THE DIAGNOSIS. v3.5.x converted 2-4% of its triggers into signals and
// produced under one trade a day on 15m. The instinct all along was that gates
// were too tight, so the fix was always to loosen one. That was wrong, and it
// is measurable: the three most redundant gates were identified by how often
// they fire together with another gate —
//     chase co-fires with risk   92%
//     delta co-fires with wick   88%
//     of%   co-fires with wick   76%
// — and removing ALL THREE bought +8% signals. Filtering was never the
// constraint. TRIGGER SUPPLY was: 26.7 triggers/day on 15m is simply not enough
// raw material, no matter how permissive the gates that follow.
//
// THE REBUILD. VWAP had been computed since v3.3 and used only as a yes/no on
// which side price sat. It was never an entry level. v4 adds volume-weighted
// deviation bands and treats a rejection at band 1 — price trades THROUGH it
// and closes back inside — as a trigger in its own right. Same shape as a
// liquidity sweep, overshoot and reclaim, but measured against the mean rather
// than a pivot, so it fires on bars where no pivot has been violated.
//
//     15m trigger supply/day    MSS 4.7   FVG 6.8   sweep 3.4   BAND 20.9
//     total                     14.9  ->  30.5   (+105%)
//
// IS THE NEW TRIGGER WORSE? No, and this was tested rather than assumed. Over
// 3,854 trades on 15m, the band trigger against the three original ones:
//     original (mss+fvg+sweep)   -0.0296R   n 1445
//     band                       -0.0438R   n 2409
//     difference -0.0142R, t = -0.42  -> indistinguishable
// It looked worst in a first pass only because it has four times the sample.
//
// A band rejection is a REVERSION setup, so it is given the volume-weighted
// mean as a target candidate rather than the continuation ladder the breakout
// triggers use. That change alone moved it from -0.075R to -0.062R. It also
// inherits the sweep's exemption from the CVD and momentum trend gates in
// Scalp, because a rejection at the lower band happens after a decline — the
// trend reads bearish at exactly the moment the setup forms, which is the same
// defect fixed for sweeps in v3.5.1.
//
// RESULT, measured over 150 days x 6 seeds:
//     tf    mode       v3.5.x/day   v4/day   gain
//     5m    Scalp        1.92        6.53    3.4x
//     5m    Balanced     0.39        3.60    9.2x
//     15m   Scalp        0.92        2.66    2.9x
//     15m   Balanced     0.25        1.71    6.7x
//     30m   Scalp        0.54        1.60    2.9x
//     1H    Scalp        0.25        0.93    3.7x
//
// The modes are now genuinely different strategies rather than three sets of
// clamps on the same one: Scalp takes every trigger, Balanced requires trend
// alignment, Strict refuses the band entirely and demands its entries come from
// violated structure.
//
// Expectancy sits between -0.03R and -0.10R across the matrix on driftless
// synthetic data, where the true value is zero. That gap is the harness's
// conservative intrabar rule — a bar touching both stop and TP1 is scored as a
// stop — and it bites hardest where stops are tight. It is a property of the
// measurement, not a forecast.
//
// Everything from v3.5.x is retained: the three repaint fixes, TP ordering,
// spacing and ceiling, the FVG closure guard, timeframe auto-scaling, the
// Heikin Ashi guard and manual level overrides.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.41 CHANGELOG — MANUAL LEVELS, AND A HEIKIN ASHI GUARD
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Two reported gaps, one of which was a safety problem rather than a feature.
//
// 1. HEIKIN ASHI IS NOT SUPPORTED, AND SILENTLY PRODUCED UNTRADEABLE LEVELS.
//    On an HA chart TradingView feeds a script the SYNTHETIC series:
//        HA close = (o + h + l + c) / 4
//        HA open  = (previous HA open + previous HA close) / 2
//    Neither is a price that ever traded. Every order-flow read here — body
//    conviction (close-open)/range, close position, the CVD sign, the body
//    gate — would be measuring a moving average of itself, and every trigger
//    that tests `close` would be testing a smoothed value rather than what the
//    market printed.
//    The serious part is the plan: the stop and all three targets are derived
//    from `close` and from pivots of the HA high/low, so the levels drawn would
//    be prices that DO NOT EXIST in the real market and cannot fill. The chart
//    would look cleaner and the numbers on it would be fiction.
//    HA has an exact signature — open == (open[1] + close[1]) / 2 on
//    essentially every bar — so it is now detected and the dashboard header
//    turns into "HEIKIN ASHI — LEVELS INVALID". Use standard candles. This is
//    not a preference; the levels are not real otherwise.
//
// 2. THE DRAWN LEVELS COULD NOT BE MOVED. Correct, and it is a platform limit:
//    Pine draws with line.new and those objects are not draggable. There is no
//    setting that changes it.
//    Adds four price inputs — Manual SL / TP1 / TP2 / TP3. Any one set to a
//    non-zero price replaces the engine's level on the open plan; leave a slot
//    at 0 to keep the engine's own, so the stop can be moved without touching
//    the targets. They can be placed by clicking the chart or typed exactly.
//    Overrides reach the DRAWN plan and the hit tracking that feeds breakeven,
//    the cooldown and the loss-streak guard, so the engine scores the trade you
//    are actually in. They deliberately do NOT feed back into signal
//    generation: the reward gate already passed on the engine's own levels, and
//    letting a manual target re-open that decision would let a user talk the
//    engine into a setup it refused. An overridden level is marked with * on
//    its label so a manual plan is never mistaken for a computed one.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.40 CHANGELOG — THE RATES IN v3.5.38 AND v3.5.39 WERE WRONG BY 4x
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// No logic changes. This entry exists because the last two versions were sold
// on measured numbers, and the measurement was broken.
//
// The harness generated price with iid gaussian returns. That whipsaws
// constantly, so pivots formed everywhere and — the real culprit — a qualifying
// FVG appeared every 3.0 bars. Broken down against a live 15m chart that
// reported "MSS fresh 27b":
//
//   event      synthetic v1        realistic
//   MSS        every 19.7 bars     every ~27 bars     (was fine)
//   sweep      every 104 bars      rare               (was fine)
//   FVG        every 3.0 bars      every ~21 bars     (10x too many)
//
// Bar wicks were too small relative to bodies, so consecutive ranges never
// overlapped and gaps opened on almost every bar. With the wick model
// calibrated so gap density matches a live chart, the real rates are:
//
//   tf    triggers/day   stock   with v3.5.38+39   PREVIOUSLY CLAIMED
//   5m      80.4          0.87      1.63             3.96
//   15m     26.9          0.46      0.76             3.06
//   30m     13.3          0.28      0.38             1.49
//
// So this engine produces roughly ONE TRADE EVERY ONE TO THREE DAYS on 15m,
// not three per day. Two consecutive blank days has a 23% probability at 0.76
// per day and 55% at stock. The reported silence was the engine's actual output
// and every reassurance given about it was based on inflated arithmetic.
//
// CONVERSION IS 1-3% OF TRIGGERS, at every timeframe. That is the binding
// constraint, and it means 3 signals/day requires ~150+ triggers/day. Only 1m
// supplies that:
//
//   tf    triggers/day   calibrated signals/day
//   1m     408.6          3.50
//   3m     133.8          2.17
//   5m      80.4          1.63
//   15m     26.9          0.76
//
// And 1m is unusable on gold. Spread against a ~1.5 ATR stop:
//   1m 25.8% of risk   3m 14.9%   5m 11.5%   15m 6.7%   30m 4.7%
// The only timeframe with enough trigger supply is the one where the spread
// eats a quarter of every stop.
//
// CONCLUSION, stated plainly: at its current selectivity this engine cannot
// deliver 3 trades/day on XAUUSD at any viable timeframe. Its honest range is
// 0.4-1.6 per day on 5m-15m. Reaching 3/day on 15m was measured to require
// disabling the reward gate, the confluence floor, the wick filter, momentum
// and CVD, and dropping body to 0.15 ATR and order flow to 50% — at which point
// it is a bar-shape alert, not a structure engine.
//
// The v3.5.38 calibration and the v3.5.39 risk cap are KEPT: both roughly
// double the rate and both were directionally right. Only the absolute numbers
// attached to them were wrong.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.39 CHANGELOG — SCALP RISK CAP, AND A CORRECTION TO v3.5.38's NUMBERS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CORRECTION FIRST. The rates quoted in v3.5.38 were measured with a harness
// that silently skipped two gates this engine actually applies:
//   • mode        — Scalp's ONLY mode gate is the VWAP overextension guard
//   • confluence  — Selective demands at least 1 of 3 directional alignments
// Neither was ported, so every v3.5.38 figure was too high, by up to 1.7x:
//
//   tf    v3.5.38 claimed   actually
//   5m    4.64/day          2.79
//   15m   1.52              1.28
//   30m   0.68              0.58
//   1H    0.36              0.32
//
// The harness now computes session VWAP, the higher-timeframe EMA bias and a
// rolling volume profile, and applies both gates.
//
// WHAT BLOCKS SIGNALS, ranked, 15m Scalp/Selective — 16,520 triggers producing
// 461 signals, a 2.8% conversion:
//
//   vol 18.5%   risk 18.0%   rr 15.5%   body 10.1%   chase 8.2%   mom 6.2%
//   mode 6.0%   cvd 4.4%     wick 3.7%  cooldown 2.8%  of% 2.6%
//   confluence 2.1%   delta 1.4%   rsi 0.5%
//
// THE CHANGE. "risk" was the second largest cause and the only one in the top
// three that costs nothing to relax. Scalp's max-risk cap was hard-coded to 2.0
// ATR. Swept alone with the stop buffer held fixed:
//
//   cap 2.0 -> 1.28 signals/day    cap 2.5 -> 1.55    cap 3.0 -> 1.79
//
// +40% from one number. Position size is derived from stop distance, so a wider
// stop means a smaller position at the same money risked — the setup still has
// to clear every other gate. The old cap rejected valid setups purely because
// their invalidation sat further away, which is a property of the structure and
// not of the trade. Expectancy over the same range: -0.053R at 2.0, -0.023R at
// 3.0, both indistinguishable from zero.
//
// COMBINED WITH THE v3.5.38 CALIBRATION, corrected figures:
//
//   tf    stock   +risk cap   +calibration (15m and above)
//   5m    2.79    3.96        3.96
//   15m   1.28    1.79        3.06
//   30m   0.58    0.95        1.49
//   1H    0.32    0.47        0.71
//
// HONEST CAVEAT. At the final settings 5m measures -0.073R with t = -2.59 over
// 1,426 trades. On driftless data the true value is zero, so that is either
// noise or a harness artefact — the outcome walk resolves a bar touching both
// stop and TP1 in the stop's favour, which biases negative and bites hardest
// where the stop is small relative to a bar's range, i.e. on 5m. It is NOT
// evidence the engine loses money, and it is not evidence it makes any.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.38 CHANGELOG — SCALP IS NOW CALIBRATED TO THE CHART TIMEFRAME
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FIRST VERSION IN THIS FILE WHOSE NUMBERS WERE MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED.
// sim/ contains a Python port of this engine's signal path — trigger
// construction, every gate, the mode presets, the TP ladder and a bar-by-bar
// outcome walk. It exists because 37 versions shipped without the logic ever
// being executed, and because v3.5.30 was shipped as a fix on a simulation that
// asked the wrong question.
//
// WHAT THE MEASUREMENT SHOWED (90 days x 4 seeds, synthetic gold-like OHLCV):
//
//   Scalp/Selective, one clamp set across every timeframe:
//     5m 4.64 signals/day   15m 1.52   30m 0.68   1H 0.36   4H 0.09
//
// The same preset therefore meant a working scalper on 5m and roughly one trade
// every two days on 30m. The rate was never designed — it just followed however
// many bars the chart happened to have.
//
// It also settled a question argued about for two days: LOWER TIMEFRAMES FIRE
// MORE, not less. A day where 30m signalled and 5m/15m did not was a quiet day,
// not a mechanism.
//
// AND IT SHOWED WHERE THE CEILING IS. Trade count above 15m is bounded by
// TRIGGER SUPPLY, not by gates: 2/day on 30m is unreachable even with the
// reward gate at 0, cooldown at 2 and the volume floor at 0.8, because only ~23
// triggers a day exist to work with.
//
// THE CALIBRATION. Timeframes at or above 15m — the starved ones — now get
// measured adjustments: trigger freshness 6, sweep wick 0.30 ATR, minimum FVG
// 0.25 ATR, cooldown 3, volume floor 0.7. 5m is left alone; it already clears
// the target and the same treatment would have taken it to ~10/day.
//
//     5m 4.64 (unchanged)   15m 2.99   30m 1.24   1H 0.59   4H 0.18
//
// Every one of those levers adds trigger SUPPLY or attempts per trigger. The
// reward gate, the confluence floor and the order-flow threshold are untouched.
// That distinction is the point: an earlier search concluded 3/day on 15m
// required disabling the reward gate outright, and it was wrong — supplying
// more triggers reaches the same rate with the gate intact.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM. Driftless synthetic data is a fair game by
// construction, so expectancy is ~0 at every setting — measured at every
// timeframe before and after, worst t-stat -1.53. That is the correct result
// for a sound plan and is NOT evidence of edge in either direction. The harness
// measures count, spacing and structural soundness. Whether this engine beats
// real gold is still unmeasured, and only real price can answer it.
//
// A single seed had shown 5m at -0.135R and looked like a structural defect;
// eight seeds put it at -0.039 with t = -2.06. It was sampling noise, and it
// would have been "fixed" without the harness.
//
// Turn i_tfCalib off for behaviour identical to v3.5.37.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.37 CHANGELOG — TRIGGER DIAGNOSTICS ROW (display only)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: 30m produces a Selective Scalp signal while 5m and 15m produce
// nothing. That is the right thing to be suspicious of — lower timeframes have
// MORE bars and therefore more trigger opportunities, so they should fire more
// often, not less.
//
// I could not answer it, and I do not have a mechanism that predicts fewer
// signals on a lower timeframe. Rather than invent one, the dashboard now
// states the two numbers that separate the only two possible causes:
//
//   "Triggers (100b)"   ->   "12 trig / 1 sig  ·  last 4b"
//
//   trig = 0            no setup is forming at all. No gate is involved — a
//                       trigger is the INPUT to every gate. Red.
//   trig > 0, sig = 0   setups ARE forming and the gates reject all of them.
//                       "Last Block" names which. Amber.
//   trig > 0, sig > 0   working. Green.
//
// Run the same symbol on 5m, 15m and 30m side by side and read that one row.
// If 5m shows a healthy trigger count and no signals while 30m converts, the
// difference is in the gates and the block reasons will say so. If 5m shows
// zero triggers, the trigger construction itself behaves differently across
// timeframes and that is a defect worth hunting.
//
// Either way the question stops being answerable by assertion, which is what it
// has been for two days — including from me.
//
// Also counts SIGNALS over the same window, so signals-per-100-bars is directly
// readable per timeframe instead of being counted by eye off the chart.
//
// Display only. No gate, level or signal behaviour changes.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.36 CHANGELOG — THE REL VOLUME READOUT WAS LYING (display only)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Two days were spent blaming the volume floor for a silent engine, on the
// strength of a dashboard row that was never showing the number the gate uses.
//
//   relVol = volume / ta.sma(volume[1], 20)
//
// The dashboard renders on barstate.islast — always the LIVE, unfinished bar —
// and on that bar `volume` is only partly accumulated. A bar 50% through its
// period reads about 0.50x of its eventual value:
//
//   bar  25% elapsed -> displayed reads 0.25x of true
//   bar  50% elapsed -> 0.50x
//   bar  67% elapsed -> 0.67x
//   bar 100% elapsed -> 1.00x   (what the gate actually evaluates)
//
// Five separate XAUUSD screenshots read 0.48x, 0.51x, 0.52x, 0.53x, 0.54x. That
// tight cluster around one half is the signature of a mid-bar reading, not of a
// volume series — a real one ranges roughly 0.1x to 3x and would never sit in a
// 0.06-wide band across five independent samples. The tell was there and I read
// it as a broken feed instead.
//
// THE GATE WAS NEVER AFFECTED. Signals fire only on barstate.isconfirmed, where
// the bar is complete and relVol is correct. Nothing about signal generation
// changes here. But the readout sent the diagnosis after the volume floor for
// two days, and it is also what made the v3.5.25 degenerate-volume detector
// look necessary on gold when the gold feed was fine all along.
//
// The row now shows the last CONFIRMED bar's value, labelled "(last bar)", and
// colours itself against the floor using that same value.
//
// v3.5.25's detector is unchanged and still correct: it measures the pass rate
// over confirmed bars, not the live one, so it was never fooled by this. The
// BTCUSD feed genuinely was degenerate.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.35 CHANGELOG — TP1 HAD NO CEILING (why trades "hit almost zero TP")
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: two days without a 15m signal, and the 30m entries that do fire
// reach almost no targets. The second half was a defect, visible on the chart.
//
// XAUUSD 30m, trade #87: SL 4384.877, TP1 4421.470, entry just above 4391.
//
//   entry    R      TP1 in R   P(reach TP1 before the stop)
//   4391.5   6.62   4.53R      18%
//   4393.0   8.12   3.50R      22%
//   4395.0  10.12   2.61R      28%
//
// TP1 is the FIRST target and it sat three to four and a half R away. Min TP1
// Reward enforces a FLOOR of 1.0R; nothing ever enforced a ceiling. TP1 is
// simply the nearest pivot or value-area edge above entry, so when no structure
// exists nearby it lands wherever the next one happens to be.
//
// Three consequences, all compounding:
//   • roughly 78% of such trades reach the stop before the first target;
//   • the breakeven move keys off TP1, so it never armed and the FULL position
//     rode full risk for the life of the trade;
//   • the scale-out never began, so a trade that went 3R in favour and turned
//     still booked a complete loss.
// That is not a market condition. That is a plan that cannot pay.
//
// Fix: when the nearest structure candidate is beyond i_maxTp1R x risk, the
// R-multiple is inserted AHEAD of it. The distant level is not discarded — it
// becomes a later target instead of the first one.
//
//   trade #87 old   TP1 3.50R  TP2 4.36R  TP3 5.32R   P(TP1) 22%
//   trade #87 new   TP1 1.00R  TP2 3.50R  TP3 4.36R   P(TP1) 50%
//
// Across a simulated distribution of nearest-structure distances, the share of
// trades reaching TP1 — and therefore arming breakeven — goes from 37.7% with
// no ceiling to 50.0% at 2.0R.
//
// Default 2.0R, ON. This is not a tuning preference: an unbounded first target
// makes the breakeven rule and the scale-out dead code on exactly the setups
// where they matter most. It only changes plans whose nearest structure was
// already out of reach. Set 0 to restore the old behaviour.
//
// Note what this does NOT claim. Expectancy on a fair game is zero at any
// target spacing — v3.5.34's analysis showed that. A closer TP1 buys reach
// probability and lets the de-risking machinery function; it does not create
// edge. The entries still have to beat random by more than costs.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.34 CHANGELOG — TIMEFRAME AUTO-SCALING (the two settings that BREAK, not
// merely differ)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Asked: fix all timeframes. Most bar-based settings are legitimately
// bar-based — pivots, trigger freshness and cooldown all measure structure, and
// structure is counted in bars. Two settings were different: they did not just
// mean something different per timeframe, they stopped working.
//
// 1. HTF TIMEFRAME WAS A FIXED 240
//      tf    HTF 240      auto     old verdict   auto verdict
//      1m    240          240      OK            OK
//      5m    240          240      OK            OK
//      15m   240          240      OK            OK
//      30m   240          D        OK            OK
//      1H    240          D        OK            OK
//      4H    240          D        degenerate    OK
//      1D    240          W        LOOKAHEAD     OK
//      1W    240          M        LOOKAHEAD     OK
//    On 4H it requested the chart's own series, collapsing the trend filter to
//    close[1] > EMA50[1] on the data being traded. On Daily and above it
//    requested a LOWER timeframe, which with lookahead_on can evaluate a
//    historical bar using information from later inside that bar's own period.
//    v3.5.28 detected this and switched the filter OFF; this supplies a correct
//    higher timeframe instead. The mapping returns 240 for everything at or
//    below 15m, so no chart that previously worked changes.
//
// 2. FRVP LOOKBACK WAS A FIXED 100 BARS
//      tf    old hrs   auto   auto hrs
//      1m      1.7      500     8.3
//      5m      8.3      288    24.0
//      15m    25.0      100    25.0
//      30m+   50.0+     100    unchanged
//    100 bars is 25 hours on 15m and more above it, but 8.3 hours on 5m and 1.7
//    on 1m — not enough trade to define a value area, while VAH and VAL are TP
//    candidates AND one of the three confluence legs. The profile was noise on
//    exactly the timeframes it was being asked to guide scalps on. Auto takes
//    max(setting, bars for 24h) capped at 500, so it only ever extends and
//    nothing at 15m or above moves.
//
// Both default ON. Both are pure corrections — no threshold was guessed, and
// every chart where the old values were sound is byte-identical.
//
// Not changed, and deliberately: i_fvgMaxAge, i_sweepLook, i_cooldown,
// i_triggerAge and i_maxBars are all counted in bars because what they measure
// is counted in bars. That they represent different durations per timeframe is
// the correct behaviour, not a defect.
//
// Process note: the linter's undefined/use-before-definition check (added in
// v3.5.25 after a different slip) caught effHtfTf being declared after the
// request.security call that consumes it. That would have been a compile error
// on the chart.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.33 CHANGELOG — MARKET CLOSURES WERE BEING RECORDED AS FAIR VALUE GAPS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: FVG accuracy differs per timeframe and is mostly broken, while
// POC/VAH/VAL read correctly. Both halves of that are right, for one reason.
//
// An FVG is a THREE-BAR pattern and nothing verified those three bars were
// contiguous in time. A market closure produces the identical shape — Friday's
// last bar, then Sunday's open — so every weekend was recorded as an imbalance,
// on every intraday timeframe, and it cleared the 0.35 ATR minimum without
// effort because a closure gap dwarfs any real one:
//
//   tf    normal 3-bar span   XAUUSD weekend span   ratio
//   5m    600s                172800s               288x
//   15m   1800s               172800s                96x
//   1H    7200s               172800s                24x
//   4H    28800s              172800s                 6x
//   1D    172800s             172800s                 1x   (unaffected)
//
// That ratio column is also the answer to "it differs per timeframe": the lower
// the timeframe, the more absurd the false gap, so the corruption scaled with
// how fast you traded.
//
// A closure gap is an absence of trading, not liquidity left behind by
// aggressive orders, and price returning to it carries no information. The cost
// went past a bad signal: v3.5.7 anchors the stop beyond the retested gap's far
// edge and v3.5.6 measures the chase cap from its near edge, so one false gap
// mispriced an entire plan — a stop placed across a weekend's worth of range.
//
// WHY POC/VAH/VAL WERE FINE. The profile aggregates volume across 100 bars into
// 32 bins. A single anomalous bar shifts a bin slightly and moves nothing else.
// A three-bar pattern has no such averaging — one bad triple IS the whole
// signal. Robustness to a data artefact is what separated the two, not accuracy
// of the maths.
//
// Fix: a three-bar window whose elapsed time exceeds i_fvgGapTol x the normal
// span is rejected. Verified across timeframes — every intraday weekend is
// rejected, every normal window is kept, and a daily Thursday-Friday-Monday
// window (4 days against a 6-day threshold) is untouched.
//
// Gap sizing, retest logic and the multi-gap tracking are unchanged.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.32 CHANGELOG — LEVEL-RETEST ENTRIES (opt-in, default off)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported, and correct: "the buy/sell you call happens after the movement, not
// during it — it is no more than a description of a previous market move."
//
// That is structural, not incidental. Every trigger requires its event to be
// COMPLETE before it can fire: MSS needs the close to have already broken the
// pivot, a sweep needs the wick to have already speared the level AND closed
// back, an FVG retest needs price already returned to the gap with a rejection
// close. Add bar-close confirmation (+1 bar), the pivot itself not being known
// until i_swingLen bars after it forms (+2-3), and up to i_triggerAge bars of
// freshness (+3), and a 15m entry can land nearly two hours after the move that
// defines it. That lag is the price of not repainting — a signal that appears
// DURING a move is a signal that can vanish when the bar closes, which is the
// exact defect v3.5.11, v3.5.17 and v3.5.19 were spent removing.
//
// What can be fixed is WHERE the entry is taken. Adds i_entryMode:
//
//   MARKET (CONFIRMED)  default, unchanged. Buys the close of the qualifying
//                       bar, wherever price has drifted to by then.
//   LEVEL RETEST        the qualifying bar ARMS an order at the trigger's own
//                       level — broken pivot for MSS, gap edge for an FVG,
//                       swept level for a sweep — and the entry fires only if
//                       price returns there within i_pendBars.
//
// The stop, risk distance, both targets and both reward gates are now measured
// from buyEntryRef/sellEntryRef rather than from close, so in level mode the
// whole plan describes the trade that would actually be filled instead of one
// priced at a bar the order never touches. In market mode those references are
// literally `close`, so every downstream formula is byte-identical to v3.5.31.
//
// SIMULATED, AND THE RESULT IS NOT A CLEAN WIN:
//   market entry   median TP1 reward 1.62R
//   level  entry   median TP1 reward 2.10R    (+30% per filled trade)
//   at an assumed 62% fill rate, expected R PER TRIGGER:
//   market 1.617   level 1.301
// Entering at the level pays materially more per trade, and you forgo roughly
// four setups in ten because price never comes back. On reward alone that nets
// NEGATIVE. It only turns positive if the setups that never fill are also the
// ones that lose more often — plausible, since a move that never retests is a
// move that ran away from a chased entry, but entirely unproven here.
//
// So this ships OFF. It is the honest answer to a real criticism, not an
// improvement I can demonstrate. Run one range in each mode and compare profit
// factor; the simulation above is a reason to test it, not a reason to trust it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.31 CHANGELOG — REVERT v3.5.30. THE SIMULATION ASKED THE WRONG QUESTION
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v3.5.30 clamped Scalp trigger freshness from 3 to 2, on a simulation showing
// that an entry taken at age 3 passes chase/risk/rr less often than one taken
// at age 1. That is true and it is beside the point.
//
// Trade COUNT depends on whether a trigger yields an entry ANYWHERE inside its
// freshness window, and each extra bar is another attempt:
//   window 1  64.6%    window 2  80.4%    window 3  86.6%    window 5  91.0%
//
// So the clamp REDUCED Scalp conversion by roughly 7% while the reported
// problem was that Scalp produced no trades at all. It made the complaint worse
// and was shipped as a fix for it.
//
// Reverted to a passthrough. The quality effect is real — a later entry is the
// same target at a worse price with a wider stop — so lowering i_triggerAge by
// hand remains a legitimate quality-for-count trade. It is not something to
// impose by default, and certainly not while the engine is silent.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.30 CHANGELOG — THREE BLOCK REASONS WERE ONE PROBLEM
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: "BUY x vol risk chase rr", every mode weak, losing.
//
// Three of those four gates are not independent. They all measure HOW LATE the
// entry is. i_triggerAge (default 3) keeps a trigger valid for three bars so
// order flow can confirm on N+1/N+2 — but every bar of delay walks the entry
// away from the level while the target stays where it is. The entry price gets
// worse, the stop widens, and the reward shrinks. chase, risk and rr then
// reject the very setups the delay produced.
//
// Simulated over 200,000 triggers with a drifting entry:
//   triggerAge   chase ok   risk ok   rr ok    all three
//   1            100.0%     81.4%     88.6%    70.9%
//   2             98.3%     79.4%     81.6%    62.5%
//   3 (default)   93.6%     76.7%     75.3%    55.7%
//   5             83.1%     70.1%     65.5%    46.4%
//
// The setups that DO pass at age 3 are disproportionately the drifted ones —
// same target, worse price, wider stop. That is a mechanism for "weak and loss
// print", not just for fewer signals.
//
// Scalp now clamps freshness to 2, keeping one bar of order-flow confirmation
// while dropping the third bar, which costs the most. Other modes are
// unchanged: they are slower by design and the confirmation is worth more to
// them. Set i_triggerAge to 1 to test same-bar-only entries.
//
// HONEST LIMIT: the simulation prices the COST of waiting. It does not price
// the BENEFIT — triggers whose own bar fails the order-flow gate and would be
// confirmed a bar later are lost as freshness shrinks. Which dominates is not
// something reasoning about the code can settle. The strategy build now
// measures it: run one range at 3 and one at 1 and compare profit factor.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.29 CHANGELOG — WIDENING THE SCALP STOP COULD BLOCK EVERY SETUP
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: zero trades on Scalp 15m.
//
// v3.5.26 made the Scalp stop buffer adjustable (i_scalpSlCap) and gave Scalp
// the volatility multiplier the other modes already had. The advice given with
// it — "raise this for a more comfortable stop" — omitted that Scalp's max-risk
// cap is a hard 2.0 ATR and that riskDist is the buffer PLUS the distance to
// the structural anchor. Widen the buffer far enough and the buffer alone
// consumes the cap, so riskOkBuy/riskOkSell can never pass and the mode goes
// silent rather than trading with a wider stop:
//
//   i_slAtr  cap   worst buf   room under a 2.0 cap
//   0.5      0.3   0.39        1.61   fine
//   0.5      1.5   0.65        1.35   fine (i_slAtr still bounds it)
//   1.5      1.5   1.95        0.05   nothing can pass
//   3.0      1.5   1.95        0.05   nothing can pass
//
// Note the second row: slBuf = min(i_slAtr, i_scalpSlCap) x slMult, so BOTH
// inputs bound it. Raising only the cap does nothing — which also means the
// earlier advice to raise it alone was wrong on its own terms.
//
// Fix: Scalp's max-risk cap now floors at the worst-case buffer plus 0.75 ATR
// of room for structure, so widening the stop widens the risk window with it
// instead of quietly closing the mode down.
//
// This is a guard, not a diagnosis. It only explains zero trades if BOTH the
// buffer inputs were raised. If they are at defaults the cause is elsewhere and
// the dashboard "Last Block" row names it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.28 CHANGELOG — HTF FILTER WAS NOT A HTF FILTER ON 4H AND ABOVE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: Strict loses on 4H while Scalp is profitable on the same chart —
// "there are errors". There was one, and it explains the asymmetry directly.
//
// i_htfTf defaults to "240". On a 4H chart that requests the SAME timeframe, so
// request.security returns the chart's own series and htfBull collapses to
//     close[1] > ta.ema(close, 50)[1]
// on the very data being traded — a lagged moving-average test wearing the name
// of a higher-timeframe trend filter. On a Daily chart it is worse: the request
// is for a LOWER timeframe than the chart.
//
//   chart   HTF 240 above chart?   Strict            Scalp
//   15m     yes                    real HTF gate     bypassed
//   1H      yes                    real HTF gate     bypassed
//   4H      NO                     bogus gate        bypassed
//   1D      NO                     bogus gate        bypassed
//
// Strict REQUIRES htfOkBull/htfOkBear. Scalp bypasses the HTF gate entirely by
// design (modePassBull = isScalp ? notExtended : ...). So on a 4H chart Strict
// was being gated by a filter that was not measuring what it claimed, and Scalp
// was not gated by it at all. One mode losing while the other wins on identical
// data is exactly what that produces — no other explanation is needed.
//
// Adds i_htfGuard (default ON): when the configured HTF is not strictly above
// the chart timeframe, the filter self-disables instead of applying a
// mislabelled test, and the dashboard shows a yellow warning on the HTF row.
//
// The REAL fix is to set an HTF genuinely above the chart — Daily on a 4H
// chart, Weekly on a Daily chart. The guard stops the engine lying about what
// it is doing; it does not supply the missing higher-timeframe context.
//
// Known and deliberately left alone: buyAligned/sellAligned still count htfBull
// as one of their three confluence legs even when the HTF is degenerate, so the
// displayed confluence score on a 4H chart includes that lagged EMA read.
// Changing it would move every confluence number and the invalidation exit that
// reads them; that belongs in its own version with its own before/after.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.27 CHANGELOG — STRATEGY SCALE-OUT WAS BROKEN (backtest, not the engine)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: the backtest showed a huge loss.
//
// The cause was in the generated strategy build, not in the signal engine. The
// indicator, its levels and its alerts were never affected — only the measured
// results were, which means every backtest run before this version understated
// the engine by an unknown but large margin.
//
// 1. THE SCALE-OUT LIQUIDATED WINNERS AT TP1
//    Exits were issued as qty_percent 33/33/34 and re-issued EVERY BAR so a
//    breakeven move would reach the live orders. But qty_percent is a
//    percentage of the CURRENT position, and an exit ID becomes reusable once
//    filled. So after TP1 filled, L1 was recreated the next bar as 33% of what
//    REMAINED, at a limit price the market had already passed — and filled
//    again immediately. And again the bar after that.
//
//    Simulated: 1.00 -> 0.67 -> 0.45 -> 0.30 -> 0.20 -> 0.13 -> 0.09, all at
//    TP1's price, while TP2 and TP3 barely participated.
//
//    Effect on a plan with TP1 1.5R, TP2 2R, TP3 5R:
//      reaches TP1 only   broken +1.459R   fixed +0.495R
//      reaches TP2        broken +1.514R   fixed +1.155R
//      reaches TP3        broken +1.514R   fixed +2.855R
//      stopped out        broken -1.000R   fixed -1.000R
//    Every winner capped near +1.5R however far it ran; every loser still paid
//    a full -1R. The right tail this engine depends on was deleted, and a
//    5R trade scored the same as one that merely tagged TP1.
//
//    Fixed: leg quantities are absolute, fixed at entry, and a leg is never
//    re-issued once its target has traded.
//
// 2. POSITION SIZE WAS UNCAPPED
//    Risk-normalised sizing divides by stop distance, so a very tight stop
//    produced an enormous position — at 1% risk and a 1-point gold stop that is
//    roughly 44x equity in notional. A handful of tight-stop trades could
//    dominate the entire equity curve. Adds i_stMaxLev, default 5x equity.
//
// This does not make the engine profitable, and nothing here should be read as
// evidence that it is. It means the previous backtest measured a broken
// execution model rather than the strategy. Re-run it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.26 CHANGELOG — SCALP STOP COMFORT + BLENDED SCALE-OUT REWARD
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Asked for: a more comfortable stop, and more Scalp trades WITHOUT giving up
// accuracy. Those normally fight each other — a wider stop means a bigger R,
// which makes the reward gate harder to clear. Two genuine defects made it
// possible to do both.
//
// 1. SCALP HAD THE THINNEST STOP IN THE WORST CONDITIONS
//    slBuf = isScalp ? riskAtrRef * math.min(i_slAtr, 0.3) : riskAtrRef *
//            i_slAtr * slMult
//    Scalp was hard-clamped to 0.3 ATR AND was the only mode denied slMult, so
//    where every other mode widened its pad by 1.3x in expanding volatility and
//    1.2x in chop, Scalp held a flat 0.3 ATR through both. The padding was at
//    its thinnest in exactly the regimes most likely to overrun it. Scalp now
//    takes slMult like every other mode, and the clamp is an input
//    (i_scalpSlCap) so it can be widened further.
//
// 2. THE REWARD GATE MEASURED A THIRD OF THE PLAN
//    The position exits 33/33/34 across TP1/TP2/TP3, but the gate judged the
//    setup on TP1 alone. A plan with a modest first target and strong second
//    and third was rejected on a number that never described it. On 15m gold
//    the nearest pivot frequently sits inside 1R, which is why "rr" appears in
//    nearly every blocked signal.
//
//    A setup now passes on EITHER the TP1 test (unchanged) OR the weighted
//    reward of the whole scale-out clearing i_minBlendRR. This raises trade
//    count by correcting WHAT IS MEASURED, not by lowering the bar.
//
//    Simulated over 200,000 synthetic setups:
//      admitted by TP1 test alone      63.56%
//      admitted by either path         73.21%
//      increase                        +9.65 points, +15% relative
//    and the extra trades are not marginal:
//      median TP1 0.61R, TP2 1.54R, TP3 3.20R, blended 1.79R
//
//    Guarded twice. TP1 must still clear i_minTp1Floor (0.5R) so a target a few
//    ticks out can never be waved through by a strong runner behind it. And a
//    trade admitted on the blended path does NOT arm the breakeven move
//    (activeBeOk) — a close TP1 dragging the stop to entry is exactly the
//    scratch machine the v3.5.8 reward gate was built to stop, and re-creating
//    it while widening the stop would have undone both changes at once.
//
// Both effects are UNMEASURED against real price. The simulation shows how many
// setups the gate admits and their shape; it says nothing about whether they
// win. Set i_minBlendRR to 0 to restore v3.5.25 behaviour exactly.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.25 CHANGELOG — DEGENERATE VOLUME DETECTION (Scalp fires nothing on a
// broker feed)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: Scalp on BTCUSD produced no trades at all, for days, while gold on
// the same build traded normally.
//
// Cause: the volume floor, not the setups. relVol is volume / 20-bar average
// volume, and Scalp clamps the floor to min(i_volFloor, 1.0) = 1.0. A feed that
// prints a low baseline with periodic large spikes keeps its own rolling
// average elevated, so ordinary bars read far below 1.0 permanently and volOk
// can essentially never be satisfied. That is not filtering — it is a standing
// veto produced by bad data, and no other gate can compensate.
//
// Observed across five screenshots of the same symbol: 0.11x, 0.19x, 0.23x,
// 0.33x, then 2.88x.
//
// The engine already tolerated a symbol with NO volume (volDataSeen). It had no
// concept of volume that exists but is unusable.
//
// Fix: measure whether the floor is passable AT ALL — the share of the last
// i_volSanityLen bars that could clear it. Below i_volSanityMin, the series
// cannot support a magnitude test and the FLOOR ALONE is bypassed. The
// dashboard shows "⚠VOL?" in amber, never green, while that is active.
//
// Threshold set from simulation, floor 1.0, 20-bar average, 500-bar window:
//   healthy (lognormal)     median relVol 0.90, pass-rate 0.41
//   spiky 1-in-20 at 100x   median relVol 0.17, pass-rate 0.05
//   spiky 1-in-10 at 30x    median relVol 0.26, pass-rate 0.10
//   all zero                pass-rate 0.00
// A first attempt defaulted to 0.05 and would have caught nothing, since the
// observed feed sits exactly there. Default is 0.15, which separates the two
// families with margin at both ends.
//
// Deliberately narrow: no volume is invented and no other gate is loosened. CVD
// and FRVP still read the same broken series, which is why the warning is
// surfaced rather than hidden. This makes the mode usable on a bad feed; it
// does not make the feed good. A real exchange symbol remains the better fix.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.24 CHANGELOG — INVALIDATION MEASURED AS DEGRADATION FROM ENTRY
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v3.5.23 compared a live trade's confluence against a fixed threshold. That
// broke Scalp outright, and the report was immediate: "healthy trade: no — it
// makes it useless in scalp."
//
// Correct. Scalp bypasses the HTF/VWAP/FRVP gates deliberately —
//   modePassBull = i_mode == "Aggressive" ? true : isScalp ? notExtended : (...)
// and effMinConf falls to 0 in Scalp/High — so a Scalp trade may legitimately
// enter at 0/3 confluence. Judging it afterwards against those same three legs
// closed it for a thesis it never claimed: entered on a sweep, killed by an HTF
// bias the setup was never asked to have. On a trending 15m chart that fires
// two bars after entry, over and over.
//
// The trade's own confluence at entry is now recorded (activeEntryConf) and
// invalidation additionally requires ownNow < activeEntryConf. A plan can only
// be invalidated by losing alignment it actually HAD:
//
//   entered 0/3 (Scalp/High)  -> can never invalidate on confluence
//   entered 2/3               -> invalidates only if it drops below 2
//   entered 3/3 (Strict)      -> the BTCUSD 1D #10 case, still closes
//
// Verified against 9 sequences covering Scalp entered at 0/3 with the HTF fully
// against it, Scalp at 1/3 drifting, Scalp 2/3 collapsing, Strict healthy,
// Strict pulling back, a one-bar flip that recovers, the observed #10 reading,
// total collapse, and a short-side flip. All behave as specified.
//
// Mirrored in the strategy build (stEntryConf) so backtest and chart agree.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.23 CHANGELOG — INVALIDATION EXIT (the engine now re-reads its own thesis)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The engine scored its conditions once, at entry, and then stopped thinking. A
// plan had exactly four ways out — stop, TP3, the time stop (which ships off)
// and a new signal — so nothing closed a trade whose reason for existing had
// gone. Frequent signals hide this: on XAUUSD 15m Scalp a new signal replaces
// the plan every few hours. Where signals are sparse, the plan simply hangs.
//
// Both observed live on BTCUSD:
//   15m  #62 open three days with no exit condition reachable
//   1D   #10 entry 66,519, price 62,991 (-0.58R), dashboard 3/3 SELL vs 1/3
//        BUY, TP1 still anchored to a VAH of 76,558 while the live VAH had
//        fallen to 65,461. The structure the plan was built on no longer
//        existed and the engine had no way to notice.
//
// Adds i_invalidExit: when the OPPOSITE side holds i_invalidConf confluence
// legs and the trade's own side sits at or below i_invalidOwn, for
// i_invalidBars consecutive CLOSED bars, the plan closes.
//
// The own-side ceiling is the part that decides whether this works at all.
// buyAligned and sellAligned are NOT mutually exclusive — frvpBullOk and
// frvpBearOk are both true whenever price is inside the value area — so a fully
// flipped market commonly reads 3/3 against and 1/3 for. A first cut demanded
// the own side at exactly 0 and, tested against the trade that motivated the
// whole rule, did not fire: #10 read 1/3 BUY for weeks. Ceiling defaults to 1.
//
// Not treated as a stop-out: no cooldown extension, no loss streak. A trade
// closed for a dead thesis says nothing about the next setup.
//
// Mirrored in the strategy build so it is measurable, reading position side
// from strategy.position_size so chart and backtest cannot drift.
//
// State machine verified against 7 sequences: healthy trade, ordinary pullback,
// 2/2 chop inside the value area, one-bar flip that recovers, the observed #10
// reading, total collapse, and a short-side flip. All behave as specified.
//
// ON by default. Shipping the time stop disabled is what let these trades hang;
// repeating that would be the same mistake twice. EFFECT ON PROFIT FACTOR IS
// UNMEASURED — it will also close trades that would have recovered. Run one
// range with it on and off and compare before trusting it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.22 CHANGELOG — SPREAD BUFFER IN ATR UNITS (portability across symbols)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The v3.5.16 spread buffer was absolute price only. That does not survive a
// change of instrument: 0.30 is roughly 3% of a scalp stop on gold at 4,400 and
// rounding error on BTC at 100,000, so one input silently meant different
// things per symbol and a setting carried from gold to crypto quietly stopped
// modelling anything.
//
// Adds i_spreadMode. "Price" is the original behaviour and remains the default,
// so nothing changes unless it is switched. "× ATR" measures the buffer in the
// instrument's own volatility, so a single setting is correct on every symbol
// and timeframe.
//
// This is a units fix, not a tuning change — no threshold was guessed at and no
// signal logic moved. It is the only code change needed to run this engine on
// crypto; everything else that differs (24/7 sessions, no news windows) is
// already handled by inputs that default to off.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.21 CHANGELOG — TP SPACING (R-multiples vs structure levels)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The v3.5.3 minGap merge kept two STRUCTURE levels from landing on top of each
// other, but it never compared structure against the R-multiple fallbacks that
// fill any leftover slot. A fallback could therefore sit a fraction of a point
// from a pivot or VAH, and two of the three targets became the same price with
// different labels — the scale-out lost a leg while still carrying full risk.
//
// On the reported XAUUSD trade: pivot 4362.000 in one slot and the 2R fallback
// at 4362.486 in another, 0.486 apart against a minGap of 2.332.
//
// Fix: structure levels are placed first and keep their EXACT prices — a nudged
// pivot is not that pivot any more and its tag would be false. Only fallbacks
// move: each is pushed outward, away from entry, until it clears every level
// already placed, then retagged with the R multiple it actually lands on. The
// push runs a bounded number of passes; there is no unbounded loop.
//
// This changes level selection on trades where a fallback previously collided,
// and only on those. When no collision existed the three levels are unchanged.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.20 CHANGELOG — TP REACH CAP, OPT-IN AND OFF BY DEFAULT
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: "didn't hit TP3 at the 4399 it put in the spot."
//
// Real asymmetry found: f_tpLevels accepts a structure candidate (pivot / VAH /
// VAL) at ANY distance — the only test is that the level sits the correct side
// of entry. Scalp meanwhile clamps its R-multiple fallbacks to 1.0/1.5/2.0R, so
// the engine enforces a ceiling on one source of targets and none on the other,
// and a VAH can hand a 15-minute scalp a 6R runner.
//
// The obvious fix — cap structure targets at the mode's own TP3 — was built,
// simulated against the reported trade, and REJECTED as the default:
//   entry 4343.830, risk 9.328, TP3 tagged VAH at 4399.366 = 5.95R
//   price ran to 4397.0 = 5.70R, missing the target by 0.25R (2.4 points)
//   reported exit 4395.0 = 5.49R
// The target was not unreachable. Capping at Scalp's 2R would have closed the
// runner at 4362 and surrendered a move that paid 5.49R. On the only trade
// there is evidence for, the cap loses money.
//
// So the cap ships as i_maxTpR, DEFAULT 0 = OFF: behaviour is identical to
// v3.5.19 unless it is deliberately switched on. It is a right-tail versus
// fill-rate trade-off and there is no backtest to settle it — a single trade
// is not evidence either way, and it happens to argue against capping.
//
// Also found while simulating, NOT yet changed: the minGap merge that keeps two
// targets from landing on top of each other runs over structure candidates
// only. R-multiple fallbacks are not gap-checked against them, so a fallback
// can land within a fraction of a point of a structure level and collapse the
// scale-out. Left alone deliberately — fixing it changes level selection on
// every trade in every mode, which is not something to ship untested on top of
// a repaint fix. Logged for a version of its own.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.19 CHANGELOG — THIRD REPAINT SOURCE (FRVP stride keyed to bar_index)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: "I did take the third TP, then it changed to TP3 4399." The v3.5.18
// note below blamed this on a second trade being drawn. That was wrong. The
// chart settled it: ONE plan, entry 4343.830, TP1 4357.822 (1.5R) and TP2
// 4362.486 (2R) identical across both renders — while TP3, tagged VAH, read
// 4395.110 on one and 4399.366 on the next. A second trade would have moved the
// entry and all three targets. Only the structure-anchored one moved.
//
// Cause: the volume profile rebuilt on `bar_index % i_frvpEvery == 0`.
// bar_index is counted from the first bar the CHART HAS LOADED, not from a
// fixed origin, so a different history depth — scrolling back, a reload, a
// settings change — shifts every index by a constant and the modulo then picks
// a DIFFERENT set of bars to snapshot the profile on. POC/VAH/VAL at any given
// bar therefore differ between two renders of the same chart, and any target
// anchored to them moves with it. TP1/TP2 are pure R-multiples off entry and
// stop and were never exposed; only VAH/VAL-tagged targets were.
//
// This is the third repaint source found in this engine, after the FRVP
// `barstate.islast` rebuild (v3.5.11) and the HTF `request.security` returning
// the developing bar (v3.5.17). It survived v3.5.11 because the fix's own
// comment asserted "the stride alone is deterministic" without checking what
// bar_index is anchored to.
//
// Fix: the stride is now keyed to the bar's open time divided by the timeframe
// — an absolute quantity identical on every render — so the same bars always
// carry the rebuild no matter how much history is loaded.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.18 CHANGELOG — LEVELS CARRY THEIR TRADE NUMBER (display only)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Added while diagnosing the report that v3.5.19 above actually fixes. The
// diagnosis in this entry was wrong — see v3.5.19 — but the change stands on
// its own: every TP/SL/BE label and every BUY/SELL arrow now carries the signal
// number (" TP3 #12 4399.00 VAH", "BUY #12"), so two plans can never be
// confused for one moving plan. The number is the same monotonic counter the
// alert payload already sends as "seq", so chart and webhook agree on identity.
//
// Display only — no gate, level or signal changes.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.17 CHANGELOG — SECOND REPAINT SOURCE (HTF security)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: a live Scalp 15m signal vanished — on v3.5.9 AND on v3.5.14. Two
// separate causes, both mine. v3.5.9 was the FRVP live-bar rebuild (fixed in
// v3.5.11). v3.5.14 already had that fix, so the disappearance there was this:
//
// The HTF request used [close, ema] with lookahead_off. On a live chart bar
// inside a still-forming HTF bar, that returns the DEVELOPING higher-timeframe
// close, updating tick by tick. After a reload the same bar is historical and
// gets the last CLOSED HTF bar instead. Different values, so htfBull could flip
// between what was watched and what is redrawn.
//
// It reached the signals through the confluence score: buyAligned/sellAligned
// count htfBull, and v3.5.13 gave Scalp a confluence floor of 1. A Scalp entry
// scoring 1/3 live could score 0/3 on reload and cease to exist. In Balanced
// and Strict it also feeds htfOkBull and the structural bias directly.
//
// Now requests close[1] with lookahead_on — the standard stable idiom, giving
// the last CLOSED HTF bar, identical live and historical. The offset is what
// makes lookahead_on safe: without it lookahead_on WOULD leak future data.
// Cost: the HTF view lags by up to one HTF bar. That is the honest price of a
// value that does not change after the fact.
//
// Note on the claim in the v3.5 header. "It does NOT repaint" has now been
// wrong twice — the FRVP path from v3.5.1, and this one since v3.5 shipped,
// reaching signals from v3.5.13. Both are fixed and the repaint surface has
// been re-audited, but the claim was made before it had been earned.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.16 CHANGELOG — SPREAD-AWARE STOPS, TP/SL ZONES, TRACKER TYPO
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. NOTHING EVER MODELLED SPREAD. The stop sat exactly on the structural
//    level, so on any symbol with a spread the level could be taken out by the
//    quote while the traded price never reached it — a loss the chart says
//    never happened. On XAUUSD a 0.30 spread against a 9-point scalp stop is
//    ~3% of risk, and worse on a tighter stop. New "Spread + Slippage Buffer"
//    (price units, default 0 = unchanged) pushes the stop further out and lets
//    the reported R grow to match; an honest R beats a flattering one.
//    Only the STOP is buffered. Buffering the targets would move them closer
//    in effective terms and make the plan look better than it fills.
//    The clamped/structural flag is evaluated BEFORE the buffer, since spread
//    is a broker cost rather than an engine decision.
//
// 2. TP/SL AS ZONES (display only). A hairline implies a precision the fill
//    will not honour — spread, slippage and the tick you actually get all sit
//    around the level, not on it. Optional bands, height in ATR. Changes no
//    signal and no level.
//
// 3. BLOCK-REASON TRACKER TESTED THE WRONG SIGNAL. The sell branch read
//    "not buySignal" where it meant "not sellSignal" — my typo from the v3.5.8
//    restructure. It was masked by the empty-reason guard (a valid SELL passes
//    every gate, so the reason string came out empty and nothing was written),
//    so it never produced a wrong message. It would have started lying the
//    moment any gate was added that a passing SELL does not satisfy.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.15 CHANGELOG — TARGETS WERE NOT IN ORDER (present since v3.4.1)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported from a live XAUUSD chart: TP1 4395.11, TP2 4357.82, TP3 4362.49 on a
// LONG. TP1 was the furthest target, not the nearest.
//
// f_tpLevels fills the three slots from structure levels first, then pads any
// remainder with R-multiple fallbacks — but it never sorted the two sets
// TOGETHER. With exactly one qualifying structure level (here the VAH at 5.5R)
// that level took TP1, and the 1.5R and 2R fallbacks landed below it as TP2 and
// TP3. Any signal with one or two structure candidates could invert this way.
//
// Consequences beyond a confusing chart:
//   • The scale-out plan was backwards — a third of the position sized for the
//     nearest target was actually assigned the furthest.
//   • The breakeven move keys off TP1. With TP1 at 5.5R it would essentially
//     never arm, so the de-risk the engine advertises never happened.
//   • activeTp3Px sat NEARER than activeTp1Px, so tracking closed on the 2R
//     level while TP1 was still unreached — the trade silently resolved on what
//     the chart called TP3.
//   • The strategy build issued its three exits in the wrong sequence.
//
// The three are now sorted in the trade's direction with their tags following
// them. Which levels get chosen is unchanged; only their order is fixed.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.14 CHANGELOG — FREQUENCY IS NOW A DIAL, NOT A SIDE EFFECT
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Asked for a higher win rate, then for at least 4 signals a day. Those pull in
// opposite directions: win rate comes from fewer/better trades, frequency from
// looser ones. Rather than quietly re-tune and pretend both were delivered, the
// trade-off is now a single Scalp-only control with its cost documented.
//
//   SELECTIVE (default) cooldown 5, full Min TP1 Reward, confluence floor 1
//                       — byte-for-byte the v3.5.13 behavior.
//   STANDARD            cooldown 4, TP1 reward capped 0.9R, confluence floor 1
//   HIGH                cooldown 2, TP1 reward capped 0.7R, no confluence floor
//
// The cooldown is the cheap part: shortening it does not lower the bar for any
// individual setup, it just lets qualifying setups sit closer together. The
// other two are not cheap. A 0.7R first target means TP1 sits nearer than the
// stop, and since TP1 also triggers the breakeven move, more trades will end as
// scratches — the exact failure mode v3.5.8's reward gate was built to stop.
// Dropping the confluence floor removes the only trend context Scalp has left.
//
// Sweep Pivot Length is deliberately NOT touched by the preset: lowering it back
// toward 2 re-creates the v3.5.13 defect where sweeps hunted 5-bar wiggles. Set
// it to 3 or 4 by hand if you want more sweeps and accept shallower liquidity.
//
// SIGNALS PER DAY IS A PROPERTY OF THE MARKET, NOT THIS SETTING. Nothing here
// guarantees 4/day. Measure the real rate in the strategy tester.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.13 CHANGELOG — SCALP SWEEPS WERE HUNTING NOISE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. SWEEP LEVELS INHERITED THE ENTRY PIVOT LENGTH. Sweep levels came from
//    lastPH/lastPL, which use effSwingLen — and Scalp clamps that to 2 bars
//    per side. A 2-bar swing on a 15m chart is a 5-bar wiggle, not a liquidity
//    pool: nobody has stops resting under it, so violating it and closing back
//    is not a stop run, it is noise with a setup's name on it. Scalp leans on
//    the sweep trigger harder than any other mode (v3.5.1 even exempted sweep
//    scalps from the CVD and momentum gates to get them firing), so the mode
//    that depends on sweeps most was aiming them at the shallowest structure
//    on the chart.
//    Sweeps now use their OWN pivot series at a dedicated length (default 5 =
//    an 11-bar swing), independent of the entry pivots and not overridden by
//    any mode. Entry structure stays fast; the liquidity being hunted is real.
//
// 2. SCALP NOW REQUIRES AT LEAST 1 OF 3 CONFLUENCE. Scalp bypasses the HTF
//    gate, the structural bias, and for sweeps the CVD and momentum gates too.
//    That is most of the engine's context switched off, which is what lets it
//    trade both directions. Requiring one of the three directional alignments
//    (HTF side / VWAP side / value-area side) puts a floor under that without
//    reverting Scalp into Balanced. Clamped upward like every other Scalp
//    setting, so 2 or 3 is respected if you set it.
//    UNMEASURED judgment, unlike (1) which is a defect. Set Min Confluence
//    higher to test, or use another mode if you want it off entirely.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.12 CHANGELOG — SELECTIVITY LEVERS (asked for: higher Scalp win rate)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Win rate is trivially gameable — move TP1 closer and it rises while
// expectancy falls. Everything here therefore takes FEWER, BETTER trades
// rather than banking sooner. All three default to permissive, so the engine
// is unchanged until one is deliberately switched on, and each can be A/B
// tested alone.
//
// 1. TRADING SESSION FILTER. The engine had a news blackout but no session
//    filter at all — it would take a 15m Scalp in the Asian session, where
//    gold ranges without follow-through and a continuation engine bleeds.
//    Probably the highest-value single lever on an intraday chart.
// 2. MIN CONFLUENCE GATE. buyAligned/sellAligned (HTF side + VWAP side +
//    value-area side) have been computed since v3.3 and, until v3.5.5, never
//    even read. Now they can gate. This matters most in Scalp, which bypasses
//    the HTF and structural-bias gates by design: requiring 1 or 2 restores
//    some trend context without collapsing Scalp back into Balanced.
// 3. VOLATILITY REGIME FILTER. volRegime has likewise been computed and
//    displayed for nine versions while gating nothing. "Skip chop" avoids
//    compression where breakouts fail; "Skip expansion" avoids entering after
//    the move is spent.
//
// Which of these helps is symbol-dependent and UNMEASURED. Enable one at a
// time and compare profit factor, not win rate.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.11 CHANGELOG — REPAINT FIX (the engine did repaint; it does not now)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: "the indicator draws different signals". It did, and this was why.
//
// The FRVP refresh condition carried an "or barstate.islast" term, added in
// v3.5.1 alongside the load-time work. It gave the LIVE bar a profile rebuild
// that the same bar no longer received once it scrolled into history, so with
// the default stride of 5 roughly four bars in five held different POC/VAH/VAL
// in real time than on reload.
//
// That leaked into signals by two paths:
//   • VAH and VAL are TP candidates, so TP1 could differ — and since v3.5.8 the
//     reward gate decides the signal FROM TP1. A signal could therefore appear
//     live and be gone on reload, in EVERY mode.
//   • Strict gates on frvpBullOk/frvpBearOk directly.
//
// The header has claimed "100% bar-close confirmed — it does NOT repaint" since
// v3.5. From v3.5.1 that was untrue for anything downstream of the profile, and
// from v3.5.8 it was untrue for the signals themselves. Removing the islast term
// makes the rebuild purely a function of bar_index: identical live and on
// reload. The cost is the disclosed one — the profile can lag up to
// i_frvpEvery-1 bars. Set the input to 1 for fresh-every-bar; that is equally
// deterministic, just slower to load.
//
// Drawing still happens on the last bar only (rendering is not state, and
// redrawing a line does not change a signal).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.10 CHANGELOG — FVG TRACKING HOLDS MULTIPLE GAPS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. ONE GAP PER DIRECTION WAS ALL THAT SURVIVED. lBullTop/lBullBot were single
//    slots: every new FVG overwrote the previous one, so an older gap that
//    price had not yet returned to was discarded the moment another formed.
//    In a fast impulsive leg — which is precisely what stacks FVGs — only the
//    newest was kept, and the newest sits FURTHEST from price, i.e. is the one
//    least likely to be retested. The engine was throwing away its best
//    candidates and keeping its worst.
//    Gaps now live in parallel arrays (new input "Max Gaps Tracked Per
//    Direction", default 5). Each expires on its own age and invalidates on
//    its own level, independently of the others.
//
// 2. THE RETEST NOW REPORTS *WHICH* GAP IT HIT. The scan walks newest-to-oldest
//    and stops at the first qualifying gap, recording that gap's edges. This
//    matters beyond bookkeeping: v3.5.7 anchors the stop to the retested gap's
//    far edge and v3.5.6 measures the chase cap from its near edge. With one
//    slot those were whatever gap happened to be stored; they are now the gap
//    price actually touched.
//
// VERIFIED BY SIMULATION, not by backtest: replaying both algorithms over
// 160,000 synthetic bars, the new one caught every retest the old one caught
// (zero regressions) plus ~25% more that the single slot had discarded.
// EXPECT MORE FVG SIGNALS in Balanced, Aggressive and Scalp. Strict disables
// the FVG trigger, so Strict is unaffected.
//
// Minor: gap expiry is now evaluated on confirmed bars only, consistent with
// invalidation, which was already gated that way. Signals only ever fire on
// confirmed bars, so this changes no signal.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.9 CHANGELOG — WEBHOOK AUTHENTICATION
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A TradingView webhook URL is an unauthenticated public endpoint: anyone who
// learns it can POST a fabricated signal straight into your automation, and
// nothing in the payload previously let a receiver tell a real alert from a
// forged one. New "Webhook Shared Secret" (Alerts group) emits a "secret"
// field as the first key of the JSON object; the companion n8n workflow in
// n8n/ rejects any payload whose secret does not match. Leave it empty to
// omit the field entirely and keep the old payload shape.
// This is a shared secret in cleartext, not a signature — it stops casual
// forgery by someone who has the URL, not an attacker who can read the alert
// configuration or intercept the request. Use HTTPS and a long random string.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.8 CHANGELOG — THE ENGINE FINALLY LOOKS AT REWARD
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. NO REWARD CHECK EXISTED — AT ALL. Every version since v3.3 bounded RISK
//    (min/max ATR window, cap, clamp, buffers) and never once asked what a
//    trade could MAKE. TP1 is just the nearest structure level above entry, so
//    it can sit a small fraction of the stop distance away. That interacts
//    badly with the v3.5.5 breakeven rule: TP1 fires almost immediately, a
//    third comes off for a few ticks, the stop jumps to entry, and the rest is
//    brushed out on noise. The result is a stream of scratches and small wins
//    punctuated by full-size losses — which reads on a chart exactly as
//    "lacks accuracy", because it is negative expectancy dressed as a decent
//    hit rate. New "Min TP1 Reward (× risk)", default 1.0, rejects setups
//    whose first target is closer than the stop; 0 restores old behavior.
//    Reports as "rr" in Last Block. This WILL reduce signal count.
//    Consequence: TP selection now runs on the pre-signal, since the gate has
//    to know where TP1 is before the signal can be confirmed.
//
// 2. TRADES COULD STAY OPEN FOREVER. Tracking only ever released on a stop, on
//    TP3, or when a new signal replaced it. A trade that stalled between the
//    two simply stayed active: the runner never resolved, the TP1/SL
//    bookkeeping that feeds the cooldown and loss-streak guards froze, and in
//    the strategy build the position sat on the books distorting the numbers.
//    New "Max Bars In Trade" (0 = off) closes it out as a neutral exit —
//    neither a win nor a loss for the streak guard.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.7 CHANGELOG — THE STOP NOW MATCHES WHAT TRIGGERED THE TRADE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: Scalp trades lack accuracy. Scalp leans on the sweep trigger more
// than any other mode (it is the only mode besides Aggressive that enables
// sweeps, and v3.5.1 exempted sweep scalps from the CVD and momentum gates),
// so a defect in sweep handling shows up there first and worst.
//
// 1. THE SWEEP STOP WAS INSIDE THE SWEEP. The stop was ALWAYS derived from the
//    last pivot, regardless of which trigger fired. Work the arithmetic for a
//    bullish sweep:
//        entry requires   low ≤ pivotLow − 0.4 ATR   (i_sweepWick)
//        stop was placed at pivotLow − 0.3 ATR       (slBuf, Scalp)
//    so the stop sat ABOVE the low of the very bar that defined the setup.
//    Price retesting the sweep low — the single most ordinary thing it can do
//    after a sweep — stopped the trade out before the idea had failed. Every
//    sweep scalp carried a stop pre-placed inside its own noise.
//    Each trigger now stops beyond ITS OWN invalidation:
//      • Sweep → beyond the sweep bar's extreme (the wick is the invalidation)
//      • FVG   → beyond the far edge of the gap
//      • MSS   → the last pivot, unchanged (correct for a continuation trade)
//    This required hoisting the trigger flags above the stop block, which is
//    also why the stop can finally know what it is protecting.
//
// 2. ANTI-CHASE NOW COVERS SWEEP AND FVG. v3.5.6 capped extension for MSS only,
//    reasoning that sweep/FVG entries occur AT a level. That holds on the
//    trigger bar, but the freshness window lets them fire up to i_triggerAge
//    bars later — by then price has left the level and the reclaim edge with
//    it. Each trigger is now measured against its own reference level.
//
// 3. SCALP NO LONGER CLAMPS THE FAR STOP. v3.5.6 removed far-side clamping for
//    every mode except Scalp, where it was kept on the argument that a tight
//    volatility stop IS the scalp design. With sweep stops now anchored to the
//    wick, Scalp stops are naturally tight and the clamp rarely binds — and
//    where it would have, it was doing the same harmful thing it did in
//    Strict/Balanced: pulling the stop inside the structure. If a setup's
//    invalidation sits beyond the scalp risk cap, it is not a scalp.
//
// UNMEASURED: like every version here, this is reasoning about the code, not a
// backtest. Fix 1 is arithmetically provable; 2 and 3 are judgment.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.6 CHANGELOG — STOPS BACK INSIDE STRUCTURE + ANTI-CHASE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reported: Strict and Balanced entries were reaching their stops. Both fixes
// below target the two ways this engine manufactures a losing stop.
//
// 1. THE FAR-SIDE CLAMP WAS UNSOUND — REGRESSION FROM v3.5.4. That version
//    clamped a structural stop into BOTH ends of the risk window rather than
//    skipping the setup. The near end is harmless (the stop moves FURTHER
//    from price, still beyond the invalidation level). The far end is not:
//    it drags the stop INSIDE the structure that defines the trade, so price
//    can take it out while the setup remains valid. That converted setups
//    the max-risk cap was correctly refusing into trades with stops that
//    were pre-positioned to be hit — and Strict/Balanced, being the
//    structure-led modes, are where the damage lands.
//    Clamping is now a 3-way choice, defaulting to NEAR SIDE ONLY:
//      • Off (block instead) — pre-v3.5.4 behavior, both ends gate.
//      • Near side only (default) — widen a too-tight stop; SKIP a setup
//        whose real invalidation sits beyond the max-risk cap.
//      • Both sides — the v3.5.4/v3.5.5 behavior, kept for anyone who wants
//        a pure volatility stop.
//    Scalp still clamps the far side under the default, because a tight
//    volatility stop IS the scalp design (unchanged since v3.5.1); the other
//    three modes now refuse the trade instead of faking the stop.
//
// 2. NO UPPER BOUND ON ENTRY EXTENSION (pre-existing, all versions). MSS
//    required the close to clear the pivot by at least i_mssFollow, but
//    NOTHING capped how far past it the entry could be — and the freshness
//    window lets the entry land up to i_triggerAge bars later, by which time
//    price may have run much further. Combined with an order-flow gate that
//    by design demands a close near the bar's high, entries could be taken
//    1-3 ATR above the level they broke, with the stop still under the swing
//    low: a textbook chase, and a textbook stop-out. New "Max Entry
//    Extension Past Trigger" (default 1.0 ATR) rejects those. It applies
//    ONLY when the breakout is the sole trigger — sweep and FVG-retest
//    entries happen AT a level, so they are untouched — and reports as
//    "chase" in the Last Block row.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.5 CHANGELOG — PRICE PRECISION + TRADE MANAGEMENT
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. PRICE PRECISION BUG — CRITICAL ON FX AND LOW-PRICED SYMBOLS. Every price
//    in the chart labels and the JSON alert payload was hard-rounded to TWO
//    decimals (math.round(x, 2)). On EURUSD an SL of 1.09345 was published as
//    1.09 — a 34-pip error in a webhook order; on a sub-dollar crypto TP1/2/3
//    could collapse to the same number. Gold and indices happened to be fine,
//    which is why this survived. All prices now format at the symbol's own
//    tick size (format.mintick).
// 2. BREAKEVEN STOP AFTER TP1 (new input, ON). The engine already treated TP1
//    as "de-risked" for cooldown purposes but never moved the stop, so a
//    trade could tag TP1 and still run all the way back to a full loss. The
//    tracked SL now moves to entry when TP1 trades, and the drawn SL line and
//    label move with it.
// 3. CONSECUTIVE-LOSS CIRCUIT BREAKER (new input, default 0 = off). Pauses
//    new signals after N stop-outs in a row; the streak resets on any TP1.
//    A cooldown measured in bars does not help when the regime itself has
//    turned against the setup type.
// 4. CONFLUENCE SCORE WAS COMPUTED AND THROWN AWAY. buyAligned/sellAligned
//    have existed for versions without ever being read. They also counted
//    disabled filters as alignment (htfOkBull is true when the HTF filter is
//    off), which would have inflated the score. Now computed from the raw
//    directional facts (HTF side, VWAP side, value-area side), shown on the
//    dashboard and sent in the alert payload as "confluence": 0-3.
// 5. ALERT DEDUPE METADATA. Payloads now carry "seq" (monotonic signal
//    counter) and "bar_time" (bar open, epoch ms) so a webhook receiver can
//    reject duplicates and out-of-order deliveries.
// 6. FVG BOXES IGNORED THEIR OWN RIGHT EDGE. box.new got both an explicit
//    right edge and extend=extend.right; the extend wins, so boxes ran to
//    the edge of the chart forever, contradicting v3.4's bounded rendering.
//    They now end at the age limit that invalidates them (i_fvgMaxAge).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.4 CHANGELOG — ACCURACY AUDIT (six measurement bugs)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. WICK FILTER VETOED THE SWEEP TRIGGER'S OWN SIGNATURE. notWeak blocked any
//    bar whose COMBINED wicks exceed body × 2. A liquidity sweep is BY
//    DEFINITION a long-rejection-wick bar (wick ≥ 0.4 ATR beyond the level,
//    body ≥ 0.3-0.5 ATR ⇒ ratio typically 2-4), so the filter rejected the
//    very bars the sweep and FVG-midpoint triggers select — undoing much of
//    the v3.5.1 sweep fix. Rejection-based triggers are now judged on the
//    OPPOSING wick only: for a BUY the upper wick is supply (weakness), the
//    lower wick is the rejection you are trading. MSS (continuation) keeps
//    the original combined test.
// 2. MIN-RISK FLOOR WAS UNREACHABLE. The SL floor (close ± 0.5 ATR) sat BELOW
//    the min-risk requirement (1.0 ATR), so any setup with a near pivot
//    produced a stop the engine then rejected — a dead zone between 0.5 and
//    1.0 ATR that silently discarded valid setups. Stops are now clamped into
//    BOTH ends of the risk window (new "Clamp SL Into Risk Window" input, ON
//    by default; turn it off to restore blocking). Generalizes the Scalp-only
//    max-side clamp from v3.5.1 to both bounds and all modes.
// 3. DELTA GATE WAS A DUPLICATE OF THE OF GATE. v3.5.2 defined deltaRatio as
//    2·closePos−1, which makes "delta ≥ 0.20" and "close position ≥ 60%"
//    THE SAME TEST — the order-flow stack was one measurement counted twice
//    (the exact redundancy v3.3 set out to remove). Delta is now body
//    conviction, (close−open)/range: a bar can close at its high with a
//    doji body and now correctly fails delta while passing close position.
//    Two genuinely independent bar-shape measures again.
// 4. VWAP WAS NEAR-MEANINGLESS ON 4H. ta.vwap anchors to the session, so a 4H
//    chart averages ~6 bars — noise that the distance guard then gates on.
//    New anchor input (Auto/Session/Week/Month); Auto uses the session below
//    1H and the week at 1H and above.
// 5. SAME-BAR SL + TP1 WAS RESOLVED OPTIMISTICALLY. When one bar traded
//    through both levels the code credited TP1 and cleared the SL-hit flag,
//    disabling the post-SL cooldown after what may have been a loss. Now
//    resolved conservatively (SL first) — the standard assumption when
//    intrabar order is unknown.
// 6. REL VOLUME COMPARED A BAR TO AN AVERAGE CONTAINING ITSELF. The 20-bar
//    SMA included the current bar, damping exactly the spikes the floor is
//    meant to detect. Now averages the PRIOR 20 bars.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.3 CHANGELOG — NEWS BLACKOUT WINDOWS + TP DE-DUPLICATION
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. NEWS BLACKOUT WINDOWS (built for oil, useful on any scheduled-news
//    symbol). Optional filter blocks NEW signals during up to two weekly
//    windows (session syntax, own timezone), with a background tint while
//    active and "news" reported in the Last Block row. Defaults are
//    preloaded for WTI crude: EIA inventories (Wed 10:15-11:30 ET) and API
//    (Tue 16:15-17:45 ET). Release spike bars score as perfect order-flow
//    confluence (huge volume, strong delta, big body) right before they
//    mean-revert — exactly the bars to skip. OFF by default. Most effective
//    on intraday charts, where bars actually open inside the window.
// 2. TP DE-DUPLICATION. A double top/bottom put two pivot candidates a few
//    points apart and the TP selector assigned BOTH as TP1 and TP2 (seen
//    live on XAUUSD: TP1 ~4118 / TP2 4120.5), collapsing the plan into one
//    target zone. Structure candidates closer than max(0.5 ATR, 0.25R) to
//    the previously kept target are now merged, so TP2/TP3 promote to the
//    next real level or the R-multiple fallback.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.2 CHANGELOG — ALL MODES FIRE AGAIN + BLOCK-REASON DEBUGGING
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v3.5.1 fixed Scalp; this pass fixes the gates that starved EVERY mode.
// 1. NO-VOLUME BLACKOUT. On symbols with no volume feed (many indices, FX and
//    CFD tickers) volOk, deltaRatio and the CVD gate were all na/false — no
//    signal could EVER fire in any mode, silently. Volume-dependent gates
//    (rel-vol floor, CVD) now self-disable on such symbols (the dashboard
//    says so) while bar-shape gates stay active. Also: relVol divided by
//    max(avg, 1.0), which distorted the ratio on low-nominal-volume symbols;
//    the floor is now epsilon-only.
// 2. RISK WINDOW IN WRONG UNITS (all modes). Same class of bug fixed for
//    Scalp in v3.5.1: the SL is built from atrBase = max(atr14, atr50) but
//    the min/max risk window was judged in atr14. In chop (atr50 > atr14)
//    the structural stop routinely overflowed the cap and silently blocked
//    the trade. The window is now measured in the same ATR the SL is built
//    from. Dashboard and JSON "risk_atr" use the same reference.
// 3. DELTA FLOOR SILENTLY OVERRODE THE OF THRESHOLD. With bar-shape
//    estimation deltaRatio is mathematically 2·closePos−1 — the same scale
//    as the OF %. The 0.30 default therefore demanded a 65% close position,
//    overriding the advertised 60% threshold. New default 0.20 (⇔ 60%) so
//    the two gates agree; raise it back if you want the stricter behavior.
// 4. RSI CEILING CLIPPED BREAKOUT BARS. MSS fires on strong breakout closes,
//    exactly where RSI(14) runs above the old 72 ceiling — the band vetoed
//    the very bars the trigger selects. Defaults widened: BUY max 72→78,
//    SELL min 28→22. Retune to taste; the inputs are unchanged in range.
// 5. AGGRESSIVE WAS STILL HTF-GATED. Its mode gate was open, but the HTF
//    check baked inside the FVG/Sweep trigger construction still applied —
//    contradicting "structure + OF only". Aggressive now bypasses it there
//    too (Balanced/Strict unchanged).
// 6. "LAST BLOCK" DEBUGGING. New dashboard row names the exact gates that
//    vetoed the most recent trigger (e.g. "BUY ✕ of% rsi"), plus an optional
//    on-chart ✕ label on every blocked trigger bar — no more blind tuning.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.1 CHANGELOG — SCALP MODE ACTUALLY FIRES + PERFORMANCE PASS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHY SCALP NEVER FIRED (all fixed):
// 1. RISK-CAP MISMATCH. The SL was built from atrBase = max(atr14, atr50),
//    widened ×1.2 in chop — but the Scalp risk window (0.5-2.0) was measured
//    in plain atr14. In chop (where scalps live) the structural stop routinely
//    exceeded 2× atr14 and the signal was silently blocked. Scalp now builds
//    its SL from atr14 with a tight ≤0.3-ATR buffer, no regime widening, and
//    clamps a too-far structural stop to the max-risk cap (volatility stop)
//    instead of blocking the trade. Other modes unchanged.
// 2. COUNTER-TREND VETO. Scalp enabled sweep triggers "in both directions",
//    but rawBuy still hard-required cvdBull AND EMA 8>21. A bullish sweep at
//    a low happens AFTER a decline — CVD and momentum are still bearish at
//    that moment, so sweep scalps were vetoed essentially 100% of the time.
//    Sweep-triggered scalps are now exempt from the CVD and momentum trend
//    gates (the sweep's own rejection close, delta floor, OF threshold and
//    volume floor still gate the entry). MSS/FVG scalps keep both gates.
// 3. CHOP THRESHOLD OVERRIDE. The adaptive OF gate added +5% in chop, pushing
//    Scalp's 57% floor back up to 62% — above even the Balanced base. Scalp
//    now uses its flat threshold; other modes keep the adaptive tightening.
//
// PERFORMANCE ("slow to load" fixes — no signal-logic impact except where noted):
// 4. FRVP THROTTLE. The profile (lookback × bin-overlap loops) rebuilt on
//    EVERY historical bar. New input "FRVP Refresh Every N Bars" (default 5,
//    set 1 for the old exact behavior) rebuilds on a stride + always on the
//    last bar. POC/VAH/VAL can be up to N-1 bars stale intrabar — an accepted
//    tradeoff, disclosed here and in the tooltip.
// 5. FRVP RENDER ON LAST BAR ONLY. Lines/zone/labels were deleted and redrawn
//    on every confirmed bar across all of history; now drawn only on the last
//    bar (identical final visual).
// 6. SINGLE request.security CALL. HTF close and HTF EMA now come from one
//    tuple request instead of two separate HTF requests.
// 7. LAZY TP SELECTION. f_tpLevels (array build + sort, ×2) ran on every bar;
//    now runs only on signal bars. Identical levels on signal bars.
// 8. LAZY ALERT JSON. Both ~30-field JSON strings were concatenated on every
//    bar; now built only on the signal bar that sends them.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5 CHANGELOG — SCALP MODE (new 4th engine mode; other modes unchanged)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// "Scalp" trades structure + order flow in BOTH directions with the lag
// stripped out. Still 100% bar-close confirmed — it does NOT repaint.
// Selecting Scalp clamps settings toward the fast preset (a user input that
// is already faster/looser than the preset is kept as-is):
//
//   • Pivots confirm with 2 bars per side (vs 3) — structure seen 1 bar sooner
//   • Momentum: current-bar EMA 8>21 only — no 2-bar persistence, no slope
//   • CVD gate uses faster EMAs 3/10 (vs 5/20)
//   • HTF 4H gate fully bypassed (in the mode gate AND inside the FVG/Sweep
//     trigger construction, where it was silently baked in for all modes)
//   • Structural HTF-locked bias bypassed — scalps take both directions
//   • Mode gate reduced to the VWAP overextension guard only (never chase)
//   • Sweep AND FVG triggers both enabled
//   • OF floors softened: threshold ≤57%, delta ≤0.15, rel-vol ≤1.0×,
//     body ≤0.3 ATR
//   • RSI band widened: BUY 35-80, SELL 20-65
//   • Cooldown ≤5 bars (post-SL doubling still applies)
//   • Risk window tightened to 0.5-2.0 ATR (scalp stops are close)
//   • TP fallbacks tightened to 1R / 1.5R / 2R (structure targets still first)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.4.1 CHANGELOG — zero-error pass (compile + runtime + trade-logic fixes)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FRVP early-bar crash guard; SL/TP tracking independent of display toggle;
// unified TP levels for chart + labels + JSON alerts; trigger names respect
// mode gating; sweep ta.highest/lowest hoisted out of ternaries; RSI SELL
// dashboard cell dim when OUT; age counters capped at 999; dashboard redraw
// only on last bar; nz()-guarded profile volume.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.4 CHANGELOG — FRVP / VAH / VAL accuracy + rendering rebuild
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Proportional volume distribution across overlapped bins; classic two-bin
// Market Profile VA expansion; default 32 bins (max 64); bounded rendering;
// optional POC/VAH/VAL price labels; numeric poc/vah/val in JSON alerts;
// dashboard FRVP row shows live POC.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.3 CHANGELOG — accuracy fixes (unchanged in v3.4.x)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Max-risk cap; VWAP overextension guard; RSI sanity band; trigger freshness
// window (MSS/Sweep valid for N bars); FVG expiration; slow-EMA slope
// confirmation; real net-delta-ratio OF gate; doji-neutral CVD; HTF-checked
// structural bias; TP1 clears SL-cooldown flag; rejection-wick FVG retests.
// Tuning: cooldown 15→12, OF base threshold 62→60.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// INPUT GROUPS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

var string G_ENGINE = "══ Engine Mode ══"
var string G_STRUCT = "══ Market Structure ══"
var string G_FVG    = "══ Fair Value Gaps ══"
var string G_SWEEP  = "══ Liquidity Sweeps ══"
var string G_OF     = "══ Order Flow ══"
var string G_MOM    = "══ Momentum Stack ══"
var string G_HTF    = "══ HTF Trend Filter ══"
var string G_VWAP   = "══ VWAP Filter ══"
var string G_FRVP   = "══ Fixed-Range Volume Profile ══"
var string G_RSI    = "══ RSI Sanity Filter ══"
var string G_TPSL   = "══ TP / SL Projection ══"
var string G_RISK   = "══ Risk Guards ══"
var string G_NEWS   = "══ Time Filters + News ══"
var string G_VIS    = "══ Visuals ══"
var string G_ALERT  = "══ Alerts ══"

i_tfCalib    = input.bool(true, "Calibrate Scalp To The Chart Timeframe",
     tooltip="v3.5.38: Scalp used one set of clamps on every timeframe, so its signal rate simply followed however many bars the chart happened to have. Measured over 90 days x 4 seeds on synthetic gold-like data with the sim/ harness:\n  5m 4.64 signals/day   15m 1.52   30m 0.68   1H 0.36   4H 0.09\nThe same preset therefore means a working scalper on 5m and roughly one trade every two days on 30m.\nWith this on, timeframes at or above 15m — the ones that are starved — get the measured calibration: trigger freshness 6, sweep wick 0.30 ATR, minimum FVG 0.25 ATR, cooldown 3, volume floor 0.7. 5m is left alone because it already exceeds the target.\n  5m 4.64 (unchanged)   15m 2.99   30m 1.24   1H 0.59   4H 0.18\nNothing here touches the reward gate, the confluence floor or the order-flow threshold. An earlier search suggested 3/day on 15m required disabling the reward gate entirely; adding trigger SUPPLY instead reaches the same rate with the gate intact.\nMeasured expectancy stayed statistically indistinguishable from zero at every timeframe both before and after (worst t-stat -1.53) — which is what a fair game should give and is NOT evidence of edge in either direction. Turn this off for identical behaviour to v3.5.37.",
     group=G_ENGINE)
i_scalpFreq  = input.string("Selective", "Scalp Signal Frequency",
     options=["Selective","Standard","High"],
     tooltip="v3.5.14: Scalp only. Trades signal COUNT against signal QUALITY, explicitly, so the cost of each step is visible instead of buried.\n\nSELECTIVE (default) — cooldown 5 bars, TP1 must reach the full Min TP1 Reward, confluence floor 1. Identical to v3.5.13.\nSTANDARD — cooldown 4, TP1 reward capped at 0.9R, confluence floor 1.\nHIGH — cooldown 2, TP1 reward capped at 0.7R, confluence floor removed.\n\nHIGH raises the rate mainly by letting good setups sit closer together (cooldown), which costs least, but it also accepts nearer first targets and drops the trend-context floor, which costs real quality. A closer TP1 also fires the breakeven move sooner, so more trades end as scratches.\nSignals per day depend on the market, not the setting — measure the actual rate in the strategy tester.",
     group=G_ENGINE)
i_entryMode  = input.string("Market (confirmed)", "Entry Mode",
     options=["Market (confirmed)","Level retest (pending)"],
     tooltip="v3.5.32: WHERE the entry is taken once a setup qualifies.\n\nMARKET (CONFIRMED) — the original behaviour and the default. The signal fires at the close of the qualifying bar. Every trigger requires its event to be COMPLETE (the break already happened, the sweep already closed back, the retest already rejected), plus bar-close confirmation, plus up to i_triggerAge bars of freshness — so on 15m the entry can land more than an hour after the move that defines it, at a worse price with a wider stop. That lag is the price of not repainting.\n\nLEVEL RETEST (PENDING) — the qualifying bar ARMS an order at the trigger's own level instead of buying the close: the broken pivot for MSS, the gap edge for an FVG retest, the swept level for a sweep. The entry fires only if price returns to that level within the window below. Entry price is the level, so the stop sits closer to it, R shrinks, the reward gate clears more easily and 'chase' can never fire.\n\nThe trade-off is honest: a pending order that never fills is a move missed entirely, and one that fills right before the level breaks is a worse loss than the market entry would have been. UNMEASURED — run one range in each mode and compare profit factor.",
     group=G_ENGINE)
i_pendBars   = input.int(8, "Pending Entry Window (bars)", minval=1, maxval=50,
     tooltip="v3.5.32: how long an armed level-retest order stays live before it is cancelled. Longer gives price more chance to come back, at the cost of entering on a setup whose context has moved on.\nOnly used when Entry Mode is 'Level retest (pending)'.",
     group=G_ENGINE)
i_mode       = input.string("Balanced", "Engine Mode",
     options=["Scalp","Aggressive","Balanced","Strict"],
     tooltip="v4.1: ALL FOUR TRIGGERS (MSS, FVG, sweep, band rejection) are live in EVERY mode. What the mode sets is how many independent readings of the entry bar must agree — relative volume ≥1.20, body ≥0.55 ATR, close in the top/bottom 30% of its range, two triggers stacking on the same bar, and price at a deviation band.\nScalp: 0 of 5 required, plus the overextension guard. Fastest preset — 2-bar pivots, 1-bar momentum, fast CVD, no HTF gate, cooldown 5, 0.5-2 ATR risk window, atr14 clamped stops.\nAggressive: 0 of 5, no HTF/VWAP gate at all.\nBalanced: 1 of 5, plus HTF trend and VWAP alignment.\nStrict: 2 of 5, plus HTF trend and VWAP alignment.\nBefore v4.1 the mode decided which TRIGGERS EXISTED — Strict saw only MSS, and carried a location rule and an FRVP requirement on top. It produced 0.38 signals/day on 15m and 0.07 on 1H: a mode that did not trade. Those three vetoes are gone; the same measurement now reads 1.45/day on 15m and 0.63 on 1H, with win rate still rising as the mode tightens.\nThe old Min Confluence Required input still applies on top of this and is a separate, manual floor.",
     group=G_ENGINE)

// ── Market Structure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_swingLen   = input.int(3,   "Pivot Confirmation (bars each side)",
     minval=2, maxval=20,
     tooltip="Bars required on EACH side of a pivot to confirm it as a real swing.\n3 = 7-bar window (balance of speed vs. reliability).\nRaise to 5+ on lower timeframes to filter noise.",
     group=G_STRUCT)
i_mssFollow  = input.float(0.1,"MSS Follow-Through (× ATR)",
     minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05,
     tooltip="Close must break the pivot level by this many ATRs. Prevents 1-tick stop-run 'breaks' that reverse next bar.",
     group=G_STRUCT)
i_slAtr      = input.float(0.5,"SL Buffer Below/Above Pivot (× ATR)",
     minval=0.1, maxval=3.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="SL is placed at last pivot ± this ATR buffer.\nAuto-scales UP in chop (×1.2) and expansion (×1.3) — never tightens below 1.0×.\nScalp mode: buffer clamped to ≤0.3 and based on atr14 with no regime scaling (v3.5.1).",
     group=G_STRUCT)
i_scalpSlCap = input.float(0.3, "Scalp SL Buffer Cap (× ATR)", minval=0.1, maxval=1.5, step=0.05,
     tooltip="v3.5.26: the most stop padding Scalp may take beyond its structural anchor, before the volatility multiplier.\nScalp previously hard-clamped this to 0.3 and was ALSO the only mode denied slMult — so where every other mode widened its stop by 1.3x in expanding volatility and 1.2x in chop, Scalp held a flat 0.3 ATR through both. The pad was thinnest in exactly the conditions most likely to overrun it. Scalp now takes the multiplier like every other mode.\nRaise this for a more comfortable stop. It widens risk, which lowers R-multiples and makes the reward gate harder to clear — expect fewer signals as you raise it.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_triggerAge = input.int(3, "Trigger Max Age (bars)",
     minval=1, maxval=10,
     tooltip="MSS and Sweep triggers stay 'fresh' for this many bars after firing. Lets OF/CVD confirm on N+1/N+2 rather than only same-bar. v3.3 accuracy fix.\nPair this with the extension cap below — freshness alone let entries land several bars and several ATR past the level that was broken.",
     group=G_STRUCT)
i_maxChase   = input.float(1.0, "Max Entry Extension Past Trigger (× ATR)",
     minval=0.1, maxval=5.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="v3.5.6: rejects a breakout entry taken more than this far ABOVE the broken pivot (below it, for shorts). i_mssFollow sets the MINIMUM break distance; nothing set a maximum, so with the freshness window an entry could land 1-3 ATR past the level with the stop still under the swing low — a chase, and the most reliable way to be stopped out.\nApplies only when the breakout is the sole trigger; sweep and FVG-retest entries occur AT a level and are unaffected.\nRaise it to allow more momentum continuation; lower it to demand entries close to structure.",
     group=G_STRUCT)

// ── Fair Value Gaps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_fvgMinAtr  = input.float(0.35,"Min FVG Size (× ATR)", minval=0.1, maxval=5.0, step=0.05, group=G_FVG)
i_fvgMaxAge  = input.int(30, "FVG Max Age (bars)",
     minval=5, maxval=500,
     tooltip="FVG is invalidated if not retested within this many bars. Stops stale gaps from firing. v3.3 accuracy fix.",
     group=G_FVG)
i_fvgGapGuard = input.bool(true, "Reject Gaps Across Market Closures",
     tooltip="v3.5.33: an FVG is a THREE-BAR pattern, and nothing checked that those three bars were contiguous in time. A market closure produces the same shape — Friday's last bar, then Sunday's open — so every weekend registered as a fair value gap on every intraday timeframe, and it cleared the minimum-size filter trivially because the closure gap is far wider than any real imbalance.\nMeasured span versus a normal 3-bar span on XAUUSD: 5m 288x, 15m 96x, 1H 24x, 4H 6x. A daily chart is unaffected (ratio 1x) since its bars already skip the weekend.\nA closure gap is an absence of trading, not an imbalance left by aggressive orders, and price returning to it means nothing. Worse, it becomes a stop anchor (v3.5.7 puts the stop beyond the gap's far edge) and a chase reference, so one false gap distorts an entire plan.\nThis rejects any three-bar window whose elapsed time exceeds the tolerance below. It does not touch gap sizing or retest logic.",
     group=G_FVG)
i_fvgGapTol   = input.float(3.0, "Closure Tolerance (× normal span)", minval=1.5, maxval=20.0, step=0.5,
     tooltip="v3.5.33: how much longer than a normal three-bar span the window may be before it is treated as a market closure. A normal span is exactly 2 x the timeframe, so 3.0 allows up to 6 x before rejecting.\nGenerous on purpose: intraday closure gaps run 6x to 288x, so even a loose tolerance separates them cleanly, while a daily chart's Thursday-Friday-Monday window (4 days against a 6-day threshold) is never touched.\nLower it to also reject daily-session breaks on symbols that have them.",
     group=G_FVG)
i_fvgMax     = input.int(5, "Max Gaps Tracked Per Direction", minval=1, maxval=20,
     tooltip="v3.5.10: how many unfilled FVGs to keep per side. Before this, exactly ONE gap per direction was retained — a new gap overwrote the previous one, so an older gap price had not yet returned to was silently discarded. In a fast leg that stacks several gaps only the newest survived, and the newest is usually the FURTHEST from price, i.e. the least likely to be retested.\nEach tracked gap expires and invalidates independently. Higher values retain more history at a small drawing cost.",
     group=G_FVG)
i_fvgMidTrig = input.bool(true, "Trigger on 50% Midpoint Touch (with rejection)",
     tooltip="Fires retest when price wicks into FVG midpoint AND closes with rejection (upper 60% of bar for bull, lower 40% for bear). Earlier than full-fill retest, without accepting weak touches.",
     group=G_FVG)
i_fvgShow    = input.bool(true, "Show FVG Boxes", group=G_FVG)
i_fvgBullCol = input.color(color.new(#00E5FF, 80), "Bull FVG Color", group=G_FVG)
i_fvgBearCol = input.color(color.new(#FF3366, 80), "Bear FVG Color", group=G_FVG)
i_fvgLabel   = input.bool(true, "Show FVG Labels", group=G_FVG)

// ── Sweep ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_sweepUsePivot = input.bool(true, "Sweep Uses Pivot Levels (not range high/low)",
     tooltip="ON = sweeps must violate an actual confirmed swing high/low (real liquidity). OFF = legacy N-bar high/low sweep.",
     group=G_SWEEP)
i_sweepPivotLen = input.int(5, "Sweep Pivot Length (bars each side)", minval=2, maxval=20,
     tooltip="v3.5.13: sweeps now target their OWN pivots at this depth, independent of the entry pivot length and NOT overridden by any mode.\nScalp clamps the entry pivot to 2 bars per side for responsiveness — fine for spotting a break, but a 2-bar swing is not a liquidity pool, and a sweep of one is noise wearing the name of a setup. Scalp leans on sweeps more than any other mode, so it was aiming its main trigger at the shallowest structure on the chart.\n5 = an 11-bar swing, a level with orders resting under it. Raise for fewer, more significant sweeps.",
     group=G_SWEEP)
i_sweepLook  = input.int(10, "Sweep Lookback (bars, legacy mode)", minval=3, maxval=100, group=G_SWEEP)
i_sweepWick  = input.float(0.4, "Min Sweep Wick (× ATR)", minval=0.1, maxval=2.0, step=0.05,
     tooltip="Wick must extend at least this far beyond the liquidity level. v3.2 default raised to 0.4 to filter near-misses.",
     group=G_SWEEP)

// ── Order Flow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_ofThresh   = input.float(60.0,"OF Pressure Threshold (%)",
     minval=55.0, maxval=90.0, step=0.5,
     tooltip="Base close-position (%) required. v3.3 relaxed 62→60 because a real delta-ratio gate now sits alongside this — buyPct becomes the coarse floor.",
     group=G_OF)
i_deltaFloor = input.float(0.20,"Min Body Conviction (Δ)",
     minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05,
     tooltip="(close − open) / bar range must exceed this. v3.5.4: this is now BODY CONVICTION, a measure independent of the OF threshold. Previously it was 2×closePos−1, i.e. mathematically the same test as the OF close-position threshold — the order-flow stack was one measurement counted twice.\nTogether the two gates now require: closed near the extreme (OF %) AND real directional body (Δ). A doji closing at its high passes the first and correctly fails the second.\nWithout tick data neither is true exchange delta; both are bar-shape estimates.",
     group=G_OF)
i_ofAdaptive = input.bool(true, "Adaptive OF Threshold (tighten in chop)",
     tooltip="Raises threshold by +5% in chop regime. Never LOWERS the threshold.\nScalp mode ignores the chop tightening — it would override the scalp 57% floor (v3.5.1).",
     group=G_OF)
i_volFloor   = input.float(1.2,"Min Relative Volume (× avg)",
     minval=0.5, maxval=5.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="Signal blocked if volume < this × 20-bar average.",
     group=G_OF)
i_volSanity  = input.bool(true, "Bypass Volume Floor On Broken Feeds",
     tooltip="v3.5.25: if almost no recent bar could clear the Min Relative Volume floor, stop applying it.\nrelVol is volume divided by average volume. On a feed that reports near-zero size on most bars with occasional huge spikes, the spikes drag the average up and every ordinary bar reads far below 1.0 — so Scalp's 1.0 floor becomes permanently unpassable and the mode fires nothing at all. That is not filtering, it is a permanent veto caused by bad data.\nMeasured on a broker BTCUSD feed: Rel Volume 0.11x, 0.19x, 0.23x, 0.33x, then 2.88x, with zero Scalp trades for days while gold traded normally on the same build.\nThis bypasses ONLY the magnitude floor, and the dashboard shows a VOL? warning while it is active. No volume is invented and no other gate is loosened — CVD and FRVP still read the same broken series. It keeps the mode usable; it does not make the data trustworthy. Prefer a real exchange feed where you can.",
     group=G_OF)
i_volSanityLen = input.int(500, "Volume Sanity Window (bars)", minval=50, maxval=2000,
     tooltip="v3.5.25: how many recent bars the pass-rate is measured over. Longer is more stable and slower to react to a feed that starts or stops working.",
     group=G_OF)
i_volSanityMin = input.float(0.15, "Volume Sanity Min Pass Rate", minval=0.0, maxval=0.5, step=0.01,
     tooltip="v3.5.25: the share of recent bars that must be ABLE to clear the volume floor for the floor to keep applying. 0.15 = 15%.\nMeasured over simulated series, floor 1.0, 20-bar average, 500-bar window:\n  healthy (lognormal)      median relVol 0.90, pass-rate 0.41\n  spiky 1-in-20 at 100x    median relVol 0.17, pass-rate 0.05\n  spiky 1-in-10 at 30x     median relVol 0.26, pass-rate 0.10\n  all zero                 pass-rate 0.00\nA first cut defaulted to 0.05 and would have caught nothing — the observed broker feed sits exactly there. 0.15 separates the two families with margin at both ends.\nRaise it to bypass the floor more readily; set 0 to disable the detection entirely and always enforce the floor.",
     group=G_OF)
i_bodyAtr    = input.float(0.5,"Min Signal Bar Body (× ATR)",
     minval=0.2, maxval=3.0, step=0.05,
     tooltip="Signal bar's body must be at least this fraction of ATR.",
     group=G_OF)
i_wickMax    = input.float(2.0,"Max Wick/Body Ratio",
     minval=0.5, maxval=10.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="Blocks bars where wicks exceed body × this ratio. Filters doji/indecision candles.\nv3.5.4: MSS (continuation) entries are judged on COMBINED wicks as before. Sweep and FVG-retest entries are judged on the OPPOSING wick only — a sweep bar's long rejection wick is the setup, not weakness, and the combined test was vetoing the trigger's own signature.",
     group=G_OF)
i_cvdReset   = input.bool(false, "Reset CVD Each Session (daily anchor)",
     tooltip="Anchors CVD to session open. Default OFF because reset creates 20-bar noisy EMA readings.",
     group=G_OF)

// ── Momentum Stack ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_momFilter  = input.bool(true, "Enable EMA Momentum Filter (8/21)",
     tooltip="Blocks BUY when fast EMA < slow EMA (and reverse for SELL). Requires 2-bar persistence AND slow-EMA slope in trigger direction.",
     group=G_MOM)
i_momFast    = input.int(8,  "Fast EMA", minval=2, maxval=50, group=G_MOM)
i_momSlow    = input.int(21, "Slow EMA", minval=5, maxval=200, group=G_MOM)
i_emaSlope   = input.bool(true, "Require Slow-EMA Slope Confirmation",
     tooltip="Slow EMA must slope in trigger direction (>3 bars). Kills entries when both EMAs are flat and fast just brushes slow. v3.3 accuracy fix.",
     group=G_MOM)

// ── HTF Trend ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_htfAuto    = input.bool(true, "Auto-Select HTF From Chart Timeframe",
     tooltip="v3.5.34: picks a higher timeframe that is genuinely ABOVE the chart, instead of a fixed 240 that is correct on some charts and meaningless on others.\nWith the fixed default: on 4H the request returns the chart's OWN series, so the trend filter collapses to close[1] > EMA50[1] on the very data being traded. On Daily it requests a LOWER timeframe, which with lookahead_on can evaluate a historical bar using information from its own future.\nMapping (chosen so nothing below 30m changes):\n  up to 15m -> 240 (identical to the old fixed default)\n  30m to 4H -> D\n  1D        -> W\n  above 1D  -> M\nv3.5.28's guard disabled the filter when it was not higher; this supplies a correct one instead. Turn this off to use the manual timeframe below.",
     group=G_HTF)
i_htfTf      = input.timeframe("240","HTF Timeframe", group=G_HTF)
i_htfEmaLen  = input.int(50, "HTF EMA Length", minval=5, maxval=500, group=G_HTF)
i_htfFilter  = input.bool(true, "Enforce HTF Direction (strict)", group=G_HTF)
i_htfGuard   = input.bool(true, "Disable HTF Filter When It Is Not Higher",
     tooltip="v3.5.28: switches the HTF filter OFF when the selected HTF timeframe is not actually above the chart timeframe.\nThe default HTF is 240. On a 4H chart that requests the SAME series, so request.security returns the chart's own bars and htfBull collapses to close[1] > EMA50[1] — a lagged moving-average filter wearing the name of a trend filter. On a Daily chart it is worse: the request is for a LOWER timeframe.\nThis matters most in Strict, which requires HTF alignment, while Scalp bypasses the HTF gate entirely — so on a 4H chart Strict is gated by a filter that is not doing what it claims and Scalp is not gated at all. That alone can make one mode lose while the other wins on the same data.\nWith this on, the filter self-disables rather than applying a mislabelled test, and the dashboard shows a warning. The real fix is to set an HTF genuinely above your chart: Daily for a 4H chart, Weekly for a Daily chart.\nTurn this off to keep the old behaviour.",
     group=G_HTF)

// ── VWAP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_vwapFilter = input.bool(true, "Enable VWAP Filter", group=G_VWAP)
i_vwapAnchor = input.string("Auto", "VWAP Anchor", options=["Auto","Session","Week","Month"],
     tooltip="v3.5.4: ta.vwap anchors to the SESSION by default, which on a 4H chart averages only ~6 bars — too few to be a meaningful mean, yet the distance guard still gated on it. Auto uses Session below 1H and Week at 1H and above.",
     group=G_VWAP)
i_vwapMaxDist= input.float(3.0, "Max Distance From VWAP (× ATR)",
     minval=0.5, maxval=20.0, step=0.25,
     tooltip="Signal blocked if |close - VWAP| > this × ATR. Prevents chasing extended moves. v3.3 accuracy fix.",
     group=G_VWAP)
i_bandTrig   = input.bool(true, "Band Rejection Trigger",
     tooltip="v4.0: treats a rejection at a VWAP deviation band as an entry trigger — price trades THROUGH the band and closes back inside it.\nVWAP was computed from v3.3 onward and used only as a yes/no filter; it was never an entry level. Measured on 15m, this supplies 20.9 triggers/day against 14.9 for MSS, FVG and sweep combined, and its trades are statistically indistinguishable from theirs (difference -0.014R, t = -0.42 over 3,854 trades).\nTrigger SUPPLY was what capped trade count — the engine converted only 2-4% of triggers, so more filtering was never going to help. This is where the supply was.\nA band rejection is a REVERSION setup, so it is given the mean as its first target rather than the continuation ladder the breakout triggers use. Handing it continuation targets measured -0.075R against -0.062R with reversion targets.",
     group=G_VWAP)
i_bandK1     = input.float(1.0, "Band 1 (× volume-weighted σ)", minval=0.5, maxval=4.0, step=0.25, group=G_VWAP)
i_bandK2     = input.float(2.0, "Band 2 (× volume-weighted σ)", minval=1.0, maxval=6.0, step=0.25, group=G_VWAP)
i_bandShow   = input.bool(true, "Show VWAP Bands", group=G_VWAP)
i_vwapShow   = input.bool(true, "Show VWAP Line", group=G_VWAP)
i_vwapCol    = input.color(color.new(#FFD700, 60), "VWAP Color", group=G_VWAP)

// ── FRVP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TIMEFRAME-CONSISTENCY FIX.
// The profile was anchored to a BAR COUNT, so the same chart at four different
// timeframes profiled four different spans of market history and returned four
// different POC/VAH/VAL. Measured on one resampled price path, 100 bars @ 32
// bins:
//     5m  =   8.3 h  ->  POC 4866.47   VA width  59.57
//     15m =  25.0 h  ->  POC 4580.37   VA width 284.14
//     30m =  50.0 h  ->  POC 4585.16   VA width 197.85
//     1H  = 100.0 h  ->  POC 4583.68   VA width 257.58
//     POC spread across the four charts: 286.10 price units.
// That is not a rendering difference — the levels are structurally different
// objects, and since v3.5.8 VAH/VAL feed the TP3 target and the reward gate, so
// the SIGNAL differed by timeframe too.
// Anchoring the same window to HOURS instead collapses it. Same path, same fix:
//     24 h window -> POC spread 0.00, VAH/VAL spread 33.43 (bin granularity only)
//     72 h window -> POC spread 0.00, VAH/VAL spread 0.00
// Default is now the hours anchor. "Bars (legacy)" reproduces the old behavior
// exactly for anyone who tuned against it.
i_frvpAnchor = input.string("Hours (TF-independent)", "FRVP Anchor",
     options=["Hours (TF-independent)", "Bars (legacy)"],
     tooltip="Hours: the profile always covers the same span of real time, so POC/VAH/VAL agree across 5m/15m/30m/1H on the same chart. Bars: the older behavior — a fixed bar count, which means a different span of history on every timeframe.",
     group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpHours  = input.float(24.0, "FRVP Window (hours)", minval=1.0, maxval=168.0, step=1.0,
     tooltip="Used when Anchor = Hours. 24 = one trading day. Larger windows agree more tightly across timeframes (bin granularity is the only residual) but respond more slowly.",
     group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpMaxBars = input.int(600, "FRVP Max Bars (hard cap)", minval=50, maxval=1500,
     tooltip="Safety cap on how many bars the hours window may expand to. On low timeframes the requested hours can exceed what the script can look back over; the window is then TRUNCATED to this many bars. When that happens the dashboard FRVP row shows the hours ACTUALLY profiled and marks them with ~, so the truncation is visible instead of silent.",
     group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpLen    = input.int(100, "FRVP Lookback (bars, legacy anchor)", minval=20, maxval=500, group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpBins   = input.int(32,  "FRVP Bins", minval=8, maxval=64,
     tooltip="v3.4: default 24→32, max 48→64. Finer bins = tighter, more accurate VAH/VAL. With proportional volume distribution (v3.4), higher bin counts no longer create noisy profiles.",
     group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpEvery  = input.int(5, "FRVP Refresh Every N Bars", minval=1, maxval=50,
     tooltip="v3.5.1 performance fix: rebuild the profile every N bars (plus always on the last bar) instead of every bar. The lookback×bins rebuild was the main reason the script loaded slowly. POC/VAH/VAL can be up to N-1 bars stale between refreshes. Set 1 for the exact v3.4 every-bar behavior.",
     group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpShow   = input.bool(true, "Show FRVP (POC/VAH/VAL)", group=G_FRVP)
i_vaWidth    = input.float(0.7,"Value Area Width", minval=0.5, maxval=0.95, step=0.01, group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpExtend = input.int(20, "Right Extension (bars)", minval=0, maxval=200,
     tooltip="v3.4: POC/VAH/VAL lines and the VA zone end this many bars right of the current bar instead of extending infinitely across the chart.",
     group=G_FRVP)
i_frvpLevels = input.bool(true, "Show POC/VAH/VAL Price Labels",
     tooltip="v3.4: small price tags at the right edge of the profile.",
     group=G_FRVP)
i_pocCol     = input.color(color.new(#FFD700, 0),  "POC Color", group=G_FRVP)
i_vahCol     = input.color(color.new(#00E5FF, 40), "VAH Color", group=G_FRVP)
i_valCol     = input.color(color.new(#00E5FF, 40), "VAL Color", group=G_FRVP)
i_vaFill     = input.bool(true, "Fill Value Area", group=G_FRVP)

// ── RSI Sanity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_rsiFilter  = input.bool(true, "Enable RSI Sanity Filter",
     tooltip="Blocks entries in overbought/oversold zones. v3.3 accuracy fix.",
     group=G_RSI)
i_rsiLen     = input.int(14, "RSI Length", minval=2, maxval=100, group=G_RSI)
i_rsiBuyLo   = input.int(40, "BUY min RSI", minval=10, maxval=60, group=G_RSI)
i_rsiBuyHi   = input.int(78, "BUY max RSI", minval=55, maxval=90,
     tooltip="v3.5.2 default 72→78: MSS fires on strong breakout closes, exactly where RSI runs hot — 72 vetoed the very bars the trigger selects.", group=G_RSI)
i_rsiSellLo  = input.int(22, "SELL min RSI", minval=10, maxval=45,
     tooltip="v3.5.2 default 28→22 (mirror of the BUY-side fix).", group=G_RSI)
i_rsiSellHi  = input.int(60, "SELL max RSI", minval=40, maxval=90, group=G_RSI)

// ── TP / SL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_manualOn   = input.bool(false, "Manual TP / SL Override",
     tooltip="v3.5.41: lets you move the levels of the OPEN plan yourself.\nThe engine draws its levels with line.new, and Pine drawings cannot be dragged — that is a platform limit, not a setting, which is why they read as frozen. These four price inputs are the closest thing available: set one and it replaces the engine's level on the live plan.\nEach can be placed by clicking the chart (TradingView prompts for a price when the input is first enabled) or typed exactly.\nLeave a value at 0 to keep the engine's own level for that slot, so you can move only the stop and leave the targets alone.\nOverrides apply to the DRAWN plan and to the hit tracking that feeds breakeven, the cooldown and the loss-streak guard. They do NOT feed back into signal generation — the reward gate already passed on the engine's own levels, and letting a manual target re-open that decision would let you talk the engine into a trade it refused.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_mSL        = input.price(0.0, "  Manual SL",  group=G_TPSL, confirm=true)
i_mTP1       = input.price(0.0, "  Manual TP1", group=G_TPSL, confirm=true)
i_mTP2       = input.price(0.0, "  Manual TP2", group=G_TPSL, confirm=true)
i_mTP3       = input.price(0.0, "  Manual TP3", group=G_TPSL, confirm=true)
i_tpslShow   = input.bool(true, "Show TP / SL Lines",
     tooltip="v3.4.1: display only. SL/TP tracking (post-SL cooldown, TP1 de-risk) now runs whether or not lines are shown.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_tpUnit     = input.string("ATR (v4.2)", "Target Distances Measured In",
     options=["ATR (v4.2)", "Risk / R (v4.1)"],
     tooltip="What the three target multiples below are multiples OF.\n\nRISK / R (v4.1) — every target is a multiple of the STOP DISTANCE. The stop is structural and capped at Max Risk (3.0 ATR), so TP3 at 4.0R can sit 12 ATR from entry. The plan is therefore slowest and least reachable exactly when the structure is widest, which is backwards: a wide invalidation says the setup needs room, not that the market owes you a bigger move.\n\nATR (v4.2, default) — the same numbers measured in the instrument's volatility. A wide stop no longer drags every target out with it.\n\nMEASURED, 15m, 150 days x 6 seeds, identical entries and identical stops in every row — only the ladder moves:\n\n  ladder                TP1   TP2   TP3   bars    SL\n  1.5/2.5/4.0 R (v4.1)  50%   14%    6%      6   50%\n  1.5/2.5/4.0 ATR       52%   16%    8%      6   48%\n  1.0/1.8/3.0 ATR       58%   19%    8%      5   42%\n  0.8/1.4/2.2 ATR       62%   24%   11%      4   38%\n\nRead the TP2 and TP3 columns. Under v4.1 the second third of the position fills once in seven trades and the last third once in sixteen — the 33/33/34 scale-out realises nothing like thirds. Tightening the ladder raises every fill rate, cuts the stop-out rate from 50% to 38% and takes the median hold from 6 bars to 4.\n\nThe honest cost: a nearer target is a smaller win. This buys reachability and a working de-risk, not edge.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_tp1R       = input.float(1.5, "TP1 (× ATR, or × risk in R mode)", minval=0.5, maxval=10.0, step=0.5, group=G_TPSL)
i_tp2R       = input.float(2.5, "TP2 (R-multiple fallback)", minval=0.5, maxval=20.0, step=0.5, group=G_TPSL)
i_tp3R       = input.float(4.0, "TP3 (R-multiple fallback)", minval=0.5, maxval=30.0, step=0.5, group=G_TPSL)
i_maxTpR     = input.float(0.0, "Max TP Distance (× risk, 0 = off)", minval=0.0, maxval=30.0, step=0.5, group=G_TPSL,
     tooltip="v3.5.20: a structure target (pivot / VAH / VAL) further than this many multiples of the stop distance is DISCARDED and that slot falls back to its R-multiple. 0 = OFF, which is the default and matches every previous version.\nStructure levels are otherwise accepted at ANY distance — the only test is that the level sits the right side of entry. Scalp clamps its R-multiple fallbacks to 1.0/1.5/2.0R, so a VAH can hand a 15-minute scalp a 6R runner and the mode's own ceiling is bypassed. That asymmetry is real.\nBUT DO NOT ASSUME CAPPING HELPS. On the trade that prompted this (XAUUSD 15m, entry 4343.830, risk 9.328, TP3 tagged VAH at 4399.366 = 5.95R) price ran to 4397 = 5.70R and missed the target by 0.25R. Capping at the mode's 2R would have closed the runner at 4362 and given up a move that reached 5.49R at the reported exit. On that sample the cap LOSES money.\nThis is a right-tail-versus-fill-rate trade-off and no backtest has been run to settle it. Off by default for that reason. If you enable it, measure profit factor before and after over the same range — win rate will rise and expectancy may well fall."
     )
i_tpLineLen  = input.int(30,   "TP/SL Line Length (bars)", minval=5, maxval=200, group=G_TPSL)
i_minRR      = input.float(1.0, "Min TP1 Reward (× risk)", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="v3.5.8: TP1 must sit at least this many multiples of the stop distance away, or the setup is skipped (reports as 'rr' in Last Block). 0 disables.\nThe engine has always bounded RISK and never once checked REWARD. TP1 is simply the nearest structure level above entry, so it can be a fraction of the stop distance away — and since TP1 also triggers the breakeven move, a too-close first target turns trades into scratches: a third off for a few ticks, stop to entry, brushed out of the rest.\nRaising this demands better setups and cuts signal count; lower it if you want closer first targets.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_minBlendRR = input.float(1.3, "Min Blended Scale-Out Reward (× risk, 0 = off)", minval=0.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="v3.5.26: an alternative way to pass the reward gate, measuring the WHOLE plan instead of TP1 alone.\nThe position exits 33/33/34 across TP1/TP2/TP3, so judging a setup purely by TP1 describes a third of it. A plan with a modest first target and strong second and third was rejected on a number that never represented it — the largest single source of 'rr' blocks on 15m gold, where the nearest pivot often sits inside 1R.\nA setup now passes on EITHER the TP1 test OR this weighted reward. That raises trade count by fixing what is measured rather than by lowering the bar.\n0 = off, and only the TP1 test applies, exactly as before.\nNOTE: a trade admitted this way does NOT arm the breakeven move, because a close TP1 dragging the stop to entry is the scratch pattern the v3.5.8 gate exists to prevent.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_maxTp1R    = input.float(2.0, "Max TP1 Distance (× risk, 0 = off)", minval=0.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="v3.5.35: if the nearest STRUCTURE level sits further than this from entry, TP1 falls back to its R-multiple instead and the distant level moves to a later slot.\nMin TP1 Reward enforced a FLOOR of 1.0R and nothing enforced a ceiling. TP1 is simply the nearest pivot or value-area edge above entry, so when no structure exists nearby it lands wherever the next one is — measured live on XAUUSD 30m trade #87: SL 4384.877, TP1 4421.470, entry just above 4391, putting the FIRST target 3.5R-4.5R away.\nThat has three consequences, all bad. Roughly 78% of such trades reach the stop before the first target. The breakeven move keys off TP1, so it never arms and the whole position rides full risk. And the scale-out never begins, so a trade that goes 3R in your favour and turns still books a full loss.\n2.0 bounds the first target's reach probability at about 33%. Lower it for a higher win rate with smaller wins; 0 restores the old unbounded behaviour.\nThis changes level selection only on setups where the nearest structure was already out of reach.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_blendWeighted = input.bool(true, "Weight Blended Reward By Reachability",
     tooltip="v4.2: how the blended reward path weights the three targets.\n\nThe blend exists so a plan with a modest TP1 and strong later targets is not rejected on TP1 alone. v3.5.26 weighted it 33/33/34 — the intended scale-out — and the gate therefore priced every leg as if it filled.\n\nMeasured on 15m, 150 days x 6 seeds: TP1 fills 50% of the time, TP2 14%, TP3 6%. So a third of the admitting number rested on a target reached once in seven trades and another third on one reached once in sixteen.\n\nThe consequence is measurable and blunt: the blended reward's median is 2.68 against a 1.3 threshold, and 100% of setups clear it. i_minBlendRR has never rejected anything. Because rrOk is an OR, that means the documented 1.0R reward gate is bypassed and the engine's real requirement is the 0.5R floor below.\n\nON (default): weights 0.55 / 0.30 / 0.15, roughly the measured fill rates, so the blend describes what the plan tends to deliver rather than what it would deliver if everything filled.\nOFF: the v4.1 33/33/34 arithmetic, exactly.\n\nExpect fewer signals with this on. That is the gate doing the job it was documented as doing.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_minTp1Floor = input.float(0.5, "Absolute TP1 Floor (× risk)", minval=0.0, maxval=3.0, step=0.1,
     tooltip="v3.5.26: TP1 must clear this no matter which path admits the trade. Stops a target a few ticks away being waved through by a strong runner sitting behind it.\nApplies only to the blended path; the ordinary TP1 test already enforces its own, higher bar.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_spreadBuf  = input.float(0.0, "Spread + Slippage Buffer", minval=0.0, step=0.01,
     tooltip="v3.5.16: pushes the stop this far FURTHER from entry, and the reported risk grows to match.\nNothing in the engine modelled spread until now: the stop sat exactly on the structural level, so on a symbol with any spread the level could be taken out by the quote alone while the traded price never reached it. On XAUUSD a 0.30 spread against a 9-point scalp stop is ~3% of risk; on a tighter stop it is worse.\nUnits are set by 'Buffer Units' below. 0 = off, and the engine behaves exactly as before.\nOnly the STOP is buffered. Targets are left alone: widening them would flatter the plan rather than protect it.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_spreadMode = input.string("Price", "Buffer Units", options=["Price", "× ATR"], group=G_TPSL,
     tooltip="v3.5.22: what the buffer above is measured in.\n'Price' — absolute price units, the original behaviour. Gold ~0.20-0.50, FX majors ~0.00010-0.00020. Correct only for the symbol you set it on: 0.30 is ~3% of a scalp stop on gold at 4,400 and rounding error on BTC at 100,000, so the same number silently means different things per instrument.\n'× ATR' — a multiple of the current ATR, so one setting transfers across every symbol and timeframe. Start around 0.03-0.05 ATR for a liquid instrument and raise it if your fills are consistently worse than the drawn stop.\nUse '× ATR' whenever you run the same settings on more than one market."
     )
i_zoneShow   = input.bool(false, "Draw TP / SL As Zones",
     tooltip="v3.5.16: renders a band around each level instead of a bare line. A price level is never exact in practice — spread, slippage and the tick you actually get all sit around it — and a hairline encourages precision the fill will not honour.\nBand height is set below. Display only; it changes no signal and no level.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_zoneAtr    = input.float(0.1, "Zone Half-Height (× ATR)", minval=0.02, maxval=1.0, step=0.02,
     tooltip="Half-height of each TP/SL band, in ATR. 0.1 = a band one fifth of an ATR tall centred on the level.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_maxBars    = input.int(24, "Max Bars In Trade (0 = off)", minval=0, maxval=500,
     tooltip="v3.5.8: closes tracking after this many bars if neither the stop nor TP3 has been reached. Without it a trade that stalls stays 'open' forever — the runner never resolves, the post-TP1/SL bookkeeping never updates, and in the strategy build the position sits there distorting results. Counts as a neutral exit, not a loss.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_invalidExit = input.bool(true, "Close Plan When Setup Invalidates",
     tooltip="v3.5.23: closes an open plan when the setup that justified it has gone, instead of waiting for the stop.\nUntil now the engine scored its conditions once at entry and then stopped thinking. A plan had four ways out — stop, TP3, the time stop (which ships off) and a new signal — so a trade whose reason for existing had evaporated simply hung. Where signals are frequent this is invisible; where they are not it is expensive. Seen live on BTCUSD 1D: entry 66,519, price 62,991, dashboard reading 3/3 SELL and 1/3 BUY, TP1 still anchored to a VAH that had fallen 11,097 points.\nFires only when the OPPOSITE side is fully aligned AND the trade's own side has collapsed to zero, held for the confirm bars below. An ordinary pullback does not trigger it — only a regime flip.\nDoes not count as a stop-out: no cooldown extension, no loss streak.\nEFFECT ON PROFIT FACTOR IS UNMEASURED. It will also close trades that would have recovered. Run the strategy over one range with it on and off and compare.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_invalidConf = input.int(3, "Invalidation Opposite Confluence", minval=1, maxval=3,
     tooltip="v3.5.23: how many of the three confluence legs (HTF bias, VWAP side, FRVP position) the OPPOSITE direction must hold before the plan is considered invalid. 3 = all of them, the strictest and the default.\nLowering it closes trades sooner and more often.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_invalidOwn  = input.int(1, "Invalidation Own-Side Ceiling", minval=0, maxval=2,
     tooltip="v3.5.23: the trade's OWN confluence must be at or below this for the plan to be considered invalid.\nThis is not cosmetic. buyAligned and sellAligned are not mutually exclusive: frvpBullOk and frvpBearOk are BOTH true while price sits inside the value area, so a fully flipped market routinely reads 3/3 against and 1/3 for. Requiring the own side at exactly 0 therefore almost never fires whenever price is inside the value area — which is most of the time.\nThe BTCUSD 1D trade this rule was built for read 1/3 BUY against 3/3 SELL for weeks. A ceiling of 0 would have ignored it entirely; 1 closes it.\nRaise to 2 to close trades sooner and far more often; drop to 0 to require total collapse and accept that the rule will rarely fire.",
     group=G_TPSL)
i_invalidBars = input.int(2, "Invalidation Confirm Bars", minval=1, maxval=20,
     tooltip="v3.5.23: the invalidation condition must hold for this many consecutive CLOSED bars before the plan closes. 1 reacts fastest and is the most easily faked out; higher values demand a sustained flip.",
     group=G_TPSL)

// ── Risk Guards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_minRisk    = input.float(1.0,"Min Risk Required (× ATR)",
     minval=0.5, maxval=5.0, step=0.25,
     tooltip="Signal blocked if entry-to-SL distance < this many ATRs.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_maxRisk    = input.float(3.0,"Max Risk Cap (× ATR)",
     minval=1.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.25,
     tooltip="Signal blocked if entry-to-SL distance > this many ATRs. Kills setups where the pivot is too far away. v3.3 accuracy fix.\nScalp mode: a structural stop beyond the cap is clamped to the cap (volatility stop) instead of blocking (v3.5.1).",
     group=G_RISK)
i_scalpMaxRisk = input.float(3.0, "Scalp Max Risk Cap (× ATR)", minval=1.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.25,
     tooltip="v3.5.39: the widest stop Scalp will accept, in ATR. Was hard-coded to 2.0.\nMeasured with the sim/ harness on 15m over 90 days x 4 seeds, sweeping this cap ALONE with the stop buffer held fixed:\n  cap 2.0 -> 1.28 signals/day    cap 2.5 -> 1.55    cap 3.0 -> 1.79\nThat is +40% signals from one number, and 'risk' was 18.0% of all blocked triggers — the second largest single cause after the volume floor.\nIt costs nothing in quality. Position size is derived from the stop distance, so a wider stop simply means a smaller position for the same money at risk; the setup still has to clear every other gate. The old cap was rejecting valid setups purely for having their invalidation further away, which is a property of the structure, not of the trade.\nMeasured expectancy over the same range: -0.053R at cap 2.0 and -0.023R at cap 3.0, both statistically indistinguishable from zero.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_riskClampMode = input.string("Near side only", "Stop Clamping",
     options=["Off (block instead)","Near side only","Both sides"],
     tooltip="What to do when the structural stop falls outside the min/max risk window.\n\nNEAR SIDE ONLY (default, v3.5.6): a too-TIGHT stop is widened to the min-risk floor — safe, because the stop only moves further from price and stays beyond the invalidation level. A setup whose real invalidation lies beyond the max-risk cap is SKIPPED, not clamped.\n\nBOTH SIDES (v3.5.4/v3.5.5 behavior): also drags a too-far stop IN to the cap, placing it inside the structure that defines the trade, so price can hit it while the setup is still valid — the cause of the Strict/Balanced stop-outs reported against v3.5.5. Choose it only if you want a pure volatility stop.\n\nOFF: both ends gate the signal (pre-v3.5.4).\n\nv3.5.7: Scalp uses the same near-side-only default as every other mode. Its sweep stops are now anchored to the sweep wick rather than the pivot, so they are naturally tight and the far clamp rarely binds; when it would, the invalidation is too far away to be a scalp.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_minConf    = input.int(0, "Min Confluence Required (0-3)", minval=0, maxval=3,
     tooltip="v3.5.12: requires this many of the three directional alignments — HTF side, VWAP side, value-area side. The score has been computed since v3.3 and only ever displayed; this makes it a gate.\nUseful in Scalp specifically, which bypasses the HTF and bias gates entirely: setting 1 or 2 restores some trend context WITHOUT reverting Scalp into Balanced. 0 = off.\nExpect materially fewer signals at 2, very few at 3.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_regimeFilter = input.string("Any", "Volatility Regime Filter", options=["Any","Skip chop","Skip expansion"],
     tooltip="v3.5.12: volRegime (atr14/atr50) has been computed and displayed since v3.3 without ever gating anything.\nSkip chop: no signals when atr14 <= 0.85x atr50 — breakouts fail most in compression.\nSkip expansion: no signals when atr14 >= 1.15x atr50 — avoids entering after the move has already gone.\nWhich helps is symbol-dependent and unmeasured; test one at a time.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_cooldown   = input.int(12,   "Signal Cooldown (bars)", minval=0, maxval=100,
     tooltip="Minimum bars between any two signals. v3.3 relaxed 15→12; all other filters compensate.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_postSlCd   = input.bool(true, "Double Cooldown After SL Hit",
     tooltip="If the prior trade hit its SL before any TP, the next signal is blocked for 2× cooldown.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_beAfterTp1 = input.bool(true, "Move SL To Breakeven After TP1",
     tooltip="v3.5.5: when TP1 trades, move the tracked stop to the entry price. The engine already treated TP1 as 'de-risked' for the cooldown guard but left the stop where it was, so a trade could tag TP1 and still return a full loss. The drawn SL line and label move with it.",
     group=G_RISK)
i_maxLossStreak = input.int(0, "Pause After N Consecutive Losses", minval=0, maxval=20,
     tooltip="v3.5.5: after N stop-outs in a row (none of which reached TP1), block new signals until a trade reaches TP1 again. 0 = disabled. A bar-count cooldown does not help when the regime has turned against the setup type.",
     group=G_RISK)

// ── News Blackout ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_newsFilter = input.bool(false, "Enable News Blackout Windows",
     tooltip="v3.5.3: blocks new signals during the windows below (background tinted while active). Built for oil — EIA/API/OPEC releases produce spike bars that score as perfect order-flow confluence right before they mean-revert. Enable on USOIL/UKOIL/CL; harmless to leave off elsewhere.",
     group=G_NEWS)
i_newsSess1  = input.session("1015-1130:4", "Blackout Window 1",
     tooltip="Session syntax HHMM-HHMM:days, days 1=Sun…7=Sat. Default: EIA crude inventories, Wednesday 10:15-11:30 (in the timezone below).",
     group=G_NEWS)
i_newsSess2  = input.session("1615-1745:3", "Blackout Window 2",
     tooltip="Default: API crude report, Tuesday 16:15-17:45 (in the timezone below).",
     group=G_NEWS)
i_sessFilter = input.bool(false, "Restrict To Trading Session",
     tooltip="v3.5.12: only allow signals inside this window. The most reliable win-rate lever on a 15m intraday chart and the one the engine never had — gold in the Asian session ranges without follow-through, and a continuation engine bleeds there. Default window is the London open through the New York session, weekdays.\nUses the timezone below.",
     group=G_NEWS)
i_sessWindow = input.session("0300-1700:23456", "Trading Session",
     tooltip="Session syntax HHMM-HHMM:days, days 1=Sun…7=Sat. Default 0300-1700 Mon-Fri in the timezone below = London open through the NY session in New York time.",
     group=G_NEWS)
i_newsTz     = input.string("America/New_York", "Blackout Timezone",
     tooltip="IANA timezone the windows are evaluated in, e.g. America/New_York, UTC, Europe/London.",
     group=G_NEWS)

// ── Visuals ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i_buyCol     = input.color(color.new(#00E5FF, 0),  "BUY Color",  group=G_VIS)
i_sellCol    = input.color(color.new(#FF3366, 0),  "SELL Color", group=G_VIS)
i_barTint    = input.bool(true, "Tint bars on OF signal", group=G_VIS)
i_dashboard  = input.bool(true, "Show Confluence Dashboard", group=G_VIS)
i_dashPos    = input.string("Top Right","Dashboard Position",
     options=["Top Right","Top Left","Bottom Right","Bottom Left"], group=G_VIS)
i_debugLabels = input.bool(false, "Show Block-Reason Labels (✕)",
     tooltip="v3.5.2: prints a small ✕ label on every trigger bar that was vetoed, listing the gates that blocked it (body, wick, vol, of%, delta, cvd, mom, mode, bias, cooldown, risk, rsi). The dashboard 'Last Block' row always shows the most recent one regardless of this toggle.",
     group=G_VIS)
i_jsonAlerts = input.bool(true, "JSON Push Alerts", group=G_ALERT)
i_webhookSecret = input.string("", "Webhook Shared Secret",
     tooltip="v3.5.9: when set, every JSON payload carries a \"secret\" field and the n8n workflow rejects anything that does not match. A TradingView webhook URL is a public endpoint — without this, anyone who learns the URL can post a fake signal into your automation. Use a long random string. Leave empty to omit the field.",
     group=G_ALERT)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// SCALP MODE — EFFECTIVE (MODE-ADJUSTED) SETTINGS (v3.5)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Scalp clamps each setting TOWARD the fast preset via min/max, so a user
// input that is already faster/looser than the preset is respected. All other
// modes pass the raw inputs through unchanged.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

isScalp       = i_mode == "Scalp"
effSwingLen   = isScalp ? 2 : i_swingLen
// v3.5.31: NO CLAMP. v3.5.30 clamped Scalp to 2 on a simulation that asked the
// wrong question — it measured whether an entry AT AGE k passes the gates, and
// later bars do score worse. But the number that decides trade COUNT is whether
// a trigger produces an entry ANYWHERE in its window, and every extra bar is
// another attempt:
//   window 1  64.6%   window 2  80.4%   window 3  86.6%   window 5  91.0%
// So the clamp cut Scalp's conversion by about 7% while the reported problem
// was no trades at all. Passthrough now; lower i_triggerAge by hand to trade
// count for entry quality.
// v3.5.38: Scalp is starved on anything above 5m — one clamp set across all
// timeframes means the rate just follows the bar count. Applied only where the
// measurement showed a shortfall; 5m already clears the target and is untouched.
scalpStarved  = isScalp and i_tfCalib and timeframe.in_seconds() >= 900
effTriggerAge = scalpStarved ? math.max(i_triggerAge, 6) : i_triggerAge
effSweepWick  = scalpStarved ? math.min(i_sweepWick, 0.30) : i_sweepWick
effFvgMin     = scalpStarved ? math.min(i_fvgMinAtr, 0.25) : i_fvgMinAtr
effVolFloor   = isScalp ? math.min(i_volFloor, scalpStarved ? 0.7 : 1.0) : i_volFloor
effDeltaFloor = isScalp ? math.min(i_deltaFloor, 0.15) : i_deltaFloor
effOfThresh   = isScalp ? math.min(i_ofThresh,   57.0) : i_ofThresh
effBodyAtr    = isScalp ? math.min(i_bodyAtr,    0.3)  : i_bodyAtr
// v3.5.14: the frequency preset moves three dials, all Scalp-only.
scalpHigh     = isScalp and i_scalpFreq == "High"
scalpStd      = isScalp and i_scalpFreq == "Standard"
effCooldown   = isScalp ? math.min(i_cooldown, scalpStarved ? 3 : (scalpHigh ? 2 : scalpStd ? 4 : 5)) : i_cooldown
effMinRisk    = isScalp ? math.min(i_minRisk,    0.5)  : i_minRisk
// v3.5.29: Scalp's max-risk cap must leave room for the stop buffer, or
// widening the buffer silently blocks every setup instead of widening the
// stop. slBuf can reach i_scalpSlCap x 1.3 (expansion), and riskDist is that
// buffer PLUS the distance to the structural anchor — so at cap 1.5 the buffer
// alone consumes 1.95 of a 2.0 ATR ceiling and riskOkBuy/riskOkSell can never
// pass. The cap now floors at the worst-case buffer plus 0.75 ATR of room.
scalpBufWorst = math.min(i_slAtr, i_scalpSlCap) * 1.3
effMaxRisk    = isScalp ? math.max(math.min(i_maxRisk, i_scalpMaxRisk), scalpBufWorst + 0.75) : i_maxRisk
effTp1R       = isScalp ? math.min(i_tp1R,       1.0)  : i_tp1R
effTp2R       = isScalp ? math.min(i_tp2R,       1.5)  : i_tp2R
effTp3R       = isScalp ? math.min(i_tp3R,       2.0)  : i_tp3R
effRsiBuyLo   = isScalp ? math.min(i_rsiBuyLo,  35)    : i_rsiBuyLo
effRsiBuyHi   = isScalp ? math.max(i_rsiBuyHi,  80)    : i_rsiBuyHi
effRsiSellLo  = isScalp ? math.min(i_rsiSellLo, 20)    : i_rsiSellLo
effRsiSellHi  = isScalp ? math.max(i_rsiSellHi, 65)    : i_rsiSellHi
// v3.5.13: Scalp bypasses the HTF gate AND the structural bias AND (for
// sweeps) the CVD and momentum gates. That is a lot of context removed, and
// it is why Scalp fires in both directions. Requiring at least ONE of the
// three directional alignments puts a floor under that without turning Scalp
// back into Balanced. A user asking for 2 or 3 keeps their stricter setting.
effMinConf    = isScalp ? (scalpHigh ? i_minConf : math.max(i_minConf, 1)) : i_minConf
effMinRR      = isScalp ? math.min(i_minRR, scalpHigh ? 0.7 : scalpStd ? 0.9 : 99.0) : i_minRR

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ATR + VOLATILITY REGIME
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.5.41 HEIKIN ASHI GUARD. On an HA chart TradingView feeds this script the
// SYNTHETIC series: HA close is (o+h+l+c)/4 and HA open is the midpoint of the
// previous HA bar. Neither is a price that ever traded. Every order-flow read
// here — body conviction, close position, CVD sign, the body gate — and every
// trigger that tests `close` would be measuring a moving average of itself.
// Worse, the STOP and TARGETS are derived from close and from pivots of the HA
// high/low, so the levels drawn would be prices that never existed in the
// market and cannot be filled.
// HA has an exact signature: open == (open[1] + close[1]) / 2 on essentially
// every bar. Detect it and warn; the engine is not usable on that chart type.
haBar    = math.abs(open - (open[1] + close[1]) / 2.0) <= syminfo.mintick * 2
haScore  = math.sum(haBar ? 1.0 : 0.0, 50)
isHeikin = bar_index > 60 and haScore >= 46

atr14 = ta.atr(14)
atr50 = ta.atr(50)
volRegime = atr50 > 0 ? atr14 / atr50 : 1.0
volExpand = volRegime >= 1.15
volChop   = volRegime <= 0.85

atrBase   = math.max(atr14, atr50)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ORDER FLOW — v3.3 replaces redundant closePos gate with real delta ratio
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

range_   = math.max(high - low, 1e-9)
closePos = (close - low) / range_
buyPct   = closePos * 100.0

// v3.5.2: symbols with no volume feed (many indices / FX / CFD tickers)
// previously produced volOk=false, deltaRatio=na and a flat CVD — no mode
// could EVER fire on them, silently. Volume-dependent gates self-disable
// when the symbol has never reported volume; bar-shape gates stay active.
var bool volDataSeen = false
if nz(volume) > 0
    volDataSeen := true

// v3.5.4: average the PRIOR 20 bars. Including the current bar in its own
// benchmark damps exactly the spikes this floor exists to detect (a 3× bar
// lifts the divisor it is measured against).
volAvg = ta.sma(nz(volume[1]), 20)
relVol = volDataSeen ? nz(volume) / math.max(volAvg, 1e-9) : 1.0

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.25 DEGENERATE VOLUME DETECTION
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The engine already tolerated a symbol with NO volume (volDataSeen). It had no
// defence against BROKEN volume, which is a different and far more common
// failure: a feed that reports near-zero size on almost every bar and an
// occasional huge spike. relVol is volume / average(volume), so those rare
// spikes drag the average up and every ordinary bar then reads far below 1.0.
// Scalp's floor is min(i_volFloor, 1.0) = 1.0, so the gate becomes permanently
// unpassable and the mode fires nothing at all.
//
// Observed on a broker BTCUSD feed across five separate screenshots: Rel Volume
// 0.11x, 0.19x, 0.23x, 0.33x, then 2.88x. Scalp produced no trades for days
// while gold on the same build traded normally. The setups were there; the
// magnitude test could not be satisfied by that data.
//
// Rather than guess at what "bad volume" looks like, measure whether the gate
// is passable AT ALL: track the share of recent bars that could clear the
// floor. If almost none can, the series cannot support a magnitude test, and
// continuing to apply one is not filtering — it is a permanent veto.
//
// When that happens the FLOOR is bypassed and the dashboard says so. Nothing is
// faked: no volume is invented and no other gate is loosened. CVD and FRVP
// still read the same broken series, which is why the warning is surfaced
// rather than hidden — this keeps Scalp usable, it does not make the data good.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.36 DISPLAY FIX. relVol divides the CURRENT bar's volume by the average
// of the prior 20 — but on the live bar `volume` is only partly accumulated, so
// a bar 50% through its period reads ~0.50x of its true value. The dashboard
// renders on barstate.islast, i.e. always on that unfinished bar, so the Rel
// Volume row systematically understated and looked like a permanently failing
// gate. Five separate XAUUSD screenshots read 0.48x 0.51x 0.52x 0.53x 0.54x — a
// cluster around half, which is a mid-bar artefact, not a volume series (a real
// one ranges roughly 0.1x to 3x).
// The GATE was never affected: signals fire only on barstate.isconfirmed, where
// the bar is complete. Only the readout was wrong — but it was wrong in a way
// that sent the diagnosis after the volume floor for two days.
var float relVolConfirmed = 1.0
if barstate.isconfirmed
    relVolConfirmed := relVol
volPassBar   = volDataSeen and relVol >= effVolFloor ? 1.0 : 0.0
volPassRate  = ta.sma(volPassBar, i_volSanityLen)
volSeriesBad = i_volSanity and volDataSeen and bar_index > i_volSanityLen and volPassRate < i_volSanityMin
volOk  = not volDataSeen or volSeriesBad or relVol >= effVolFloor

// BODY CONVICTION (v3.5.4). Was 2·closePos−1, which is algebraically the same
// test as the OF close-position threshold — the "second" order-flow gate was
// the first one restated, so the stack had one measurement, not two.
// (close−open)/range is independent of where in the range the bar closed:
// a doji closing at its high scores ~0 here and is correctly rejected.
deltaRatio    = (close - open) / range_
deltaBullOk   = deltaRatio >=  effDeltaFloor
deltaBearOk   = deltaRatio <= -effDeltaFloor

// v3.5.1: Scalp ignores the chop tightening — +5% pushed the scalp floor from
// 57% back to 62%, silently overriding the mode preset exactly where scalps
// trade most (chop). Other modes keep the adaptive behavior.
adaptThresh = i_ofAdaptive and volChop and not isScalp ? effOfThresh + 5.0 : effOfThresh

// v3.3: doji contributes zero to CVD (was volume*(2*closePos-1) which drifted EMAs)
sessionChange = ta.change(time("D")) != 0
cvdVol = close > open ? nz(volume) : close < open ? -nz(volume) : 0.0
var float cvd = 0.0
if i_cvdReset and sessionChange
    cvd := 0.0
cvd := cvd + cvdVol
// v3.5: Scalp uses faster CVD EMAs (3/10). All four EMAs are computed
// unconditionally so their series stay consistent; the mode only selects.
cvdEmaF  = ta.ema(cvd, 5)
cvdEmaS  = ta.ema(cvd, 20)
cvdEmaF2 = ta.ema(cvd, 3)
cvdEmaS2 = ta.ema(cvd, 10)
cvdBull  = isScalp ? cvdEmaF2 > cvdEmaS2 : cvdEmaF > cvdEmaS
cvdBear  = isScalp ? cvdEmaF2 < cvdEmaS2 : cvdEmaF < cvdEmaS

bodySize  = math.abs(close - open)
upperWick = high - math.max(close, open)
lowerWick = math.min(close, open) - low
wickRatio = bodySize > 0 ? (upperWick + lowerWick) / bodySize : 999.0
notWeak   = wickRatio <= i_wickMax

// v3.5.4: directional wick tests for REJECTION-based triggers (sweep, FVG
// retest). Those setups are long-wick bars by construction — a sweep must
// spear ≥0.4 ATR through the level and close back — so the combined-wick
// test above rejected the exact bars the trigger selects. For a BUY only the
// UPPER wick is supply/weakness; the lower wick is the rejection being
// traded. Selection happens at signal time, where the trigger flags exist.
wickRatioBuy   = bodySize > 0 ? upperWick / bodySize : 999.0
wickRatioSell  = bodySize > 0 ? lowerWick / bodySize : 999.0
notWeakRejBuy  = wickRatioBuy  <= i_wickMax
notWeakRejSell = wickRatioSell <= i_wickMax

// v3.3: replaced redundant `closePos >= 0.55` with `deltaBullOk` (real second signal)
ofBull = volOk and buyPct          >= adaptThresh and deltaBullOk
ofBear = volOk and (100.0 - buyPct) >= adaptThresh and deltaBearOk

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// MOMENTUM STACK — 2-bar persistence + slope confirmation
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

emaFast = ta.ema(close, i_momFast)
emaSlow = ta.ema(close, i_momSlow)
momBullNow = emaFast > emaSlow
momBearNow = emaFast < emaSlow
momBull2   = momBullNow and momBullNow[1]
momBear2   = momBearNow and momBearNow[1]

emaSlopeBull = emaSlow > emaSlow[3]
emaSlopeBear = emaSlow < emaSlow[3]

momBull = momBull2 and (not i_emaSlope or emaSlopeBull)
momBear = momBear2 and (not i_emaSlope or emaSlopeBear)

// v3.5: Scalp requires only the current-bar EMA relation — no 2-bar
// persistence, no slope. Momentum confirms direction, not maturity.
momBullEff = isScalp ? momBullNow : momBull
momBearEff = isScalp ? momBearNow : momBear

momOkBuy  = not i_momFilter or momBullEff
momOkSell = not i_momFilter or momBearEff

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF TREND — v3.5.1: single tuple request instead of two HTF calls
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.5.17 SECOND REPAINT FIX. This previously requested [close, ema] with
// lookahead_off. On a LIVE chart bar sitting inside a still-forming HTF bar
// that returns the DEVELOPING higher-timeframe close, updating tick by tick.
// After a reload the same chart bar is historical and receives the last
// CLOSED HTF bar instead — a different number, so htfBull could flip between
// what you watched and what the chart redraws.
// It reached the signals because buyAligned/sellAligned count htfBull, and
// since v3.5.13 Scalp enforces a confluence floor of 1: a Scalp entry that
// scored 1/3 live could score 0/3 on reload and simply cease to exist.
// Requesting close[1] with lookahead_on is the standard stable idiom — it
// returns the last CLOSED HTF bar, identical live and historical, and does
// NOT look ahead (the offset is what makes lookahead_on safe here). Cost: the
// HTF view lags by up to one HTF bar, which is the honest price of a value
// that does not change after the fact.
// v3.5.34 AUTO HTF. Derived only from the chart timeframe, so it stays a simple
// string and is valid as a request.security argument. Must be defined BEFORE
// that call — the linter caught it declared after and would have been a compile
// error on the chart.
// Returns 240 for anything at or below 15m, identical to the previous fixed
// default, so the mapping only changes the charts where 240 was actually wrong:
// 4H requested its own timeframe, and Daily requested a LOWER one.
autoHtfTf = timeframe.in_seconds() <= 900 ? "240" : timeframe.in_seconds() <= 14400 ? "D" : timeframe.in_seconds() <= 86400 ? "W" : "M"
effHtfTf  = i_htfAuto ? autoHtfTf : i_htfTf

[htfClose, htfEma] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, effHtfTf, [close[1], ta.ema(close, i_htfEmaLen)[1]], lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_on)
htfBull  = htfClose > htfEma
htfBear  = htfClose < htfEma
htfLabel = htfBull ? "BULL" : "BEAR"

// v3.5.28: is the configured HTF actually ABOVE the chart timeframe? The
// default 240 is on a 15m or 1H chart, and is NOT on a 4H or Daily one — there
// request.security returns the chart's own series (or a lower one) and the
// "HTF trend filter" silently becomes a lagged EMA test on the same data.
htfDegenerate = timeframe.in_seconds(effHtfTf) <= timeframe.in_seconds()
htfFilterOn   = i_htfFilter and not (i_htfGuard and htfDegenerate)
htfOkBull = not htfFilterOn or htfBull
htfOkBear = not htfFilterOn or htfBear

// v3.5: Scalp bypasses the HTF gate everywhere — including inside the
// FVG-retest and Sweep trigger construction, where it was baked in for all
// modes. v3.5.2: Aggressive bypasses it there too — its description says
// "structure + OF only (no HTF gate)" but this trigger-level check still
// applied, quietly delaying or blocking Aggressive entries.
trigHtfBull = isScalp or i_mode == "Aggressive" or htfOkBull
trigHtfBear = isScalp or i_mode == "Aggressive" or htfOkBear

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// VWAP + overextension guard (v3.3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.5.4: anchor is selectable. Session-anchored VWAP on a 4H chart averages
// ~6 bars — not a mean the distance guard can meaningfully gate on.
vwapUseWeek  = i_vwapAnchor == "Week" or (i_vwapAnchor == "Auto" and timeframe.in_seconds() >= 3600)
vwapUseMonth = i_vwapAnchor == "Month"
// The three ta.change() calls are UNCONDITIONAL — stateful builtins placed in
// ternary branches execute inconsistently and corrupt their own history (the
// same defect fixed for ta.highest/ta.lowest in v3.4.1). Select afterwards.
newDay    = ta.change(time("D")) != 0
newWeek   = ta.change(time("W")) != 0
newMonth  = ta.change(time("M")) != 0
vwapAnchor = vwapUseMonth ? newMonth : vwapUseWeek ? newWeek : newDay
vwapVal   = ta.vwap(hlc3, vwapAnchor)
aboveVwap = close > vwapVal
belowVwap = close < vwapVal
vwapLabel = aboveVwap ? "ABOVE" : "BELOW"
plot(i_vwapShow ? vwapVal : na, "VWAP", color=i_vwapCol, linewidth=1)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.0 VWAP DEVIATION BANDS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The engine has computed VWAP since v3.3 and used it only as a filter — a
// yes/no on which side price sits. It was never an entry level. Measured on the
// harness, a rejection at a deviation band supplies 20.9 triggers/day on 15m,
// more than MSS, FVG and sweep combined (14.9), and its trades are
// statistically indistinguishable from theirs (difference -0.014R, t = -0.42
// over 3,854 trades). Trigger SUPPLY was the binding constraint on trade count;
// this is where the supply was hiding.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var float vwSumPV  = 0.0
var float vwSumV   = 0.0
var float vwSumPV2 = 0.0
if vwapAnchor
    vwSumPV  := 0.0
    vwSumV   := 0.0
    vwSumPV2 := 0.0
vwTp = hlc3
vwSumPV  := vwSumPV  + vwTp * nz(volume, 1.0)
vwSumV   := vwSumV   + nz(volume, 1.0)
vwSumPV2 := vwSumPV2 + vwTp * vwTp * nz(volume, 1.0)
vwMean = vwSumPV / math.max(vwSumV, 1e-9)
vwVar  = math.max(vwSumPV2 / math.max(vwSumV, 1e-9) - vwMean * vwMean, 0.0)
vwSd   = math.sqrt(vwVar)
vwUpper1 = vwMean + i_bandK1 * vwSd
vwLower1 = vwMean - i_bandK1 * vwSd
vwUpper2 = vwMean + i_bandK2 * vwSd
vwLower2 = vwMean - i_bandK2 * vwSd

plot(i_bandShow ? vwUpper1 : na, "VWAP +1σ", color=color.new(i_vwapCol, 40), linewidth=1)
plot(i_bandShow ? vwLower1 : na, "VWAP -1σ", color=color.new(i_vwapCol, 40), linewidth=1)
plot(i_bandShow ? vwUpper2 : na, "VWAP +2σ", color=color.new(i_vwapCol, 70), linewidth=1)
plot(i_bandShow ? vwLower2 : na, "VWAP -2σ", color=color.new(i_vwapCol, 70), linewidth=1)

vwapDistAtr = atr14 > 0 ? math.abs(close - vwapVal) / atr14 : 0.0
notExtended = vwapDistAtr <= i_vwapMaxDist

vwapOkBull = (not i_vwapFilter or aboveVwap) and notExtended
vwapOkBear = (not i_vwapFilter or belowVwap) and notExtended

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RSI SANITY (v3.3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

rsi     = ta.rsi(close, i_rsiLen)
rsiOkBuy  = not i_rsiFilter or (rsi >= effRsiBuyLo  and rsi <= effRsiBuyHi)
rsiOkSell = not i_rsiFilter or (rsi >= effRsiSellLo and rsi <= effRsiSellHi)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// NEWS BLACKOUT WINDOWS (v3.5.3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

inNews1 = i_newsFilter and not na(time(timeframe.period, i_newsSess1, i_newsTz))
inNews2 = i_newsFilter and not na(time(timeframe.period, i_newsSess2, i_newsTz))
inNewsWindow = inNews1 or inNews2
newsOk = not inNewsWindow

// v3.5.12: session + regime gates. Both default to permissive so the baseline
// engine is unchanged until deliberately switched on.
inSession    = not i_sessFilter or not na(time(timeframe.period, i_sessWindow, i_newsTz))
regimeOk     = i_regimeFilter == "Any" or (i_regimeFilter == "Skip chop" ? not volChop : not volExpand)
bgcolor(inNewsWindow ? color.new(#FF3366, 94) : na, title="News Blackout Tint")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// PIVOTS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.5: Scalp confirms pivots with 2 bars per side (structure seen 1 bar sooner)
ph = ta.pivothigh(high, effSwingLen, effSwingLen)
pl = ta.pivotlow(low,   effSwingLen, effSwingLen)

var float ph1 = na
var float ph2 = na
var float ph3 = na
var float pl1 = na
var float pl2 = na
var float pl3 = na

if not na(ph)
    ph3 := ph2
    ph2 := ph1
    ph1 := ph

if not na(pl)
    pl3 := pl2
    pl2 := pl1
    pl1 := pl

lastPH = ph1
lastPL = pl1

// v3.5.13: a SECOND, deeper pivot series used only for sweep levels, so the
// liquidity a sweep targets does not shrink to noise when a mode speeds the
// entry pivots up.
phSweep = ta.pivothigh(high, i_sweepPivotLen, i_sweepPivotLen)
plSweep = ta.pivotlow(low,   i_sweepPivotLen, i_sweepPivotLen)
var float sweepPH = na
var float sweepPL = na
if not na(phSweep)
    sweepPH := phSweep
if not na(plSweep)
    sweepPL := plSweep

plotshape(not na(ph), style=shape.triangledown, location=location.abovebar, color=color.new(#FF3366, 60), size=size.tiny, offset=-effSwingLen, title="Pivot High")
plotshape(not na(pl), style=shape.triangleup,   location=location.belowbar, color=color.new(#00E5FF, 60), size=size.tiny, offset=-effSwingLen, title="Pivot Low")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// MSS + trigger freshness (v3.3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

mssFollowPts = atr14 * i_mssFollow
mssUpEvent   = barstate.isconfirmed and not na(lastPH) and close >= lastPH + mssFollowPts and close[1] < lastPH
mssDownEvent = barstate.isconfirmed and not na(lastPL) and close <= lastPL - mssFollowPts and close[1] > lastPL

// Track how many bars since the last MSS event, invalidated by opposite side.
// v3.4.1: counters capped at 999 so they never grow unbounded.
var int mssUpAge = 999
var int mssDnAge = 999
mssUpAge := mssUpEvent ? 0 : math.min(mssUpAge + 1, 999)
mssDnAge := mssDownEvent ? 0 : math.min(mssDnAge + 1, 999)
if mssUpEvent
    mssDnAge := 999   // reset opposite
if mssDownEvent
    mssUpAge := 999
mssUp   = mssUpAge <= effTriggerAge
mssDown = mssDnAge <= effTriggerAge

// ── STRUCTURAL BIAS (v3.3: cross-checked with HTF) ───────────────────────────
var bool structBull = false
var bool structInit = false

if mssUpEvent and htfBull   // only lock bull bias when HTF also bull
    structBull := true
    structInit := true
if mssDownEvent and htfBear
    structBull := false
    structInit := true

// v3.5: Scalp trades both directions — the HTF-locked structural bias would
// block half the scalps, so it is bypassed in Scalp mode only.
biasOkBuy  = isScalp or not structInit or structBull == true
biasOkSell = isScalp or not structInit or structBull == false

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FVGs — with expiration (v3.3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

fvgMinPts    = atr14 * effFvgMin
// v3.5.33 CLOSURE GUARD. The three bars forming an FVG were never checked for
// contiguity, so a market closure — Friday's last bar followed by Sunday's open
// — produced the identical shape and was recorded as an imbalance. On XAUUSD
// the weekend span is 96x a normal 3-bar span on 15m and 288x on 5m, so it
// passes the minimum-size filter without effort. That is an absence of trading,
// not liquidity left behind by aggressive orders, and price returning to it
// carries no information. It also becomes a stop anchor and a chase reference,
// so a single false gap misprices a whole plan.
fvgSpanMs     = time - time[2]
fvgNormalMs   = timeframe.in_seconds() * 1000 * 2
fvgContiguous = not i_fvgGapGuard or na(fvgSpanMs) or fvgSpanMs <= fvgNormalMs * i_fvgGapTol
bullFvgTop   = low
bullFvgBot   = high[2]
bullFvgValid = barstate.isconfirmed and fvgContiguous and bullFvgBot < bullFvgTop and (bullFvgTop - bullFvgBot) >= fvgMinPts and close[1] > open[1]
bearFvgBot   = high
bearFvgTop   = low[2]
bearFvgValid = barstate.isconfirmed and fvgContiguous and bearFvgTop > bearFvgBot and (bearFvgTop - bearFvgBot) >= fvgMinPts and close[1] < open[1]

// ── MULTI-GAP STORAGE (v3.5.10) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Parallel arrays, one entry per unfilled gap. Each expires and invalidates on
// its own; previously a single slot per direction meant a new gap silently
// discarded an older, untested one.
var float[] bullTops  = array.new_float(0)
var float[] bullBots  = array.new_float(0)
var int[]   bullBars  = array.new_int(0)
var box[]   bullBoxes = array.new_box(0)
var float[] bearTops  = array.new_float(0)
var float[] bearBots  = array.new_float(0)
var int[]   bearBars  = array.new_int(0)
var box[]   bearBoxes = array.new_box(0)

// ── RECORD NEW GAPS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// At most one gap per side per bar, so a single shift is enough to hold the cap.
if bullFvgValid
    array.push(bullTops, bullFvgTop)
    array.push(bullBots, bullFvgBot)
    array.push(bullBars, bar_index)
    box nbx = na
    if i_fvgShow
        nbx := box.new(bar_index - 2, bullFvgTop, bar_index + i_fvgMaxAge, bullFvgBot, border_color=color.new(i_fvgBullCol, 30), bgcolor=i_fvgBullCol, border_width=1)
        if i_fvgLabel
            label.new(bar_index, (bullFvgTop + bullFvgBot) / 2.0, "FVG", color=color.new(i_fvgBullCol, 60), textcolor=color.new(i_buyCol, 0), style=label.style_label_right, size=size.tiny)
    array.push(bullBoxes, nbx)
    if array.size(bullTops) > i_fvgMax
        array.shift(bullTops)
        array.shift(bullBots)
        array.shift(bullBars)
        box obx = array.shift(bullBoxes)
        if not na(obx)
            box.delete(obx)

if bearFvgValid
    array.push(bearTops, bearFvgTop)
    array.push(bearBots, bearFvgBot)
    array.push(bearBars, bar_index)
    box nbx2 = na
    if i_fvgShow
        nbx2 := box.new(bar_index - 2, bearFvgTop, bar_index + i_fvgMaxAge, bearFvgBot, border_color=color.new(i_fvgBearCol, 30), bgcolor=i_fvgBearCol, border_width=1)
        if i_fvgLabel
            label.new(bar_index, (bearFvgTop + bearFvgBot) / 2.0, "FVG", color=color.new(i_fvgBearCol, 60), textcolor=color.new(i_sellCol, 0), style=label.style_label_right, size=size.tiny)
    array.push(bearBoxes, nbx2)
    if array.size(bearTops) > i_fvgMax
        array.shift(bearTops)
        array.shift(bearBots)
        array.shift(bearBars)
        box obx2 = array.shift(bearBoxes)
        if not na(obx2)
            box.delete(obx2)

// ── EXPIRY + INVALIDATION, PER GAP ──────────────────────────────────────────
// Walk newest-to-oldest so a removal never shifts an index still to be visited.
// A bar can never both retest and invalidate the same gap: a bull retest needs
// close >= bot, invalidation needs close < bot.
if barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(bullTops) > 0
    for i = array.size(bullTops) - 1 to 0
        if (bar_index - array.get(bullBars, i)) > i_fvgMaxAge or close < array.get(bullBots, i)
            array.remove(bullTops, i)
            array.remove(bullBots, i)
            array.remove(bullBars, i)
            box dbx = array.remove(bullBoxes, i)
            if not na(dbx)
                box.delete(dbx)

if barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(bearTops) > 0
    for i = array.size(bearTops) - 1 to 0
        if (bar_index - array.get(bearBars, i)) > i_fvgMaxAge or close > array.get(bearTops, i)
            array.remove(bearTops, i)
            array.remove(bearBots, i)
            array.remove(bearBars, i)
            box dbx2 = array.remove(bearBoxes, i)
            if not na(dbx2)
                box.delete(dbx2)

// ── RETEST SCAN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Newest gap first, take the first that qualifies, and remember WHICH gap it
// was: v3.5.7 anchors the stop to the retested gap's far edge and measures the
// chase cap from its near edge, so it must be that gap and not merely the most
// recent one.
float hitBullTop = na
float hitBullBot = na
bool  bullRetestHit = false
if array.size(bullTops) > 0
    for i = array.size(bullTops) - 1 to 0
        if not bullRetestHit
            gTopB = array.get(bullTops, i)
            gBotB = array.get(bullBots, i)
            gMidB = (gTopB + gBotB) / 2.0
            if (low <= gTopB and close >= gBotB and closePos >= 0.6) or (i_fvgMidTrig and low <= gMidB and close > gMidB and closePos >= 0.6)
                bullRetestHit := true
                hitBullTop := gTopB
                hitBullBot := gBotB

float hitBearTop = na
float hitBearBot = na
bool  bearRetestHit = false
if array.size(bearTops) > 0
    for i = array.size(bearTops) - 1 to 0
        if not bearRetestHit
            gTopS = array.get(bearTops, i)
            gBotS = array.get(bearBots, i)
            gMidS = (gTopS + gBotS) / 2.0
            if (high >= gBotS and close <= gTopS and closePos <= 0.4) or (i_fvgMidTrig and high >= gMidS and close < gMidS and closePos <= 0.4)
                bearRetestHit := true
                hitBearTop := gTopS
                hitBearBot := gBotS

// Exposed under the historical names so the stop anchor and chase reference
// downstream keep working — now carrying the gap that was actually retested.
lBullTop = hitBullTop
lBullBot = hitBullBot
lBearTop = hitBearTop
lBearBot = hitBearBot

fvgRetestBull = barstate.isconfirmed and bullRetestHit and trigHtfBull
fvgRetestBear = barstate.isconfirmed and bearRetestHit and trigHtfBear

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// SWEEP — with trigger-age (same policy as MSS) — v3.3
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.4.1: ta.highest/ta.lowest hoisted OUT of the ternary. Called inside a
// conditional branch they execute inconsistently and corrupt their window.
legacySweepHi = ta.highest(high[1], i_sweepLook)
legacySweepLo = ta.lowest(low[1],   i_sweepLook)
sweepLevelHi  = i_sweepUsePivot and not na(sweepPH) ? sweepPH : legacySweepHi
sweepLevelLo  = i_sweepUsePivot and not na(sweepPL) ? sweepPL : legacySweepLo

sweepBullEvent = barstate.isconfirmed and not na(sweepLevelLo) and (sweepLevelLo - low) >= atr14 * effSweepWick and close > sweepLevelLo and closePos >= 0.6 and trigHtfBull
sweepBearEvent = barstate.isconfirmed and not na(sweepLevelHi) and (high - sweepLevelHi) >= atr14 * effSweepWick and close < sweepLevelHi and closePos <= 0.4 and trigHtfBear

var int sweepBullAge = 999
var int sweepBearAge = 999
sweepBullAge := sweepBullEvent ? 0 : math.min(sweepBullAge + 1, 999)
sweepBearAge := sweepBearEvent ? 0 : math.min(sweepBearAge + 1, 999)
if sweepBullEvent
    sweepBearAge := 999
if sweepBearEvent
    sweepBullAge := 999
sweepBull = sweepBullAge <= effTriggerAge
sweepBear = sweepBearAge <= effTriggerAge

// v3.5.7: remember the sweep bar's OWN extreme and the level it violated.
// The stop for a sweep entry belongs below that wick — see the SL section.
var float sweepBullLow  = na
var float sweepBullLvl  = na
var float sweepBearHigh = na
var float sweepBearLvl  = na
if sweepBullEvent
    sweepBullLow := low
    sweepBullLvl := sweepLevelLo
if sweepBearEvent
    sweepBearHigh := high
    sweepBearLvl  := sweepLevelHi

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.0 BAND REJECTION TRIGGER
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Price trades THROUGH a deviation band and closes back inside it. Same shape
// as a liquidity sweep — overshoot then reclaim — but measured against the
// volume-weighted mean rather than a pivot, which is why it fires on bars where
// no pivot has been violated. Its invalidation is the extreme of the rejecting
// bar, exactly as a sweep's is.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bandBullEvent = i_bandTrig and barstate.isconfirmed and low  <= vwLower1 and close > vwLower1 and closePos >= 0.55
bandBearEvent = i_bandTrig and barstate.isconfirmed and high >= vwUpper1 and close < vwUpper1 and closePos <= 0.45

var int bandBullAge = 999
var int bandBearAge = 999
bandBullAge := bandBullEvent ? 0 : math.min(bandBullAge + 1, 999)
bandBearAge := bandBearEvent ? 0 : math.min(bandBearAge + 1, 999)
if bandBullEvent
    bandBearAge := 999
if bandBearEvent
    bandBullAge := 999
bandBull = bandBullAge <= effTriggerAge
bandBear = bandBearAge <= effTriggerAge

var float bandBullLow  = na
var float bandBullLvl  = na
var float bandBearHigh = na
var float bandBearLvl  = na
if bandBullEvent
    bandBullLow := low
    bandBullLvl := vwLower1
if bandBearEvent
    bandBearHigh := high
    bandBearLvl  := vwUpper1

// v4.1: the OUTER band (2σ) rejection, a separate and rarer event. Measured at
// 26% of 5m and 17% of 15m entries — supply the 1σ trigger cannot produce
// because a 2σ excursion never touches the 1σ close-back-inside condition on
// the same bar. Its invalidation is the rejecting bar's extreme, as above.
band2BullEvent = i_bandTrig and barstate.isconfirmed and low  <= vwLower2 and close > vwLower2 and closePos >= 0.55
band2BearEvent = i_bandTrig and barstate.isconfirmed and high >= vwUpper2 and close < vwUpper2 and closePos <= 0.45

var int band2BullAge = 999
var int band2BearAge = 999
band2BullAge := band2BullEvent ? 0 : math.min(band2BullAge + 1, 999)
band2BearAge := band2BearEvent ? 0 : math.min(band2BearAge + 1, 999)
if band2BullEvent
    band2BearAge := 999
if band2BearEvent
    band2BullAge := 999
band2Bull = band2BullAge <= effTriggerAge
band2Bear = band2BearAge <= effTriggerAge

var float band2BullLow  = na
var float band2BullLvl  = na
var float band2BearHigh = na
var float band2BearLvl  = na
if band2BullEvent
    band2BullLow := low
    band2BullLvl := vwLower2
if band2BearEvent
    band2BearHigh := high
    band2BearLvl  := vwUpper2

// The band trigger is now either rejection, so the level and invalidation the
// stop is built from must come from whichever fired MORE RECENTLY. Reading the
// 1σ variables unconditionally would anchor a 2σ entry to a stale 1σ event.
bandRefBullLvl  = band2BullAge < bandBullAge ? band2BullLvl  : bandBullLvl
bandRefBullLow  = band2BullAge < bandBullAge ? band2BullLow  : bandBullLow
bandRefBearLvl  = band2BearAge < bandBearAge ? band2BearLvl  : bandBearLvl
bandRefBearHigh = band2BearAge < bandBearAge ? band2BearHigh : bandBearHigh

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FRVP — v3.4 ACCURACY REBUILD (v3.5.1 adds the refresh stride)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// (1) PROPORTIONAL DISTRIBUTION: each bar's volume is spread across every bin
//     its high-low range overlaps, weighted by overlap fraction.
// (2) CLASSIC VA EXPANSION: value area grows by comparing the sum of the next
//     TWO bins above vs the next TWO below (standard Market Profile method).
// (3) BOUNDED RENDERING: lines/zone end at a configurable right offset.
// (4) v3.4.1: profile only builds once a full lookback window exists —
//     referencing high[i]/low[i] before that produced na bin indices and a
//     runtime error on the first bars of the chart.
// (5) v3.5.1: rebuild throttled to every i_frvpEvery bars (+ last bar) — this
//     loop was the dominant load-time cost. Levels persist between rebuilds.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Resolve the window ONCE, here, and use effFrvpLen everywhere below.
// timeframe.in_seconds() and the inputs are all simple values, so effFrvpLen is
// a simple int and ta.highest/ta.lowest accept it directly.
frvpTfSec   = math.max(timeframe.in_seconds(), 1)
frvpWantLen = int(math.round(i_frvpHours * 3600.0 / frvpTfSec))
effFrvpLen  = i_frvpAnchor == "Bars (legacy)" ? i_frvpLen : math.max(20, math.min(i_frvpMaxBars, frvpWantLen))
// what the window ACTUALLY covers, in hours — reported on the dashboard so a
// truncated window is never presented as the window you asked for
frvpEffHrs  = effFrvpLen * frvpTfSec / 3600.0
frvpClamped = i_frvpAnchor != "Bars (legacy)" and effFrvpLen < frvpWantLen
frvpHigh = ta.highest(high, effFrvpLen)
frvpLow  = ta.lowest(low,   effFrvpLen)
binSize  = math.max(frvpHigh - frvpLow, 1e-9) / i_frvpBins

var float[] volBins = array.new_float(0)

frvpReady  = bar_index >= effFrvpLen and not na(frvpHigh) and not na(frvpLow)
// v3.5.11 REPAINT FIX. This previously read
//   (bar_index % i_frvpEvery == 0 or barstate.islast)
// The islast term gave the LIVE bar an extra profile rebuild that it lost the
// moment it became historical, so POC/VAH/VAL differed between what you saw in
// real time and what the chart redrew on reload. VAH/VAL are TP candidates, and
// since v3.5.8 the reward gate decides the signal from TP1 — so a signal could
// appear live and disappear on reload. That is repainting, in a script that
// claims not to.
//
// v3.5.19 THIRD REPAINT FIX. The v3.5.11 note above went on to claim "the
// stride alone is deterministic: identical live and on reload". It is not, and
// this is the bug that claim hid. bar_index counts from the FIRST BAR THE CHART
// HAS LOADED — not from any fixed origin. Scrolling back, reloading, changing a
// setting or simply getting a different history depth shifts every bar's index
// by the same offset, so bar_index % i_frvpEvery == 0 selects an entirely
// DIFFERENT set of bars to rebuild the profile on. POC/VAH/VAL at any given bar
// then differ between two renders of the same chart.
//
// Seen live on XAUUSD 15m: one trade, entry 4343.830, TP1 4357.822 (1.5R) and
// TP2 4362.486 (2R) identical across both renders because they are pure
// arithmetic off entry and stop — while TP3, tagged VAH, read 4395.110 on one
// render and 4399.366 on the next. Only the structure-anchored target moved,
// which is the signature of this bug.
//
// The stride is now keyed to the bar's OPEN TIME divided by the timeframe —
// an absolute quantity that is the same on every render regardless of how much
// history is loaded — so the same bars always carry the rebuild.
frvpBarNo  = int(time / (math.max(timeframe.in_seconds(), 1) * 1000))
frvpRecalc = barstate.isconfirmed and frvpReady and frvpBarNo % i_frvpEvery == 0

if frvpRecalc
    array.clear(volBins)
    for b = 0 to i_frvpBins - 1
        array.push(volBins, 0.0)
    for i = 0 to effFrvpLen - 1
        barHi    = high[i]
        barLo    = low[i]
        barVol   = nz(volume[i])
        barRange = barHi - barLo
        if barRange <= 0
            // zero-range bar: all volume at its single price level
            oneIdx = int(math.floor((barHi - frvpLow) / binSize))
            oneIdx := math.max(0, math.min(oneIdx, i_frvpBins - 1))
            array.set(volBins, oneIdx, array.get(volBins, oneIdx) + barVol)
        else
            // v3.4: distribute volume proportionally across overlapped bins
            loIdx = int(math.floor((barLo - frvpLow) / binSize))
            hiIdx = int(math.floor((barHi - frvpLow) / binSize))
            loIdx := math.max(0, math.min(loIdx, i_frvpBins - 1))
            hiIdx := math.max(0, math.min(hiIdx, i_frvpBins - 1))
            for b2 = loIdx to hiIdx
                binBot  = frvpLow + b2 * binSize
                binTop  = binBot + binSize
                overlap = math.min(binTop, barHi) - math.max(binBot, barLo)
                if overlap > 0
                    array.set(volBins, b2, array.get(volBins, b2) + barVol * (overlap / barRange))

pocBin   = 0
pocVol   = 0.0
totalVol = 0.0

if frvpRecalc and array.size(volBins) == i_frvpBins
    totalVol := array.sum(volBins)
    for b = 0 to i_frvpBins - 1
        bv = array.get(volBins, b)
        if bv > pocVol
            pocVol := bv
            pocBin := b

var float pocPrice = na
var float vahPrice = na
var float valPrice = na

if frvpRecalc and array.size(volBins) == i_frvpBins and totalVol > 0
    pocPrice := frvpLow + (pocBin + 0.5) * binSize
    // v3.4: classic Market Profile VA — extend toward the larger PAIR of bins
    vaTarget  = totalVol * i_vaWidth
    vaVol     = pocVol
    upperIdx  = pocBin
    lowerIdx  = pocBin
    maxIter   = i_frvpBins * 2
    iter      = 0
    while vaVol < vaTarget and iter < maxIter
        // sum of next two bins above the current VA
        upVol   = 0.0
        upSteps = 0
        if upperIdx < i_frvpBins - 1
            upVol   += array.get(volBins, upperIdx + 1)
            upSteps := 1
            if upperIdx < i_frvpBins - 2
                upVol   += array.get(volBins, upperIdx + 2)
                upSteps := 2
        // sum of next two bins below the current VA
        dnVol   = 0.0
        dnSteps = 0
        if lowerIdx > 0
            dnVol   += array.get(volBins, lowerIdx - 1)
            dnSteps := 1
            if lowerIdx > 1
                dnVol   += array.get(volBins, lowerIdx - 2)
                dnSteps := 2
        if upSteps == 0 and dnSteps == 0
            break
        takeUp = upSteps > 0 and (dnSteps == 0 or upVol >= dnVol)
        if takeUp
            upperIdx += upSteps
            vaVol    += upVol
        else
            lowerIdx -= dnSteps
            vaVol    += dnVol
        iter += 1
    vahPrice := frvpLow + (upperIdx + 1) * binSize
    valPrice := frvpLow + lowerIdx       * binSize

frvpPosStr = na(pocPrice) ? "N/A" : close > vahPrice ? "ABOVE VA" : close < valPrice ? "BELOW VA" : close > pocPrice ? "IN VA+" : "IN VA-"
frvpBullOk = na(vahPrice) or close >= valPrice
frvpBearOk = na(valPrice) or close <= vahPrice

// v3.4: bounded rendering — no infinite extend.right, VA fill is a clean box
// v3.5.1: drawn only on the LAST bar. Deleting + recreating 3 lines, a box and
// 3 labels on every confirmed bar across all of history was pure load-time
// waste — the final visual is identical.
var line  pocLine = na
var line  vahLine = na
var line  valLine = na
var box   vaZone  = na
var label pocLab  = na
var label vahLab  = na
var label valLab  = na

if i_frvpShow and barstate.islast and not na(pocPrice)
    if not na(pocLine)
        line.delete(pocLine)
    if not na(vahLine)
        line.delete(vahLine)
    if not na(valLine)
        line.delete(valLine)
    if not na(vaZone)
        box.delete(vaZone)
    if not na(pocLab)
        label.delete(pocLab)
    if not na(vahLab)
        label.delete(vahLab)
    if not na(valLab)
        label.delete(valLab)
    xL = bar_index - effFrvpLen
    xR = bar_index + i_frvpExtend
    if i_vaFill
        vaZone := box.new(xL, vahPrice, xR, valPrice, bgcolor=color.new(i_vahCol, 92), border_color=color.new(i_vahCol, 100))
    pocLine := line.new(xL, pocPrice, xR, pocPrice, color=i_pocCol, width=2, style=line.style_solid)
    vahLine := line.new(xL, vahPrice, xR, vahPrice, color=i_vahCol, width=1, style=line.style_dashed)
    valLine := line.new(xL, valPrice, xR, valPrice, color=i_valCol, width=1, style=line.style_dashed)
    if i_frvpLevels
        pocLab := label.new(xR, pocPrice, "POC " + str.tostring(pocPrice, format.mintick), style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#000000, 100), textcolor=i_pocCol, size=size.tiny)
        vahLab := label.new(xR, vahPrice, "VAH " + str.tostring(vahPrice, format.mintick), style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#000000, 100), textcolor=color.new(i_vahCol, 0), size=size.tiny)
        valLab := label.new(xR, valPrice, "VAL " + str.tostring(valPrice, format.mintick), style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#000000, 100), textcolor=color.new(i_valCol, 0), size=size.tiny)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// MODE GATES
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.5: Scalp gates only on the VWAP overextension guard (never chase an
// extended move); Aggressive remains fully ungated.
// v4.1: the Strict-only FRVP requirement is gone. It was a third veto stacked on
// top of the trigger ban and the location rule, and with all three in place
// Strict could not reach one trade a day on any timeframe. Selectivity now comes
// from the confluence requirement below, which every mode is measured against on
// the same five readings.
modePassBull = i_mode == "Aggressive" ? true : isScalp ? notExtended : (htfOkBull and vwapOkBull)
modePassBear = i_mode == "Aggressive" ? true : isScalp ? notExtended : (htfOkBear and vwapOkBear)

// v3.5.5: confluence score, 0-3. Was computed from the *Ok flags, which are
// true whenever their filter is switched OFF — a disabled filter counted as
// alignment and inflated the score. Built from raw directional facts now, and
// actually surfaced (dashboard row + "confluence" in the alert payload);
// previously these two values were computed every bar and never read.
buyAligned  = (htfBull ? 1 : 0) + (aboveVwap ? 1 : 0) + (frvpBullOk ? 1 : 0)
sellAligned = (htfBear ? 1 : 0) + (belowVwap ? 1 : 0) + (frvpBearOk ? 1 : 0)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ADAPTIVE SL — never tightens (non-Scalp modes)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v3.5.1 SCALP FIX. The SL was built from atrBase = max(atr14, atr50) and
// widened ×1.2 in chop, while the risk window was measured in atr14 — so in
// chop the structural stop routinely exceeded Scalp's 2× atr14 cap and the
// signal was blocked. This mismatch is why Scalp mode "did nothing".
// Scalp now: (a) builds the SL from atr14 (the same ATR the risk window
// uses), (b) clamps the pivot buffer to ≤0.3 ATR with no regime widening,
// (c) clamps a structural stop that lands beyond the max-risk cap TO the cap
// (volatility stop) instead of blocking the trade. Other modes are unchanged.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.5.2/v3.5.4: ONE ATR reference — the stop is built, judged, displayed and
// reported in the same units. Mixing atr14 and atrBase across those four
// places is what silently blocked entries in every mode before v3.5.2.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.1 TRIGGER ADMISSION — every trigger, every mode
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Through v4.0 the mode decided WHICH TRIGGERS EXIST. Strict saw only MSS: FVG,
// sweep and band were all switched off for it, so Strict was starved by
// construction, not by market conditions. Measured on 150 days x 6 seeds it
// produced 0.38 signals/day on 15m, 0.21 on 30m and 0.07 on 1H — a mode that
// does not trade. The location veto behind it was the sole cause of 1066 of
// 30m Strict's blocks and appeared in 69-74% of all of them.
//
// Every trigger is now admitted in every mode. The mode instead sets HOW MANY
// INDEPENDENT THINGS MUST AGREE (the confluence requirement below). That is a
// condition any bar can satisfy, rather than one only a rare price location can,
// which is what restores supply: 15m Strict 0.38 -> 1.45/day, 30m 0.21 -> 1.08,
// 1H 0.07 -> 0.63, while win rate still rises with strictness
// (Scalp 44.6% -> Balanced 46.9% -> Strict 46.5% on 15m; 47.6 -> 48.8 -> 48.9
// on 4H). Trade count falls and selection quality rises across the ladder, which
// is what mode is supposed to mean.
buyTrigFvg   = fvgRetestBull
sellTrigFvg  = fvgRetestBear
buyTrigSweep = sweepBull
sellTrigSweep= sweepBear
buyTrigBand  = bandBull  or band2Bull
sellTrigBand = bandBear  or band2Bear

buyTrigger  = mssUp   or buyTrigFvg  or buyTrigSweep  or buyTrigBand
sellTrigger = mssDown or sellTrigFvg or sellTrigSweep or sellTrigBand

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.2 THE SELECTED TRIGGER — decided ONCE, and everything downstream reads it
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v4.1 decided which trigger a trade "is" in FOUR separate places, and the four
// used THREE different priority orders:
//
//     buyType      name and alert      MSS   > FVG  > SWEEP > BAND
//     buySlAnchor  where the stop goes  SWEEP > BAND > FVG  > MSS
//     buyChaseRef  the chase reference  SWEEP > BAND > FVG  > MSS
//     tpMeanBuy    target style         BAND, whenever a band was live at all
//
// So when MSS and a sweep fired on the same bar, the chart said MSS, the stop
// was placed beyond the SWEEP's wick, the chase cap was measured from the swept
// level, and a live band injected the VWAP mean as a target. One signal number,
// four different trades.
//
// MEASURED, 15m, 150 days x 6 seeds: 9,832 of 17,738 bullish trigger bars carry
// TWO OR MORE distinct triggers — 55%. This is the majority case, not an edge.
// Worse, v4.1's own confluence score AWARDS A POINT for stacking
// (buyTrigCount >= 2), so the engine rates highest exactly the bars on which its
// four descriptions disagree most.
//
// ONE order now, chosen by how precisely the trigger defines its own
// invalidation — the thing the stop is built from:
//
//     SWEEP  the rejecting bar's wick     tightest, a dated single-bar extreme
//     BAND   the rejecting bar's extreme  same shape, measured off the mean
//     FVG    the far edge of the gap      a zone, not a bar
//     MSS    the last pivot               widest, and the least specific
//
// This is the order the STOP already used, so stop placement is unchanged. What
// changes is that the name, the chase reference and the target style now agree
// with it instead of contradicting it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
buySel  = buyTrigSweep  ? "SWEEP" : buyTrigBand  ? "BAND" : buyTrigFvg  ? "FVG" : mssUp   ? "MSS" : ""
sellSel = sellTrigSweep ? "SWEEP" : sellTrigBand ? "BAND" : sellTrigFvg ? "FVG" : mssDown ? "MSS" : ""

// ── Confluence score, 0-5 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Five independent readings of the same bar. None is a restatement of another,
// and none is a filter that can be switched off and then silently count as
// agreement (the v3.5.5 defect).
buyTrigCount  = (mssUp   ? 1 : 0) + (buyTrigFvg  ? 1 : 0) + (buyTrigSweep  ? 1 : 0) + (buyTrigBand  ? 1 : 0)
sellTrigCount = (mssDown ? 1 : 0) + (sellTrigFvg ? 1 : 0) + (sellTrigSweep ? 1 : 0) + (sellTrigBand ? 1 : 0)
// v4.2: did more than one trigger fire on this bar? Measured at 55% of bullish
// trigger bars on 15m, which is why v4.1's four disagreeing priority orders were
// the majority case rather than a corner. The label carries a "+" when set.
buySelStack  = buyTrigCount  >= 2
sellSelStack = sellTrigCount >= 2
// Location scores only for a trigger that is NOT itself a band rejection — a
// band trigger sits at a band by definition, so counting it is double-counting.
// Leaving it in made Strict and Balanced produce identical signals on 1H and 4H,
// where band rejections supply 74% of entries.
buyAtBand  = not buyTrigBand  and close <= vwLower1 * 1.002
sellAtBand = not sellTrigBand and close >= vwUpper1 * 0.998

confV4Buy  = (relVol >= 1.20 ? 1 : 0) + (bodySize >= atr14 * 0.55 ? 1 : 0) + (closePos >= 0.70 ? 1 : 0) + (buyTrigCount  >= 2 ? 1 : 0) + (buyAtBand  ? 1 : 0)
confV4Sell = (relVol >= 1.20 ? 1 : 0) + (bodySize >= atr14 * 0.55 ? 1 : 0) + (closePos <= 0.30 ? 1 : 0) + (sellTrigCount >= 2 ? 1 : 0) + (sellAtBand ? 1 : 0)

// Aggressive takes anything; Scalp takes any single trigger; Balanced wants one
// corroborating reading; Strict wants two.
confNeed = i_mode == "Strict" ? 2 : i_mode == "Balanced" ? 1 : 0
v4ConfOkBuy  = confV4Buy  >= confNeed
v4ConfOkSell = confV4Sell >= confNeed

riskAtrRef = isScalp ? atr14 : atrBase
// v3.5.32: each trigger's OWN reference level, hoisted above the stop block so
// it can also serve as the ENTRY price in level-retest mode. Previously it was
// computed further down and used only by the anti-chase cap.
// v4.2: keyed to buySel, so the level the chase cap is measured from is the
// level of the trigger the trade is actually reported as.
buyChaseRef  = buySel  == "SWEEP" and not na(sweepBullLvl)   ? sweepBullLvl   : buySel  == "BAND" and not na(bandRefBullLvl)  ? bandRefBullLvl  : buySel  == "FVG" and not na(lBullTop) ? lBullTop : buySel  == "MSS" ? lastPH : na
sellChaseRef = sellSel == "SWEEP" and not na(sweepBearLvl)   ? sweepBearLvl   : sellSel == "BAND" and not na(bandRefBearLvl)  ? bandRefBearLvl  : sellSel == "FVG" and not na(lBearBot) ? lBearBot : sellSel == "MSS" ? lastPL : na

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.32 ENTRY REFERENCE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Everything downstream — the stop, the risk distance, the targets and the
// reward gate — is measured from the ENTRY. At market that is the bar close. In
// level-retest mode it is the trigger's own level, because that is where the
// pending order sits. Using the close there would size the plan against a price
// the trade will never be filled at.
// pendBuyLvl/pendSellLvl are declared further down and hold NA until a level is
// armed, so on the arming bar this correctly falls through to buyChaseRef.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
levelMode    = i_entryMode == "Level retest (pending)"
var float pendBuyLvl  = na
var float pendSellLvl = na
var int   pendBuyBar  = na
var int   pendSellBar = na
buyEntryRef  = levelMode ? (not na(pendBuyLvl)  ? pendBuyLvl  : not na(buyChaseRef)  ? buyChaseRef  : close) : close
sellEntryRef = levelMode ? (not na(pendSellLvl) ? pendSellLvl : not na(sellChaseRef) ? sellChaseRef : close) : close

slMult    = volExpand ? 1.3 : volChop ? 1.2 : 1.0
// v3.5.26 SCALP STOP COMFORT. Scalp hard-clamped the pad to 0.3 ATR AND was
// the only mode denied slMult, so where every other mode widened its stop by
// 1.3x in expanding volatility and 1.2x in chop, Scalp held a flat 0.3 ATR
// through both. The pad is thinnest in exactly the conditions that overrun it.
// Scalp now takes slMult like the rest, and the clamp is an input.
slBuf     = isScalp ? riskAtrRef * math.min(i_slAtr, i_scalpSlCap) * slMult : riskAtrRef * i_slAtr * slMult

// v3.5.7 TRIGGER-AWARE INVALIDATION. The stop was ALWAYS built from the last
// pivot, whatever fired the entry. For a sweep that is provably wrong: the
// sweep is DEFINED by price trading at least i_sweepWick (0.4 ATR) BELOW the
// pivot, while the stop was placed only slBuf (≤0.3 ATR in Scalp) below the
// same pivot — so the stop sat ABOVE the sweep's own wick, inside the move
// that created the setup. An ordinary retest of the sweep low took it out.
// Each trigger now stops beyond its own invalidation:
//   sweep → beyond the sweep bar's extreme (the wick IS the invalidation)
//   FVG   → beyond the far edge of the gap
//   MSS   → the last pivot, as before (correct for continuation)
// v4.2: keyed to buySel. The order is the one this line already used, so stop
// placement does not move — it is now simply the SAME decision the label makes.
buySlAnchor  = buySel  == "SWEEP" and not na(sweepBullLow)   ? sweepBullLow   : buySel  == "BAND" and not na(bandRefBullLow)  ? bandRefBullLow  : buySel  == "FVG" and not na(lBullBot) ? lBullBot : lastPL
sellSlAnchor = sellSel == "SWEEP" and not na(sweepBearHigh)  ? sweepBearHigh  : sellSel == "BAND" and not na(bandRefBearHigh) ? bandRefBearHigh : sellSel == "FVG" and not na(lBearTop) ? lBearTop : lastPH

buySLraw  = not na(buySlAnchor)  ? math.min(buySlAnchor - slBuf,  buyEntryRef - riskAtrRef * 0.5) : buyEntryRef - riskAtrRef * 1.5
sellSLraw = not na(sellSlAnchor) ? math.max(sellSlAnchor + slBuf, sellEntryRef + riskAtrRef * 0.5) : sellEntryRef + riskAtrRef * 1.5

// v3.5.4 DEAD-ZONE FIX. The structural stop was floored at 0.5 ATR from
// price while the min-risk gate demanded 1.0 ATR — so a near pivot produced
// a stop the engine then rejected, discarding the setup instead of using a
// stop it would accept. Clamp into BOTH ends of the window (all modes;
// v3.5.1 already did the max side for Scalp). Turning the clamp off restores
// the old block-instead-of-clamp behavior.
slFar     = riskAtrRef * effMaxRisk
slNear    = riskAtrRef * effMinRisk
clampOn   = i_riskClampMode != "Off (block instead)"
// v3.5.6: the FAR side is the unsound one — it pulls the stop inside the
// structure that defines the trade. Only Scalp (whose design IS a tight
// volatility stop) or an explicit "Both sides" choice gets it.
clampFar  = clampOn and i_riskClampMode == "Both sides"
clampNear = clampOn
buySLfar  = clampFar  ? math.max(buySLraw,  buyEntryRef - slFar) : buySLraw
sellSLfar = clampFar  ? math.min(sellSLraw, sellEntryRef + slFar) : sellSLraw
buySLbase  = clampNear ? math.min(buySLfar,  buyEntryRef - slNear) : buySLfar
sellSLbase = clampNear ? math.max(sellSLfar, sellEntryRef + slNear) : sellSLfar

// v3.5.6: is the stop the real structural level, or one we moved? Shown on
// the SL label, the dashboard and in the alert payload, so a synthetic stop
// is never mistaken for a structural one. Compared BEFORE the spread buffer,
// which is a broker cost rather than an engine decision.
buyClamped   = buySLbase  != buySLraw
sellClamped  = sellSLbase != sellSLraw

// v3.5.16 SPREAD BUFFER. The stop previously sat exactly on the structural
// level, so on any symbol with a spread the level could be taken out by the
// quote while the traded price never reached it. Push the stop out by the
// buffer and let the reported risk grow to match — an honest R beats a
// flattering one. Targets are deliberately NOT buffered.
// v3.5.22: the buffer was absolute price only, which does not survive a change
// of instrument. 0.30 is ~3% of a scalp stop on gold at 4,400 and meaningless
// on BTC at 100,000 — the same input silently means different things per
// symbol. "× ATR" expresses it in the instrument's own volatility instead, so
// one setting is correct everywhere.
spreadPx  = i_spreadMode == "× ATR" ? atr14 * i_spreadBuf : i_spreadBuf
buySLpx   = buySLbase  - spreadPx
sellSLpx  = sellSLbase + spreadPx
buyStopTxt   = buyClamped  ? " SL~ " : " SL  "
sellStopTxt  = sellClamped ? " SL~ " : " SL  "

buyRiskDist  = math.max(buyEntryRef - buySLpx,  atr14 * 0.1)
sellRiskDist = math.max(sellSLpx - sellEntryRef, atr14 * 0.1)

// v3.3: both a floor AND a cap. With the clamp ON these always pass (the stop
// was moved into the window); they still gate when the clamp is OFF.
riskOkBuy  = buyRiskDist  >= riskAtrRef * effMinRisk and buyRiskDist  <= riskAtrRef * effMaxRisk
riskOkSell = sellRiskDist >= riskAtrRef * effMinRisk and sellRiskDist <= riskAtrRef * effMaxRisk

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// UNIFIED TP SELECTION (v3.4.1; v3.5.1 computes it lazily on signal bars)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One function picks TP1/2/3 from structure (pivots + VAH/VAL) with R-multiple
// fallbacks. The SAME levels feed the chart lines, the labels AND the JSON
// alert — v3.4 alerts sent raw R-multiples while the chart showed pivot
// targets, so webhook fills never matched the displayed plan.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v4.0: a band rejection is a REVERSION trade — price overshot the mean and is
// coming back to it — so the mean itself is its natural first target. Giving it
// the breakout ladder measured -0.075R against -0.062R with the mean included.
// Set to na for every other trigger, so nothing else is affected.
// v4.2: fires only when BAND is the SELECTED trigger. v4.1 read buyTrigBand
// directly, so a trade reported and stopped as an MSS continuation also had the
// VWAP mean injected as a target whenever any band happened to be live — a
// reversion target inside a continuation plan.
tpMeanBuy  = buySel  == "BAND" ? vwMean : na
tpMeanSell = sellSel == "BAND" ? vwMean : na

// v4.2 LADDER UNIT. In v4.1 every target was a multiple of the STOP DISTANCE,
// so a structural stop near the 3.0 ATR cap pushed TP3 out to 12 ATR and the
// scale-out's last two legs stopped being reachable. Measured on 15m over 150
// days x 6 seeds with identical entries and identical stops, only the ladder
// changing: TP2 filled 14% of the time and TP3 6%. A plan that leaves in thirds
// realised nothing like thirds.
// Measuring the multiples in ATR instead breaks that coupling — the stop still
// goes where structure puts it, and the targets stop being dragged out with it.
tpUnitBuy  = i_tpUnit == "Risk / R (v4.1)" ? buyRiskDist  : atr14
tpUnitSell = i_tpUnit == "Risk / R (v4.1)" ? sellRiskDist : atr14

f_tpLevels(bool _isBuy, float _entry, float _risk, float _mean, float _unit) =>
    // v3.5.20 REACH CAP. Structure candidates were accepted at ANY distance —
    // the only test was that the level sat the right side of entry. Scalp
    // clamps its R-multiple fallbacks to 1.0/1.5/2.0R, so a VAH handing it a
    // 6R target broke the mode's own ceiling. Anything beyond the cap is
    // dropped here and the slot falls back to its R-multiple.
    float maxDist = i_maxTpR > 0 ? _risk * i_maxTpR : 1.0e12
    float[] raw = array.new_float(0)
    if _isBuy
        if not na(ph1) and ph1 > _entry and ph1 - _entry <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, ph1)
        if not na(ph2) and ph2 > _entry and ph2 - _entry <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, ph2)
        if not na(ph3) and ph3 > _entry and ph3 - _entry <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, ph3)
        if not na(vahPrice) and vahPrice > _entry and vahPrice - _entry <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, vahPrice)
        if not na(_mean) and _mean > _entry and _mean - _entry <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, _mean)
        if array.size(raw) > 1
            array.sort(raw, order.ascending)
    else
        if not na(pl1) and pl1 < _entry and _entry - pl1 <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, pl1)
        if not na(pl2) and pl2 < _entry and _entry - pl2 <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, pl2)
        if not na(pl3) and pl3 < _entry and _entry - pl3 <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, pl3)
        if not na(valPrice) and valPrice < _entry and _entry - valPrice <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, valPrice)
        if not na(_mean) and _mean < _entry and _entry - _mean <= maxDist
            array.push(raw, _mean)
        if array.size(raw) > 1
            array.sort(raw, order.descending)
    // v3.5.3: merge structure candidates closer than max(0.5 ATR, 0.25R) to
    // the previously kept one — a double top/bottom otherwise fills TWO TP
    // slots with near-identical prices and the plan loses a real target.
    float minGap = math.max(atr14 * 0.5, _risk * 0.25)
    float[] cands = array.new_float(0)
    if array.size(raw) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(raw) - 1
            c = array.get(raw, i)
            if array.size(cands) == 0 or math.abs(c - array.get(cands, array.size(cands) - 1)) >= minGap
                array.push(cands, c)
    int n     = array.size(cands)
    float lvl = _isBuy ? vahPrice : valPrice
    string lt = _isBuy ? " VAH" : " VAL"
    float dir = _isBuy ? 1.0 : -1.0
    // v3.5.21 SPACING FIX. The minGap merge above ran over structure candidates
    // ONLY. Structure filled the slots first and any leftover slot took an
    // R-multiple, but the two sets were never gap-checked against each other —
    // so a fallback could land a fraction of a point from a structure level and
    // two of the three targets collapsed onto effectively one price, costing
    // the scale-out a real leg. On the reported XAUUSD trade the pivot at
    // 4362.000 and the 2R fallback at 4362.486 sat 0.486 apart against a minGap
    // of 2.332 — TP2 and TP3 were the same target wearing two labels.
    //
    // Structure levels are placed first and keep their EXACT prices (a nudged
    // pivot is no longer that pivot, and its tag would be a lie). Only
    // fallbacks move: each is pushed outward, away from entry, until it clears
    // every level already placed, and is retagged with the R it actually ends
    // up at. Bounded passes, no unbounded loop.
    // Slot s keeps its original source: structure candidate s when one exists,
    // otherwise R-multiple s. That mapping is unchanged from every previous
    // version — only the collision nudge is new, so a plan that never collided
    // comes out bit-identical.
    // v3.5.35 TP1 CEILING. The nearest structure level becomes TP1 no matter how
    // far away it is — the reward gate only ever enforced a MINIMUM. With no
    // structure near entry the first target lands several R out, so the trade
    // must run 3-4R to bank anything, the breakeven move (which keys off TP1)
    // never arms, and the scale-out never begins. Measured live on XAUUSD 30m
    // #87: TP1 3.5R-4.5R from entry, ~78% chance of hitting the stop first.
    // When the nearest candidate is out of reach, insert the R-multiple ahead
    // of it: the distant level is not discarded, it simply becomes a later
    // target instead of the first one.
    float capT1 = i_maxTp1R > 0 ? _risk * i_maxTp1R : 1.0e12
    bool t1Inj  = false
    if n >= 1 and math.abs(array.get(cands, 0) - _entry) > capT1
        array.unshift(cands, _entry + dir * _unit * effTp1R)
        n := array.size(cands)
        t1Inj := true
    float[]  rMul = array.from(effTp1R, effTp2R, effTp3R)
    float[]  fV   = array.new_float(0)
    string[] fT   = array.new_string(0)
    for s = 0 to 2
        if s < n
            sv = array.get(cands, s)
            array.push(fV, sv)
            array.push(fT, s == 0 and t1Inj ? " " + str.tostring(effTp1R) + "R" : (not na(lvl) and sv == lvl ? lt : " Pivot"))
        else
            float rv = _entry + dir * _unit * array.get(rMul, s)
            for pass = 0 to 3
                if array.size(fV) > 0
                    for j = 0 to array.size(fV) - 1
                        if math.abs(rv - array.get(fV, j)) < minGap
                            rv := array.get(fV, j) + dir * minGap
            array.push(fV, rv)
            rTag = math.round(math.abs(rv - _entry) / math.max(_risk, 1e-9), 1)
            array.push(fT, " " + str.tostring(rTag) + "R")
    tp1 = array.get(fV, 0)
    tp2 = array.get(fV, 1)
    tp3 = array.get(fV, 2)
    tag1 = array.get(fT, 0)
    tag2 = array.get(fT, 1)
    tag3 = array.get(fT, 2)
    // v3.5.15 ORDERING FIX. Structure candidates fill the slots first and any
    // remainder falls back to R-multiples — but the two sets were never sorted
    // TOGETHER, so a lone distant structure level took TP1 while the nearer
    // R-multiples sat below it. Seen live on XAUUSD: TP1 4395.11 (VAH, 5.5R),
    // TP2 4357.82 (1.5R), TP3 4362.49 (2R) — the scale-out plan inverted, and
    // because the breakeven move keys off TP1 it would essentially never arm.
    // Order the final three in the trade's direction, tags following values.
    float[]  tpV = array.from(tp1, tp2, tp3)
    string[] tpT = array.from(tag1, tag2, tag3)
    for a = 0 to 1
        for b = a + 1 to 2
            vA = array.get(tpV, a)
            vB = array.get(tpV, b)
            if _isBuy ? vB < vA : vB > vA
                array.set(tpV, a, vB)
                array.set(tpV, b, vA)
                tmpT = array.get(tpT, a)
                array.set(tpT, a, array.get(tpT, b))
                array.set(tpT, b, tmpT)
    [array.get(tpV, 0), array.get(tpV, 1), array.get(tpV, 2), array.get(tpT, 0), array.get(tpT, 1), array.get(tpT, 2)]

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// SIGNAL GENERATION
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

var int  lastSigBar   = 0
var bool lastTradeBuy = false
var bool lastSlHit    = false
effCd = i_postSlCd and lastSlHit ? effCooldown * 2 : effCooldown
coolOk = bar_index - lastSigBar >= effCd

// v3.5.5: consecutive-loss circuit breaker. Declared here (before the signal
// gates read it) but UPDATED in the TP/SL tracking section further down, so
// on any bar it holds the streak as of that bar's open — the same pattern
// lastSlHit already uses.
var int lossStreak = 0
breakerOk = i_maxLossStreak == 0 or lossStreak < i_maxLossStreak

// v3.5.1 SCALP FIX. A bullish sweep happens at the END of a decline — CVD and
// EMA momentum are still bearish on that bar by construction, so requiring
// cvdBull + momOkBuy vetoed every sweep-reversal scalp. Sweep-triggered
// scalps are exempt from both trend gates; the sweep's own rejection close
// (closePos ≥ 0.6 back above the level), the delta floor, the OF threshold
// and the volume floor still gate the entry. MSS/FVG scalps (continuation
// setups) keep both gates. Non-Scalp modes are unchanged.
// v3.5.2: CVD gate also self-disables on symbols with no volume feed —
// with volume permanently na the CVD was flat and vetoed everything.
cvdOkBuy   = not volDataSeen or cvdBull  or (isScalp and (buyTrigSweep  or buyTrigBand))
cvdOkSell  = not volDataSeen or cvdBear  or (isScalp and (sellTrigSweep or sellTrigBand))
momOkBuyEff  = momOkBuy  or (isScalp and (buyTrigSweep  or buyTrigBand))
momOkSellEff = momOkSell or (isScalp and (sellTrigSweep or sellTrigBand))

// v3.5.4: rejection-based entries (sweep, FVG retest) are judged on the
// opposing wick only — see the wickRatioBuy/Sell definitions. MSS entries
// keep the combined-wick test. Without this the wick filter cancelled the
// sweep trigger it was paired with.
notWeakBuy  = (buyTrigSweep  or buyTrigFvg  or buyTrigBand)  ? notWeakRejBuy  : notWeak
notWeakSell = (sellTrigSweep or sellTrigFvg or sellTrigBand) ? notWeakRejSell : notWeak

// v3.5.6 ANTI-CHASE. i_mssFollow sets a MINIMUM break distance past the pivot;
// nothing ever set a maximum. With the freshness window the entry could land
// several bars and several ATR beyond the level that was broken, while the
// stop still sat under the swing low — the widest-risk, worst-price version
// of the trade. Only gate this when the breakout is the SOLE trigger: sweep
// and FVG-retest entries occur AT a level and are never "extended".
// v3.5.7: extended to sweep and FVG entries, each measured from its own
// reference. v3.5.6 only capped MSS, on the reasoning that sweep/FVG entries
// occur AT a level — true on the trigger bar, but the freshness window lets
// them fire up to i_triggerAge bars later, by which time price has left the
// level and the reclaim edge is gone.
chaseOkBuy   = na(buyChaseRef)  or (close - buyChaseRef)  <= atr14 * i_maxChase
chaseOkSell  = na(sellChaseRef) or (sellChaseRef - close) <= atr14 * i_maxChase

barBull = close > open
barBear = close < open

strongBull = bodySize >= atr14 * effBodyAtr
strongBear = bodySize >= atr14 * effBodyAtr

rawBuyPre  = barstate.isconfirmed and buyTrigger  and barBull and strongBull and notWeakBuy  and ofBull and cvdOkBuy  and momOkBuyEff  and modePassBull and biasOkBuy  and coolOk and riskOkBuy  and rsiOkBuy  and newsOk and breakerOk and chaseOkBuy  and inSession and regimeOk
rawSellPre = barstate.isconfirmed and sellTrigger and barBear and strongBear and notWeakSell and ofBear and cvdOkSell and momOkSellEff and modePassBear and biasOkSell and coolOk and riskOkSell and rsiOkSell and newsOk and breakerOk and chaseOkSell and inSession and regimeOk

// v3.5.1: TP selection ran on EVERY bar; it is only consumed on signal bars.
// v3.5.8: it now runs on the PRE-signal, because the reward gate below has
// to know where TP1 is before the signal can be confirmed. Still rare.
float  buyTp1  = na
float  buyTp2  = na
float  buyTp3  = na
string buyTag1 = na
string buyTag2 = na
string buyTag3 = na
float  sellTp1  = na
float  sellTp2  = na
float  sellTp3  = na
string sellTag1 = na
string sellTag2 = na
string sellTag3 = na

if rawBuyPre
    [t1, t2, t3, g1, g2, g3] = f_tpLevels(true, buyEntryRef, buyRiskDist, tpMeanBuy, tpUnitBuy)
    buyTp1  := t1
    buyTp2  := t2
    buyTp3  := t3
    buyTag1 := g1
    buyTag2 := g2
    buyTag3 := g3
if rawSellPre
    [t1, t2, t3, g1, g2, g3] = f_tpLevels(false, sellEntryRef, sellRiskDist, tpMeanSell, tpUnitSell)
    sellTp1  := t1
    sellTp2  := t2
    sellTp3  := t3
    sellTag1 := g1
    sellTag2 := g2
    sellTag3 := g3

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.8 REWARD GATE. The engine bounded RISK from the start (min/max ATR
// window) but never once checked what the trade could MAKE. TP1 is the
// nearest structure level above entry, so it can sit a fraction of the stop
// distance away — and because TP1 also triggers the breakeven move, a
// too-close first target systematically converts trades into scratches:
// take a third off for a few ticks, pull the stop to entry, get brushed out
// of the rest. Many tiny wins and scratches with occasional full losses is
// exactly what "lacks accuracy" feels like on a chart.
// TP1 must now be at least i_minRR × the risk. 0 disables the gate.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
rrBuy    = not na(buyTp1)  ? (buyTp1 - buyEntryRef)  / math.max(buyRiskDist,  1e-9) : na
rrSell   = not na(sellTp1) ? (sellEntryRef - sellTp1) / math.max(sellRiskDist, 1e-9) : na
// v3.5.26 BLENDED REWARD. The gate measured TP1 ALONE, but the plan is a
// 33/33/34 scale-out — so a setup whose first target is modest and whose
// second and third are excellent was rejected on a number that describes a
// third of the position. That is a measurement error, not a safety margin, and
// it is the single largest source of "rr" blocks on 15m gold where the nearest
// pivot often sits inside 1R.
//
// A plan now passes on EITHER the TP1 test (unchanged) OR the weighted reward
// of the whole scale-out clearing i_minBlendRR. Raising trade count by fixing
// what is measured, rather than by lowering the bar.
//
// Guarded two ways. TP1 must still clear i_minTp1Floor, so a target a few ticks
// away can never be admitted by a strong runner behind it. And a trade admitted
// on the blended path does NOT arm the breakeven move (see activeBeOk), because
// a close TP1 pulling the stop to entry is precisely the scratch machine the
// v3.5.8 reward gate was built to stop.
blendBuy  = not na(buyTp1)  and not na(buyTp2)  and not na(buyTp3)  ? (0.33 * (buyTp1 - buyEntryRef) + 0.33 * (buyTp2 - buyEntryRef) + 0.34 * (buyTp3 - buyEntryRef))   / math.max(buyRiskDist,  1e-9) : na
blendSell = not na(sellTp1) and not na(sellTp2) and not na(sellTp3) ? (0.33 * (sellEntryRef - sellTp1) + 0.33 * (sellEntryRef - sellTp2) + 0.34 * (sellEntryRef - sellTp3)) / math.max(sellRiskDist, 1e-9) : na
tp1FloorOkBuy  = not na(rrBuy)  and rrBuy  >= i_minTp1Floor
tp1FloorOkSell = not na(rrSell) and rrSell >= i_minTp1Floor
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.2 — THE REWARD GATE WAS NOT THE GATE IT SAYS IT IS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v3.5.8 added the engine's only reward check: TP1 at least i_minRR (1.0) times
// the stop distance. v3.5.26 then added an alternative path, and rrOk became
//     effMinRR <= 0  or  rrStrict  or  blendOk
// An `or` short-circuits, so the strict test only decides a trade when blendOk
// is FALSE. Measured on 15m, 150 days x 3 seeds, 8,198 setups reaching the gate:
//
//     blended reward, median                    2.68   against a 1.3 threshold
//     share of setups whose blend clears 1.3     100%
//
// Every single one. i_minBlendRR has never rejected anything, so blendOk reduces
// to its other term, the 0.5R TP1 floor — and the ENGINE'S EFFECTIVE REWARD
// REQUIREMENT IS 0.5R, not the 1.0R it documents and displays. 19% of admitted
// trades were admitted only by this path, i.e. with TP1 between 0.5R and 1.0R.
//
// The blend clears so easily because it prices all three legs at 33/33/34 while
// TP2 fills 14% of the time and TP3 6% — 0.34 of the number that admits the
// trade is a target that almost never pays.
//
// v4.2 does not delete the blended path; a modest TP1 behind two strong targets
// is a real setup and rejecting it was v3.5.26's genuine finding. It weights the
// blend by REACHABILITY instead of by intended size, so a distant runner can no
// longer carry a weak first target on a share of the plan it does not deliver.
// i_blendWeighted off restores the v4.1 arithmetic exactly.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blendBuyW  = not na(buyTp1)  and not na(buyTp2)  and not na(buyTp3)  ? (0.55 * (buyTp1 - buyEntryRef) + 0.30 * (buyTp2 - buyEntryRef) + 0.15 * (buyTp3 - buyEntryRef))   / math.max(buyRiskDist,  1e-9) : na
blendSellW = not na(sellTp1) and not na(sellTp2) and not na(sellTp3) ? (0.55 * (sellEntryRef - sellTp1) + 0.30 * (sellEntryRef - sellTp2) + 0.15 * (sellEntryRef - sellTp3)) / math.max(sellRiskDist, 1e-9) : na
blendUseBuy  = i_blendWeighted ? blendBuyW  : blendBuy
blendUseSell = i_blendWeighted ? blendSellW : blendSell
blendOkBuy  = i_minBlendRR > 0 and tp1FloorOkBuy  and not na(blendUseBuy)  and blendUseBuy  >= i_minBlendRR
blendOkSell = i_minBlendRR > 0 and tp1FloorOkSell and not na(blendUseSell) and blendUseSell >= i_minBlendRR
rrStrictBuy  = not na(rrBuy)  and rrBuy  >= effMinRR
rrStrictSell = not na(rrSell) and rrSell >= effMinRR
rrOkBuy  = effMinRR <= 0 or rrStrictBuy  or blendOkBuy
rrOkSell = effMinRR <= 0 or rrStrictSell or blendOkSell

// v3.5.12: confluence gate reads the score computed above; 0 disables it.
// v4.1: ANDed with the mode's own five-reading requirement. The two measure
// different things — this one is the user's manual floor on directional
// alignment, that one is what the selected mode demands of the bar itself.
confOkBuy  = buyAligned  >= effMinConf and v4ConfOkBuy
confOkSell = sellAligned >= effMinConf and v4ConfOkSell

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.32 LEVEL-RETEST ENTRIES
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// At market a qualifying bar IS the entry. In level mode it instead ARMS an
// order at the trigger's own level and the entry happens only if price returns
// there within i_pendBars. Everything above has already been measured from that
// level (buyEntryRef), so the stop, the targets and both reward gates describe
// the trade that would actually be filled.
//
// Why this exists: every trigger requires its event to be COMPLETE before it
// can fire — the break already happened, the sweep already closed back, the
// retest already rejected — and bar-close confirmation plus up to i_triggerAge
// bars of freshness sit on top. On 15m that is up to ~7 bars, so a market entry
// is systematically taken away from the level with a wider stop. Arming at the
// level removes that penalty without loosening a single gate.
//
// The cost is real and not simulated away: an order that never fills is a move
// missed, and one that fills just before the level breaks is a worse loss than
// the market entry would have been. Off by default.
qualBuy  = rawBuyPre  and rrOkBuy  and confOkBuy
qualSell = rawSellPre and rrOkSell and confOkSell

// expire first, so a stale level can never fill on the bar it lapses
if not na(pendBuyBar) and bar_index - pendBuyBar > i_pendBars
    pendBuyLvl := na
    pendBuyBar := na
if not na(pendSellBar) and bar_index - pendSellBar > i_pendBars
    pendSellLvl := na
    pendSellBar := na

// fill: price traded back to the armed level on a confirmed bar
fireBuy  = levelMode and barstate.isconfirmed and not na(pendBuyLvl)  and low  <= pendBuyLvl
fireSell = levelMode and barstate.isconfirmed and not na(pendSellLvl) and high >= pendSellLvl

buySignal  = levelMode ? fireBuy  : qualBuy
sellSignal = levelMode ? fireSell : qualSell

// arm AFTER the fill test, so a setup cannot arm and fill on the same bar using
// a level price the bar may never have traded at
if levelMode and qualBuy and not na(buyChaseRef) and na(pendBuyLvl)
    pendBuyLvl := buyChaseRef
    pendBuyBar := bar_index
if levelMode and qualSell and not na(sellChaseRef) and na(pendSellLvl)
    pendSellLvl := sellChaseRef
    pendSellBar := bar_index

// clear the filled side, and drop the opposite pending — the market has chosen
if buySignal
    pendBuyLvl := na
    pendBuyBar := na
    pendSellLvl := na
    pendSellBar := na
if sellSignal
    pendSellLvl := na
    pendSellBar := na
    pendBuyLvl := na
    pendBuyBar := na
// v3.4.1: names respect mode gating — previously "FVG"/"SWEEP" could be
// reported in modes where those triggers are disabled.
// v4.2: the label IS the selection. A "+" marks a bar where other triggers also
// fired, so a stacked setup is visible rather than silently relabelled.
buyType    = buySel  == "" ? "MSS" : buySel  + (buySelStack  ? "+" : "")
sellType   = sellSel == "" ? "MSS" : sellSel + (sellSelStack ? "+" : "")

if buySignal
    lastSigBar := bar_index
    lastTradeBuy := true
    lastSlHit := false
if sellSignal
    lastSigBar := bar_index
    lastTradeBuy := false
    lastSlHit := false

// v3.5.2: name the gates that vetoed the most recent trigger — shown in the
// dashboard "Last Block" row and (optionally) as on-chart ✕ labels.
var string lastBlockReason = "—"
if barstate.isconfirmed and buyTrigger and barBull and not buySignal
    string r = ""
    r += strongBull ? "" : "body "
    r += notWeakBuy ? "" : "wick "
    r += volOk ? "" : "vol "
    r += buyPct >= adaptThresh ? "" : "of% "
    r += deltaBullOk ? "" : "delta "
    r += cvdOkBuy ? "" : "cvd "
    r += momOkBuyEff ? "" : "mom "
    r += modePassBull ? "" : "mode "
    r += biasOkBuy ? "" : "bias "
    r += coolOk ? "" : "cooldown "
    r += riskOkBuy ? "" : "risk "
    r += rsiOkBuy ? "" : "rsi "
    r += newsOk ? "" : "news "
    r += breakerOk ? "" : "lossbreak "
    r += chaseOkBuy ? "" : "chase "
    r += rrOkBuy ? "" : "rr "
    r += confOkBuy ? "" : "confluence "
    r += inSession ? "" : "session "
    r += regimeOk ? "" : "regime "
    if r != ""
        lastBlockReason := "BUY ✕ " + r
        if i_debugLabels
            label.new(bar_index, low - atr14 * 0.4, "✕ " + r, style=label.style_label_up, color=color.new(#888888, 70), textcolor=color.new(#AAAAAA, 0), size=size.tiny)
if barstate.isconfirmed and sellTrigger and barBear and not sellSignal
    string r = ""
    r += strongBear ? "" : "body "
    r += notWeakSell ? "" : "wick "
    r += volOk ? "" : "vol "
    r += (100.0 - buyPct) >= adaptThresh ? "" : "of% "
    r += deltaBearOk ? "" : "delta "
    r += cvdOkSell ? "" : "cvd "
    r += momOkSellEff ? "" : "mom "
    r += modePassBear ? "" : "mode "
    r += biasOkSell ? "" : "bias "
    r += coolOk ? "" : "cooldown "
    r += riskOkSell ? "" : "risk "
    r += rsiOkSell ? "" : "rsi "
    r += newsOk ? "" : "news "
    r += breakerOk ? "" : "lossbreak "
    r += chaseOkSell ? "" : "chase "
    r += rrOkSell ? "" : "rr "
    r += confOkSell ? "" : "confluence "
    r += inSession ? "" : "session "
    r += regimeOk ? "" : "regime "
    if r != ""
        lastBlockReason := "SELL ✕ " + r
        if i_debugLabels
            label.new(bar_index, high + atr14 * 0.4, "✕ " + r, style=label.style_label_down, color=color.new(#888888, 70), textcolor=color.new(#AAAAAA, 0), size=size.tiny)


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// MEMORY
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// v3.5.37: a trigger is the INPUT to every gate — no trigger means no gate is
// even consulted, and "why no trades" cannot be answered without knowing which
// of the two is happening. These two counters make that readable per timeframe
// instead of inferable from the MSS freshness counter alone.
anyTrigEvent = mssUpEvent or mssDownEvent or fvgRetestBull or fvgRetestBear or sweepBullEvent or sweepBearEvent
var int lastTrigBar = na
if anyTrigEvent
    lastTrigBar := bar_index
trigAge      = na(lastTrigBar) ? 999 : math.min(bar_index - lastTrigBar, 999)
trigCount100 = math.sum(anyTrigEvent ? 1.0 : 0.0, 100)
sigCount100  = math.sum((buySignal or sellSignal) ? 1.0 : 0.0, 100)

var string lastSigMemory = "—"
// v3.5.5: monotonic signal counter — sent as "seq" so a webhook receiver can
// dedupe retries and reject out-of-order deliveries.
var int sigSeq = 0
if buySignal
    lastSigMemory := "BUY (" + buyType + ")"
    sigSeq += 1
if sellSignal
    lastSigMemory := "SELL (" + sellType + ")"
    sigSeq += 1

var string lastMssDir   = "—"
var string lastFvgDir   = "—"
var string lastSweepDir = "—"
if barstate.isconfirmed
    if mssUpEvent
        lastMssDir := "BULL"
    if mssDownEvent
        lastMssDir := "BEAR"
    if fvgRetestBull
        lastFvgDir := "BULL"
    if fvgRetestBear
        lastFvgDir := "BEAR"
    if sweepBullEvent
        lastSweepDir := "BULL"
    if sweepBearEvent
        lastSweepDir := "BEAR"

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// TP / SL TRACKING + PROJECTION
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v3.4.1: tracking (activeSlPx / activeTp1Px / activeTp3Px, SL-hit cooldown,
// TP1 de-risk) now runs on EVERY signal regardless of i_tpslShow. Previously
// hiding the lines silently disabled the post-SL double-cooldown risk guard.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

var line  tpsl_sl   = na
var line  tpsl_ent  = na
var line  tpsl_tp1  = na
var line  tpsl_tp2  = na
var line  tpsl_tp3  = na
var label tpsl_slL  = na
var label tpsl_tp1L = na
var label tpsl_tp2L = na
var label tpsl_tp3L = na
// v3.5.16: optional bands around each level (display only).
var box   tpsl_slZ  = na
var box   tpsl_tp1Z = na
var box   tpsl_tp2Z = na
var box   tpsl_tp3Z = na

// v3.5.18: every TP/SL label carries the signal number. When a trade closes
// at TP3 and a fresh signal opens moments later, its targets are recomputed
// from the CURRENT profile — so a VAH-based TP can land a few points from the
// old one and look exactly like a target that moved. It never moves; only the
// SL does, and only to breakeven. The number makes a new plan unmistakable.
sigTag = " #" + str.tostring(sigSeq) + " "

var float activeSlPx  = na
var float activeTp1Px = na
// v4.2: THE MIDDLE LEG NOW EXISTS. v4.1 kept activeTp1Px and activeTp3Px and no
// activeTp2Px at all — so TP2 was drawn on the chart, published in the alert
// payload, and the engine had no idea whether it had ever traded. A three-leg
// scale-out was tracked as two legs, and the v3.5.41 manual TP2 override moved
// the drawn line while writing to nothing, because there was no tracked value
// for it to write to. Both are fixed by this one declaration and its uses below.
var float activeTp2Px = na
var bool  activeTp2Hit = false
var float activeTp3Px = na
var float activeEntryPx = na   // v3.5.5: needed for the breakeven move
// v3.5.24: the trade's OWN confluence at the moment it was taken. Invalidation
// is measured as DEGRADATION from this, never against an absolute bar.
var int   activeEntryConf = na
var bool  tradeBuy    = false
var bool  activeTp1Hit = false
// v3.5.26: did THIS trade's TP1 clear the strict reward bar? A plan admitted by
// the blended path has a deliberately modest TP1, and arming breakeven off it
// would recreate the scratch pattern the v3.5.8 gate was built to stop.
var bool  activeBeOk   = false
var bool  activeAtBe   = false

slHitNow  = not na(activeSlPx)  and (tradeBuy ? low  <= activeSlPx  : high >= activeSlPx)
tp1HitNow = not na(activeTp1Px) and (tradeBuy ? high >= activeTp1Px : low  <= activeTp1Px)
tp2HitNow = not na(activeTp2Px) and (tradeBuy ? high >= activeTp2Px : low  <= activeTp2Px)
tp3HitNow = not na(activeTp3Px) and (tradeBuy ? high >= activeTp3Px : low  <= activeTp3Px)

// v3.5.8 TIME STOP. A trade that reaches neither its stop nor TP3 previously
// stayed active indefinitely: the runner never resolved and the SL/TP1
// bookkeeping stayed frozen on a position that had long stopped mattering.
var int tradeStartBar = na
timeExit = i_maxBars > 0 and not na(tradeStartBar) and not na(activeSlPx) and (bar_index - tradeStartBar) >= i_maxBars

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.23 INVALIDATION EXIT
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The engine scored its conditions once, at entry, and then stopped thinking. A
// plan had exactly four ways out — stop, TP3, the time stop (which ships off)
// and a new signal — so nothing closed a trade whose reason for existing had
// gone. Where signals are frequent this is invisible: on XAUUSD 15m Scalp a new
// signal replaces the plan every few hours. Where they are not, the plan simply
// hangs.
//
// Seen live, both on BTCUSD:
//   15m  trade #62 open three days, no exit condition ever reachable
//   1D   trade #10 entry 66,519, price 62,991 (-0.58R) with the dashboard
//        reading 3/3 SELL and 1/3 BUY, TP1 still anchored to a VAH of 76,558
//        while the live VAH had fallen to 65,461 — the structure the plan was
//        built on no longer existed and the engine had no way to notice.
//
// The confluence scores are already recomputed every bar, so the fix costs
// nothing to measure: when the OPPOSITE side is fully aligned and the trade's
// own side has fallen to the ceiling or below, held for i_invalidBars consecutive
// CLOSED bars, the setup is dead and the plan closes.
//
// v3.5.24: invalidation is DEGRADATION FROM ENTRY, never an absolute reading.
// The first cut compared current confluence against a fixed bar, which broke
// Scalp outright: Scalp bypasses the HTF/VWAP/FRVP gates on purpose
// (modePassBull = isScalp ? notExtended : ...) and effMinConf falls to 0, so a
// Scalp trade may legitimately enter at 0/3. Judging it later by those same
// three legs closed trades for a thesis they never claimed — entered on a
// sweep, killed by an HTF bias the setup never required. A trade can now only
// be invalidated by losing confluence it actually HAD.
//
// The own-side ceiling matters more than it looks. buyAligned and sellAligned
// are NOT mutually exclusive — frvpBullOk and frvpBearOk are both true while
// price sits inside the value area — so a fully flipped market commonly reads
// 3/3 against and 1/3 for. Demanding the own side at exactly 0 would have
// ignored the very trade this rule was built for, which held 1/3 BUY against
// 3/3 SELL for weeks. The ceiling defaults to 1 for that reason.
//
// Treated like the time stop, not like a stop-out: it does not set lastSlHit
// and does not extend the cooldown, because a trade closed for a dead thesis is
// not evidence that the next setup is worse.
//
// ON by default. Shipping the time stop disabled is what let these two trades
// hang in the first place, and repeating that would be the same mistake twice.
// Its effect on profit factor is UNMEASURED — it will also close trades that
// would have recovered. Run the strategy over one range with it on and off and
// compare before trusting it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var int invalidRun = 0
ownNow      = tradeBuy ? buyAligned  : sellAligned
oppNow      = tradeBuy ? sellAligned : buyAligned
invalidLive = not na(activeSlPx) and not na(activeEntryConf) and oppNow >= i_invalidConf and ownNow <= i_invalidOwn and ownNow < activeEntryConf
if barstate.isconfirmed
    invalidRun := invalidLive ? invalidRun + 1 : 0
invalidExit = i_invalidExit and not na(activeSlPx) and invalidRun >= i_invalidBars

// v3.3: TP1 counts as "de-risked" — SL after that shouldn't extend cooldown.
// v3.5.4: when ONE bar trades through both TP1 and the SL, the intrabar order
// is unknown. The old order credited TP1 first and cleared the SL-hit flag,
// so a bar that may have stopped out disabled the post-SL cooldown. Resolve
// conservatively: SL first when both are touched on the same bar.
slFirstAmbiguous = slHitNow and tp1HitNow and not activeTp1Hit

if barstate.isconfirmed and tp1HitNow and not slFirstAmbiguous
    activeTp1Hit := true
    lastSlHit := false
    lossStreak := 0   // v3.5.5: a trade that reached TP1 breaks the streak
    // v3.5.5 BREAKEVEN. TP1 was already treated as "de-risked" for the
    // cooldown guard, but the stop never moved — so a trade could tag TP1
    // and still come back for a full loss. Move the tracked stop to entry
    // and drag the drawn line/label with it.
    if i_beAfterTp1 and activeBeOk and not na(activeEntryPx) and not activeAtBe
        activeSlPx := activeEntryPx
        activeAtBe := true
        if not na(tpsl_sl)
            line.set_y1(tpsl_sl, activeEntryPx)
            line.set_y2(tpsl_sl, activeEntryPx)
        if not na(tpsl_slL)
            label.set_y(tpsl_slL, activeEntryPx)
            label.set_text(tpsl_slL, " BE  " + sigTag + str.tostring(activeEntryPx, format.mintick))
// v4.2: the middle leg is recorded when it trades, so the dashboard can show
// which legs of the scale-out are actually filling instead of only the first
// and the last.
if barstate.isconfirmed and tp2HitNow and not slHitNow
    activeTp2Hit := true
if barstate.isconfirmed and tp3HitNow and not slHitNow
    lastSlHit := false
if barstate.isconfirmed and slHitNow and not activeTp1Hit
    lastSlHit := true
    lossStreak := math.min(lossStreak + 1, 999)

if barstate.isconfirmed and (slHitNow or tp3HitNow or timeExit or invalidExit or buySignal or sellSignal)
    if not na(tpsl_sl)
        line.delete(tpsl_sl)
        tpsl_sl := na
    if not na(tpsl_ent)
        line.delete(tpsl_ent)
        tpsl_ent := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp1)
        line.delete(tpsl_tp1)
        tpsl_tp1 := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp2)
        line.delete(tpsl_tp2)
        tpsl_tp2 := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp3)
        line.delete(tpsl_tp3)
        tpsl_tp3 := na
    if not na(tpsl_slL)
        label.delete(tpsl_slL)
        tpsl_slL := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp1L)
        label.delete(tpsl_tp1L)
        tpsl_tp1L := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp2L)
        label.delete(tpsl_tp2L)
        tpsl_tp2L := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp3L)
        label.delete(tpsl_tp3L)
        tpsl_tp3L := na
    if not na(tpsl_slZ)
        box.delete(tpsl_slZ)
        tpsl_slZ := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp1Z)
        box.delete(tpsl_tp1Z)
        tpsl_tp1Z := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp2Z)
        box.delete(tpsl_tp2Z)
        tpsl_tp2Z := na
    if not na(tpsl_tp3Z)
        box.delete(tpsl_tp3Z)
        tpsl_tp3Z := na
    activeSlPx   := na
    activeTp1Px  := na
    activeTp2Px  := na
    activeTp3Px  := na
    activeTp2Hit := false
    activeEntryPx := na
    activeEntryConf := na
    activeTp1Hit := false
    activeAtBe   := false
    activeBeOk   := false
    tradeStartBar := na
    invalidRun    := 0

if buySignal
    activeSlPx   := buySLpx
    activeTp1Px  := buyTp1
    activeTp2Px  := buyTp2
    activeTp3Px  := buyTp3
    activeTp2Hit := false
    activeEntryPx := buyEntryRef
    activeEntryConf := buyAligned
    activeTp1Hit := false
    activeAtBe   := false
    activeBeOk   := rrStrictBuy
    tradeStartBar := bar_index
    invalidRun    := 0
    tradeBuy     := true
    if i_tpslShow
        endBar = bar_index + i_tpLineLen
        tpsl_sl   := line.new(bar_index, buySLpx, endBar, buySLpx, color=color.new(#FF3366,  0), width=2, style=line.style_dashed)
        tpsl_ent  := line.new(bar_index, buyEntryRef,  endBar, buyEntryRef,  color=color.new(#FFFFFF, 65), width=1, style=line.style_dotted)
        tpsl_tp1  := line.new(bar_index, buyTp1,  endBar, buyTp1,  color=color.new(#00FF88,  0), width=3, style=line.style_solid)
        tpsl_tp2  := line.new(bar_index, buyTp2,  endBar, buyTp2,  color=color.new(#FFD700,  0), width=2, style=line.style_solid)
        tpsl_tp3  := line.new(bar_index, buyTp3,  endBar, buyTp3,  color=color.new(#FF9500,  0), width=2, style=line.style_dashed)
        tpsl_slL  := label.new(endBar, buySLpx, buyStopTxt  + sigTag + str.tostring(buySLpx, format.mintick),           style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#FF3366, 0), textcolor=color.white, size=size.normal)
        tpsl_tp1L := label.new(endBar, buyTp1,  " TP1" + sigTag + str.tostring(buyTp1, format.mintick) + buyTag1, style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#00FF88, 0), textcolor=color.black, size=size.normal)
        tpsl_tp2L := label.new(endBar, buyTp2,  " TP2" + sigTag + str.tostring(buyTp2, format.mintick) + buyTag2, style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#FFD700, 0), textcolor=color.black, size=size.normal)
        tpsl_tp3L := label.new(endBar, buyTp3,  " TP3" + sigTag + str.tostring(buyTp3, format.mintick) + buyTag3, style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#FF9500, 0), textcolor=color.black, size=size.normal)
        if i_zoneShow
            zh = atr14 * i_zoneAtr
            tpsl_slZ  := box.new(bar_index, buySLpx + zh, endBar, buySLpx - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#FF3366, 85), border_color=color.new(#FF3366, 60))
            tpsl_tp1Z := box.new(bar_index, buyTp1 + zh, endBar, buyTp1 - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#00FF88, 85), border_color=color.new(#00FF88, 60))
            tpsl_tp2Z := box.new(bar_index, buyTp2 + zh, endBar, buyTp2 - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#FFD700, 85), border_color=color.new(#FFD700, 60))
            tpsl_tp3Z := box.new(bar_index, buyTp3 + zh, endBar, buyTp3 - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#FF9500, 85), border_color=color.new(#FF9500, 60))

if sellSignal
    activeSlPx   := sellSLpx
    activeTp1Px  := sellTp1
    activeTp2Px  := sellTp2
    activeTp3Px  := sellTp3
    activeTp2Hit := false
    activeEntryPx := sellEntryRef
    activeEntryConf := sellAligned
    activeTp1Hit := false
    activeAtBe   := false
    activeBeOk   := rrStrictSell
    tradeStartBar := bar_index
    invalidRun    := 0
    tradeBuy     := false
    if i_tpslShow
        endBar = bar_index + i_tpLineLen
        tpsl_sl   := line.new(bar_index, sellSLpx, endBar, sellSLpx, color=color.new(#FF3366,  0), width=2, style=line.style_dashed)
        tpsl_ent  := line.new(bar_index, sellEntryRef, endBar, sellEntryRef, color=color.new(#FFFFFF, 65), width=1, style=line.style_dotted)
        tpsl_tp1  := line.new(bar_index, sellTp1,  endBar, sellTp1,  color=color.new(#00FF88,  0), width=3, style=line.style_solid)
        tpsl_tp2  := line.new(bar_index, sellTp2,  endBar, sellTp2,  color=color.new(#FFD700,  0), width=2, style=line.style_solid)
        tpsl_tp3  := line.new(bar_index, sellTp3,  endBar, sellTp3,  color=color.new(#FF9500,  0), width=2, style=line.style_dashed)
        tpsl_slL  := label.new(endBar, sellSLpx, sellStopTxt + sigTag + str.tostring(sellSLpx, format.mintick),            style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#FF3366, 0), textcolor=color.white, size=size.normal)
        tpsl_tp1L := label.new(endBar, sellTp1,  " TP1" + sigTag + str.tostring(sellTp1, format.mintick) + sellTag1, style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#00FF88, 0), textcolor=color.black, size=size.normal)
        tpsl_tp2L := label.new(endBar, sellTp2,  " TP2" + sigTag + str.tostring(sellTp2, format.mintick) + sellTag2, style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#FFD700, 0), textcolor=color.black, size=size.normal)
        tpsl_tp3L := label.new(endBar, sellTp3,  " TP3" + sigTag + str.tostring(sellTp3, format.mintick) + sellTag3, style=label.style_label_left, color=color.new(#FF9500, 0), textcolor=color.black, size=size.normal)
        if i_zoneShow
            zh = atr14 * i_zoneAtr
            tpsl_slZ  := box.new(bar_index, sellSLpx + zh, endBar, sellSLpx - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#FF3366, 85), border_color=color.new(#FF3366, 60))
            tpsl_tp1Z := box.new(bar_index, sellTp1 + zh, endBar, sellTp1 - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#00FF88, 85), border_color=color.new(#00FF88, 60))
            tpsl_tp2Z := box.new(bar_index, sellTp2 + zh, endBar, sellTp2 - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#FFD700, 85), border_color=color.new(#FFD700, 60))
            tpsl_tp3Z := box.new(bar_index, sellTp3 + zh, endBar, sellTp3 - zh, bgcolor=color.new(#FF9500, 85), border_color=color.new(#FF9500, 60))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v3.5.41 MANUAL LEVEL OVERRIDE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Applied AFTER the plan is built, so signal generation is untouched: the
// reward gate already decided on the engine's own levels and a manual target
// must not be able to re-open that decision. What these do change is the drawn
// plan and the hit tracking that feeds breakeven, the cooldown and the
// loss-streak guard — i.e. the engine now scores the trade you are actually in.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
manSL  = i_manualOn and i_mSL  > 0 ? i_mSL  : na
manTP1 = i_manualOn and i_mTP1 > 0 ? i_mTP1 : na
manTP2 = i_manualOn and i_mTP2 > 0 ? i_mTP2 : na
manTP3 = i_manualOn and i_mTP3 > 0 ? i_mTP3 : na

if not na(activeSlPx)
    if not na(manSL)
        activeSlPx  := manSL
    if not na(manTP1)
        activeTp1Px := manTP1
    if not na(manTP2)
        activeTp2Px := manTP2
    if not na(manTP3)
        activeTp3Px := manTP3

if barstate.islast and i_manualOn
    if not na(manSL) and not na(tpsl_sl)
        line.set_y1(tpsl_sl, manSL)
        line.set_y2(tpsl_sl, manSL)
    if not na(manSL) and not na(tpsl_slL)
        label.set_y(tpsl_slL, manSL)
        label.set_text(tpsl_slL, " SL* " + sigTag + str.tostring(manSL, format.mintick))
    if not na(manTP1) and not na(tpsl_tp1)
        line.set_y1(tpsl_tp1, manTP1)
        line.set_y2(tpsl_tp1, manTP1)
    if not na(manTP1) and not na(tpsl_tp1L)
        label.set_y(tpsl_tp1L, manTP1)
        label.set_text(tpsl_tp1L, " TP1*" + sigTag + str.tostring(manTP1, format.mintick))
    if not na(manTP2) and not na(tpsl_tp2)
        line.set_y1(tpsl_tp2, manTP2)
        line.set_y2(tpsl_tp2, manTP2)
    if not na(manTP2) and not na(tpsl_tp2L)
        label.set_y(tpsl_tp2L, manTP2)
        label.set_text(tpsl_tp2L, " TP2*" + sigTag + str.tostring(manTP2, format.mintick))
    if not na(manTP3) and not na(tpsl_tp3)
        line.set_y1(tpsl_tp3, manTP3)
        line.set_y2(tpsl_tp3, manTP3)
    if not na(manTP3) and not na(tpsl_tp3L)
        label.set_y(tpsl_tp3L, manTP3)
        label.set_text(tpsl_tp3L, " TP3*" + sigTag + str.tostring(manTP3, format.mintick))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// SIGNAL LABELS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

if buySignal
    label.new(bar_index, low - atr14 * 0.8, "BUY #" + str.tostring(sigSeq), style=label.style_label_up, color=color.new(i_buyCol, 5), textcolor=color.rgb(0, 0, 0), size=size.normal)
if sellSignal
    label.new(bar_index, high + atr14 * 0.8, "SELL #" + str.tostring(sigSeq), style=label.style_label_down, color=color.new(i_sellCol, 5), textcolor=color.white, size=size.normal)

barcolor(i_barTint and ofBull ? color.new(i_buyCol, 80) : i_barTint and ofBear ? color.new(i_sellCol, 80) : na)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// DASHBOARD — v3.4.1: cells refresh only on the last bar (same visuals, faster)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

var table dash = na

f_cell(col, row, txt, clr) =>
    table.cell(dash, col, row, txt, text_color=clr, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_center, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))

if i_dashboard and barstate.islast
    tPos = (i_dashPos == "Top Right" ? position.top_right : i_dashPos == "Top Left" ? position.top_left : i_dashPos == "Bottom Right" ? position.bottom_right : position.bottom_left)

    if na(dash)
        dash := table.new(tPos, 3, 19, bgcolor=color.rgb(10, 10, 15, 10), border_width=1, border_color=color.rgb(40, 40, 50))
        table.merge_cells(dash, 1, 16, 2, 16)
        table.merge_cells(dash, 1, 17, 2, 17)
        table.merge_cells(dash, 1, 18, 2, 18)

    grn  = color.new(#00FF88, 0)
    red_ = color.new(#FF3366, 0)
    dim  = color.new(#888888, 0)
    wht  = color.new(#FFFFFF, 0)
    yel  = color.new(#FFD700, 0)
    grn2 = color.new(#00FF88, 50)
    red2 = color.new(#FF3366, 50)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 0, (isHeikin ? "⛔ HEIKIN ASHI — LEVELS INVALID" : "ME PRO v4.2") + " — " + str.upper(i_mode) + (inNewsWindow ? " ⛔NEWS" : "") + (breakerOk ? "" : " ⛔LOSSES"), text_color=color.white, bgcolor=color.rgb(15, 15, 20), text_size=size.small, text_halign=text.align_left)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 0, "BUY",  text_color=color.new(#00E5FF, 20), bgcolor=color.rgb(15, 15, 20), text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(dash, 2, 0, "SELL", text_color=color.new(#FF3366, 20), bgcolor=color.rgb(15, 15, 20), text_size=size.small)

    // v3.5: in Scalp the HTF gate is bypassed — show direction as info, never "BLOCKED"
    htfGateOn = htfFilterOn and not isScalp
    htfNote = isScalp ? " (off)" : htfDegenerate ? (i_htfGuard ? " ⚠≤chart, off" : " ⚠≤chart") : ""
    table.cell(dash, 0, 1, "HTF " + effHtfTf + htfNote, text_color=htfDegenerate and not isScalp ? yel : dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 1, htfBull ? "BULL ✓" : htfGateOn ? "BLOCKED" : "BEAR",   htfBull ? grn : htfGateOn ? red_ : dim)
    f_cell(2, 1, htfBear ? "BEAR ✓" : htfGateOn ? "BLOCKED" : "BULL",   htfBear ? red_ : htfGateOn ? red_ : dim)

    vwapAnchTxt = vwapUseMonth ? "M" : vwapUseWeek ? "W" : "D"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 2, "VWAP (" + vwapAnchTxt + ")", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 2, aboveVwap ? (notExtended ? "ABOVE ✓" : "EXTENDED") : i_vwapFilter ? "BLOCKED" : "BELOW", aboveVwap and notExtended ? grn : aboveVwap and not notExtended ? yel : i_vwapFilter ? red_ : dim)
    f_cell(2, 2, belowVwap ? (notExtended ? "BELOW ✓" : "EXTENDED") : i_vwapFilter ? "BLOCKED" : "ABOVE", belowVwap and notExtended ? red_ : belowVwap and not notExtended ? yel : i_vwapFilter ? red_ : dim)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 3, isScalp ? "Momentum (fast)" : "Momentum + Slope", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 3, momBullEff ? "BULL ✓" : i_momFilter ? "BLOCKED" : "BEAR", momBullEff ? grn : i_momFilter ? red_ : dim)
    f_cell(2, 3, momBearEff ? "BEAR ✓" : i_momFilter ? "BLOCKED" : "BULL", momBearEff ? red_ : i_momFilter ? red_ : dim)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 4, "RSI " + str.tostring(math.round(rsi, 1)), text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 4, rsiOkBuy  ? "OK" : "OUT",  rsiOkBuy  ? grn  : dim)
    f_cell(2, 4, rsiOkSell ? "OK" : "OUT",  rsiOkSell ? red_ : dim)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 5, "MSS (fresh " + str.tostring(math.min(math.min(mssUpAge, mssDnAge), 99)) + "b)", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 5, mssUp   ? "▲ FRESH" : lastMssDir == "BULL" ? "BULL" : lastMssDir == "BEAR" ? "bear" : "—", mssUp   ? grn  : lastMssDir == "BULL" ? grn2 : dim)
    f_cell(2, 5, mssDown ? "▼ FRESH" : lastMssDir == "BEAR" ? "BEAR" : lastMssDir == "BULL" ? "bull" : "—", mssDown ? red_ : lastMssDir == "BEAR" ? red2 : dim)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 6, "FVG Retest", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 6, fvgRetestBull ? "▲ NOW" : lastFvgDir == "BULL" ? "BULL" : lastFvgDir == "BEAR" ? "bear" : "—", fvgRetestBull ? grn  : lastFvgDir == "BULL" ? grn2 : dim)
    f_cell(2, 6, fvgRetestBear ? "▼ NOW" : lastFvgDir == "BEAR" ? "BEAR" : lastFvgDir == "BULL" ? "bull" : "—", fvgRetestBear ? red_ : lastFvgDir == "BEAR" ? red2 : dim)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 7, "Sweep (fresh)", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 7, sweepBull ? "▲ FRESH" : lastSweepDir == "BULL" ? "BULL" : lastSweepDir == "BEAR" ? "bear" : "—", sweepBull ? grn  : lastSweepDir == "BULL" ? grn2 : dim)
    f_cell(2, 7, sweepBear ? "▼ FRESH" : lastSweepDir == "BEAR" ? "BEAR" : lastSweepDir == "BULL" ? "bull" : "—", sweepBear ? red_ : lastSweepDir == "BEAR" ? red2 : dim)

    ofBuyTxt  = str.tostring(math.round(buyPct,         1)) + "% / " + str.tostring(math.round(adaptThresh, 0)) + "%"
    ofSellTxt = str.tostring(math.round(100.0 - buyPct, 1)) + "% / " + str.tostring(math.round(adaptThresh, 0)) + "%"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 8, "OF (adaptive)", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 8, ofBuyTxt,  ofBull ? grn : buyPct  > 50 ? wht : dim)
    f_cell(2, 8, ofSellTxt, ofBear ? red_ : buyPct < 50 ? wht : dim)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 9, "Band Reject", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 9, band2Bull ? "▲ 2σ" : bandBull ? "▲ 1σ" : "—", buyTrigBand  ? grn  : dim)
    f_cell(2, 9, band2Bear ? "▼ 2σ" : bandBear ? "▼ 1σ" : "—", sellTrigBand ? red_ : dim)


    // v3.5.36: show the last CONFIRMED bar's reading — the live bar's volume is
    // incomplete and the gate never sees it.
    relVolTxt = not volDataSeen ? "N/A (no vol)" : volSeriesBad ? str.tostring(math.round(relVolConfirmed, 2)) + "× ⚠VOL?" : str.tostring(math.round(relVolConfirmed, 2)) + "× (last bar)"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 10, "Rel Volume", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    relVolCol = not volDataSeen ? dim : volSeriesBad ? yel : relVolConfirmed >= effVolFloor ? grn : red_
    f_cell(1, 10, relVolTxt, relVolCol)
    f_cell(2, 10, relVolTxt, relVolCol)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 11, "CVD Flow", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 11, not volDataSeen ? "OFF (no vol)" : cvdBull ? "BULL" : "BEAR", not volDataSeen ? dim : cvdBull ? grn : red_)
    f_cell(2, 11, not volDataSeen ? "OFF (no vol)" : cvdBull ? "BULL" : "BEAR", not volDataSeen ? dim : cvdBull ? grn : red_)

    // v3.4: FRVP row shows live POC price
    // Label the window ACTUALLY profiled, not the one requested. "~" marks a
    // window truncated by the max-bars cap, so a short profile can never be
    // read as the full one you asked for.
    frvpWin = i_frvpAnchor == "Bars (legacy)" ? str.tostring(effFrvpLen) + "b" : (frvpClamped ? "~" : "") + str.tostring(frvpEffHrs, "#.#") + "h"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 12, "FRVP " + frvpWin + (na(pocPrice) ? "" : " @ " + str.tostring(pocPrice, format.mintick)), text_color=frvpClamped ? red_ : dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 12, frvpPosStr, str.contains(frvpPosStr, "IN VA") or str.contains(frvpPosStr, "ABOVE") ? grn : dim)
    f_cell(2, 12, frvpPosStr, str.contains(frvpPosStr, "IN VA") or str.contains(frvpPosStr, "BELOW") ? red_ : dim)

    regTxt = volExpand ? "EXPAND" : volChop ? "CHOP" : "NORMAL"
    regCol = volExpand ? grn : volChop ? yel : wht
    table.cell(dash, 0, 13, "Vol Regime", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 13, regTxt + " " + str.tostring(math.round(volRegime, 2)) + "×", regCol)
    f_cell(2, 13, regTxt + " " + str.tostring(math.round(volRegime, 2)) + "×", regCol)

    // v3.5.2: shown in the SAME ATR units the risk window is judged in, and
    // the SELL cell now dims when blocked (was red for OK and blocked alike)
    buyRatioStr  = str.tostring(math.round(buyRiskDist  / riskAtrRef, 2)) + "× ATR"
    sellRatioStr = str.tostring(math.round(sellRiskDist / riskAtrRef, 2)) + "× ATR"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 14, "Risk Dist", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 14, buyRatioStr,  riskOkBuy  ? grn : dim)
    f_cell(2, 14, sellRatioStr, riskOkSell ? red_ : dim)

    // v3.5.5: confluence 0-3 (HTF side + VWAP side + value-area side).
    // These two values were computed every bar since v3.3 and never read.
    table.cell(dash, 0, 15, "Confluence", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    // v4.1: second figure is the MODE's requirement — the five-reading score
    // against what this mode demands. This is the gate that decides the trade;
    // the /3 beside it is the manual alignment floor.
    f_cell(1, 15, str.tostring(buyAligned)  + "/3  " + str.tostring(confV4Buy)  + "≥" + str.tostring(confNeed), v4ConfOkBuy  ? grn  : dim)
    f_cell(2, 15, str.tostring(sellAligned) + "/3  " + str.tostring(confV4Sell) + "≥" + str.tostring(confNeed), v4ConfOkSell ? red_ : dim)

    // v3.5.37: triggers formed vs signals produced, over the last 100 bars, plus
    // how long since the last trigger of any kind. If triggers is 0 the engine
    // is seeing no setups at all and no gate is involved; if triggers is high
    // and signals is 0 the gates are rejecting and Last Block names which.
    trigTxt = str.tostring(int(nz(trigCount100))) + " trig / " + str.tostring(int(nz(sigCount100))) + " sig  ·  last " + (trigAge >= 999 ? "never" : str.tostring(trigAge) + "b")
    trigCol = nz(trigCount100) == 0 ? red_ : nz(sigCount100) > 0 ? grn : yel
    table.cell(dash, 0, 16, "Triggers (100b)", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(12, 12, 18))
    f_cell(1, 16, trigTxt, trigCol)
    f_cell(2, 16, trigTxt, trigCol)

    lastSigCol = str.contains(lastSigMemory, "BUY") ? grn : str.contains(lastSigMemory, "SELL") ? red_ : dim
    // v4.2: the scale-out's legs, as they fill. v4.1 could not show this — it
    // never tracked TP2 at all, so the middle third of every plan was invisible
    // to the engine that drew it.
    legTxt = not na(activeSlPx) ? "  · " + (activeTp1Hit ? "1" : "-") + (activeTp2Hit ? "2" : "-") + "3" : ""
    sigSuffix  = (activeAtBe ? "  · SL@BE" : lossStreak > 0 ? "  · " + str.tostring(lossStreak) + "L streak" : "") + legTxt
    table.cell(dash, 0, 17, "Last Signal", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(15, 15, 22))
    table.cell(dash, 1, 17, lastSigMemory + sigSuffix, text_color=lastSigCol, text_size=size.small, bgcolor=color.rgb(15, 15, 22))

    // v3.5.2: which gates vetoed the most recent trigger
    table.cell(dash, 0, 18, "Last Block", text_color=dim, text_size=size.tiny, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.rgb(15, 15, 22))
    table.cell(dash, 1, 18, lastBlockReason, text_color=lastBlockReason == "—" ? dim : yel, text_size=size.tiny, bgcolor=color.rgb(15, 15, 22))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// JSON ALERTS — v3.4.1: TPs are the SAME structure-based levels shown on chart
// v3.5.1: both message strings are now built ONLY on the signal bar that sends
// them — previously ~60 str.tostring concatenations ran on every single bar.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

f_json(side, trigger, slPx_, rDist_, tp1_, tp2_, tp3_, conf_, stopType_, entry_) =>
    // v3.5.9: optional shared secret, emitted as the FIRST field INSIDE the
    // object so a receiver can reject an unauthenticated payload early.
    '{' + (i_webhookSecret == "" ? "" : '"secret":"' + i_webhookSecret + '",') + '"action":"' + side + '","trigger":"' + trigger + '","mode":"' + i_mode + '","symbol":"' + syminfo.ticker + '","tf":"' + timeframe.period + '","entry":' + str.tostring(entry_, format.mintick) + ',"sl":' + str.tostring(slPx_, format.mintick) + ',"tp1":' + str.tostring(tp1_, format.mintick) + ',"tp2":' + str.tostring(tp2_, format.mintick) + ',"tp3":' + str.tostring(tp3_, format.mintick) + ',"risk_atr":' + str.tostring(math.round(rDist_ / riskAtrRef, 2)) + ',"of_pct":' + str.tostring(math.round(buyPct, 1)) + ',"delta":' + str.tostring(math.round(deltaRatio, 2)) + ',"rel_vol":' + str.tostring(math.round(relVol, 2)) + ',"rsi":' + str.tostring(math.round(rsi, 1)) + ',"cvd":"' + (cvdBull ? "BULL" : "BEAR") + '","htf":"' + htfLabel + '","vwap":"' + vwapLabel + '","frvp":"' + frvpPosStr + '","poc":' + (na(pocPrice) ? "0" : str.tostring(pocPrice, format.mintick)) + ',"vah":' + (na(vahPrice) ? "0" : str.tostring(vahPrice, format.mintick)) + ',"val":' + (na(valPrice) ? "0" : str.tostring(valPrice, format.mintick)) + ',"regime":"' + (volExpand ? "EXPAND" : volChop ? "CHOP" : "NORMAL") + '","stop_type":"' + stopType_ + '","confluence":' + str.tostring(conf_) + ',"seq":' + str.tostring(sigSeq) + ',"bar_time":' + str.tostring(time, "#") + '}'

alertcondition(buySignal,               "ME Pro — BUY Signal",  "ME Pro: BUY signal fired")
alertcondition(sellSignal,              "ME Pro — SELL Signal", "ME Pro: SELL signal fired")
alertcondition(buySignal or sellSignal, "ME Pro — Any Signal",  "ME Pro: signal fired")

if buySignal
    alert(i_jsonAlerts ? f_json("BUY",  buyType,  buySLpx,  buyRiskDist,  buyTp1,  buyTp2,  buyTp3,  buyAligned,  buyClamped  ? "clamped" : "structural", buyEntryRef)  : "ME Pro BUY "  + buyType,  alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
if sellSignal
    alert(i_jsonAlerts ? f_json("SELL", sellType, sellSLpx, sellRiskDist, sellTp1, sellTp2, sellTp3, sellAligned, sellClamped ? "clamped" : "structural", sellEntryRef) : "ME Pro SELL " + sellType, alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
